INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_3',
  'A financial institution is developing an anomaly detection model using Amazon SageMaker to identify fraudulent transactions. The dataset is highly imbalanced, with only 0.1% of transactions being fraudulent. After initial training, the model achieves 99.9% accuracy. However, the fraud detection team reports that many actual fraudulent transactions are still being missed, indicating a failure to capture the rare positive class.',
  'mcq',
  '["Accuracy, which measures the proportion of total correct predictions.","Precision, which measures the proportion of positive predictions that were actually correct.","Recall, which measures the proportion of actual positive cases that were correctly identified.","F1 Score, which is the harmonic mean of precision and recall."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Recall) is the most appropriate metric in this scenario. Recall measures the proportion of actual positive cases (fraudulent transactions) that were correctly identified by the model. In a highly imbalanced dataset where the positive class is rare, high accuracy can be misleading if the model simply predicts the majority class (non-fraudulent) most of the time. The business goal is to minimize missed fraudulent transactions (false negatives), which directly aligns with maximizing recall.
Option A (Accuracy) is incorrect because, as described in anti-pattern [ap-1.3-001], optimizing for accuracy alone in imbalanced datasets can be misleading. A model that predicts no fraud for all transactions would still achieve 99.9% accuracy but would miss all fraudulent transactions.
Option B (Precision) measures the proportion of positive predictions that were actually correct. While important, optimizing solely for precision might lead to missing many actual fraudulent transactions if the model becomes too conservative in its predictions to avoid false positives.
Option D (F1 Score) is the harmonic mean of precision and recall. While a good overall metric, in this specific scenario where minimizing missed fraudulent transactions (false negatives) is the primary concern, recall is the more direct and critical metric to prioritize.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_4',
  'A retail company has successfully deployed a product recommendation model using Amazon SageMaker. Initially, the model performed well, but over time, customer feedback suggests that the recommendations are becoming less relevant. The data science team suspects that the underlying customer preferences have shifted, causing the model''s performance to degrade in production.',
  'mcq',
  '["Redeploying the model weekly regardless of performance, which is an inefficient and potentially unnecessary action.","Continuously monitoring model performance and data drift, allowing for proactive intervention.","Manually retraining the model only when customer complaints peak, which is a reactive and suboptimal approach.","Archiving the model and developing a completely new one, which is an extreme and costly measure."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Continuously monitoring model performance and data drift) is the most crucial activity. As described in anti-pattern [ap-1.3-003], model deployment is not the end of the ML lifecycle. Models degrade over time due to changes in data distribution (data drift) or concept drift. Continuous monitoring allows the team to detect these issues early and trigger retraining or updates proactively, ensuring the model maintains its effectiveness with minimal manual intervention. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor can automate this.
Option A (Redeploying the model weekly regardless of performance) is incorrect. Blindly redeploying without understanding the cause of degradation or verifying improvement is inefficient and may not solve the underlying issue. It also adds unnecessary operational overhead if not needed.
Option C (Manually retraining the model only when customer complaints peak) is incorrect. This is a reactive approach that allows performance to degrade significantly before action is taken, leading to poor customer experience. Proactive monitoring is more effective.
Option D (Archiving the model and developing a completely new one) is incorrect. This is an extreme and highly inefficient measure. Often, retraining an existing model with fresh data or fine-tuning it is sufficient, rather than starting from scratch.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_5',
  'A retail company has developed a machine learning model to predict product demand based on historical sales data. They need to deploy this model to serve real-time predictions for their e-commerce platform. The company wants to minimize the operational overhead associated with managing the inference endpoint, as their data science team is small and focused on model development, not infrastructure. They expect prediction requests to fluctuate significantly throughout the day, with periods of high demand followed by long idle times.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker Real-time Endpoint, configuring auto-scaling policies to manage traffic fluctuations.","Use Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference to deploy the model endpoint, which automatically scales compute capacity.","Package the model in a Docker container and deploy it on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer, managing scaling manually.","Run Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform jobs periodically to generate predictions for all products at once."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice because Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference is designed for workloads with intermittent or unpredictable traffic, automatically scaling compute capacity up and down, including scaling to zero during idle periods. This significantly minimizes operational overhead as there are no instances to manage, and users only pay for the duration of inference requests. Option A, an Amazon SageMaker Real-time Endpoint with auto-scaling, requires configuring and managing scaling policies and instance types, incurring more operational overhead than serverless. Option C, deploying on Amazon EC2 instances, represents the highest operational overhead as it requires manual management of the entire infrastructure, including patching, scaling, and load balancing. Option D, Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform, is suitable for offline, batch predictions and does not meet the requirement for real-time predictions for an e-commerce platform.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_6',
  'An e-commerce company wants to build a machine learning model to predict customer churn using historical customer data, including browsing behavior, purchase history, and demographic information. The raw data is stored in various formats across different databases and includes missing values, inconsistent categorical encodings, and irrelevant columns. The data science team plans to use Amazon SageMaker for model development.',
  'mcq',
  '["Directly train a model on the raw data to leverage SageMaker''s auto-tuning capabilities, as it can handle data inconsistencies automatically.","Focus solely on hyperparameter tuning after initial model training, assuming the raw data is sufficient for a baseline.","Prioritize data preprocessing and feature engineering to clean, transform, and select relevant attributes before model training.","Deploy the model immediately after initial training to gather real-world performance metrics, then iterate on data quality."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct. Data preprocessing and feature engineering are critical initial steps in the ML development lifecycle. Cleaning missing values, standardizing formats, handling inconsistent encodings, and selecting relevant features (feature engineering) directly impact model quality and performance. High-quality, well-prepared data is foundational for a robust and accurate model.

Option A is incorrect. Directly training on raw, inconsistent data will lead to poor model performance and unreliable predictions, regardless of auto-tuning capabilities. SageMaker''s auto-tuning optimizes model parameters, not data quality.

Option B is incorrect. Hyperparameter tuning is important, but it cannot compensate for poor data quality (Anti-pattern: [ap-1.3-002]). Optimizing hyperparameters on a flawed dataset will only result in an optimized flawed model.

Option D is incorrect. Deploying a model without proper data preparation and evaluation is premature and risky. It could lead to inaccurate predictions in production, negatively impacting customer experience and business outcomes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_7',
  'A retail company has successfully deployed a machine learning model using Amazon SageMaker to recommend products to customers. Initially, the model performed very well, but after several months, customer feedback indicates that recommendations are becoming less relevant. The data science team suspects that customer preferences and purchasing patterns have subtly shifted over time, causing the model''s performance to degrade. The company wants to maintain the model''s effectiveness over the long term.',
  'mcq',
  '["Perform a one-time re-evaluation of the model''s accuracy using the original training dataset to confirm its initial performance.","Implement continuous monitoring of model performance and data drift in production, with automated alerts and triggers for retraining.","Manually review customer feedback periodically and retrain the model only when a significant drop in sales is observed.","Increase the frequency of hyperparameter tuning on the existing model without updating the training data."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Models in production are subject to data drift and concept drift, where the underlying data distribution or the relationship between features and targets changes over time. Continuous monitoring of model performance and data characteristics (e.g., using Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor) is essential to detect this degradation and trigger timely retraining with fresh data, ensuring the model remains relevant and accurate (Anti-pattern: [ap-1.3-003]).

Option A is incorrect. Re-evaluating with the original training data will only confirm past performance and will not reveal current degradation due to new data patterns. It does not address the issue of model drift.

Option C is incorrect. Manual review and reactive retraining only after a significant business impact is inefficient and costly. Proactive monitoring allows for earlier detection and intervention, minimizing performance degradation.

Option D is incorrect. Hyperparameter tuning on the existing model without updated training data will not resolve issues caused by data or concept drift. The model needs to learn from the new patterns in the data, which requires retraining with fresh data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_8',
  'A healthcare provider has developed a complex deep learning model using Amazon SageMaker for early disease detection from medical images. The training process for this model involved processing terabytes of data on high-end GPU instances over several weeks. Now, they need to deploy this model to provide real-time predictions to doctors, where low latency is critical for patient care. The team is evaluating the resource requirements for this deployment.',
  'mcq',
  '["Both training and real-time inference typically require similar high-end GPU resources and extended processing times.","Real-time inference generally requires significantly more computational resources than training due to the need for immediate responses.","Inference typically requires fewer computational resources but demands low latency, often using specialized hardware or optimized instances, compared to training which requires high computational power over extended periods.","Training primarily requires CPU resources, while inference exclusively relies on specialized FPGA hardware for optimal performance."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct. Model training is computationally intensive, often requiring powerful GPUs and significant time to learn patterns from large datasets. In contrast, real-time inference, while still needing computational power, typically involves forward propagation through a trained network, which is less resource-intensive than the iterative optimization of training. However, inference demands low latency and high throughput, often necessitating optimized instances, specialized hardware (like AWS Inferentia or GPU-backed instances), or efficient model serving techniques to deliver predictions quickly.

Option A is incorrect. Training and inference have distinct resource profiles. Training is about learning, inference is about applying what''s learned; their resource needs are generally different in scale and type.

Option B is incorrect. While inference can be demanding, especially at scale, the peak computational load for training a complex deep learning model is typically higher and sustained over a longer duration than a single inference request.

Option D is incorrect. Both training and inference for deep learning models often benefit significantly from GPU resources. While FPGAs can be used for inference optimization, they are not an exclusive or universal requirement, and CPUs are rarely the primary resource for deep learning training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_5_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_5',
  'A large e-commerce company wants to segment its customer base to offer personalized product recommendations. They have a vast amount of historical customer data, including purchase history, browsing behavior, and demographics, but no predefined customer groups or labels. The data science team needs to discover natural groupings within this unlabeled data with the LEAST amount of manual effort for data preparation.',
  'mcq',
  '["Supervised Learning, which requires labeled training data to predict outcomes.","Unsupervised Learning, which finds patterns and structures in unlabeled data.","Reinforcement Learning, which trains agents to make sequential decisions in an environment.","Deep Learning, which uses neural networks with multiple layers for complex pattern recognition."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Unsupervised Learning) is the correct approach. Unsupervised learning algorithms are designed to find patterns and structures in unlabeled datasets. Since the company has no predefined customer groups or labels, and the goal is to discover natural groupings with minimal manual effort for labeling, unsupervised learning (specifically clustering) is the most suitable method.
Option A (Supervised Learning) is incorrect because it requires labeled training data, as described in anti-pattern [ap-1.1-002]. The scenario explicitly states there are no predefined customer groups or labels, making supervised learning impractical without extensive manual labeling, which contradicts the "LEAST manual effort" constraint.
Option C (Reinforcement Learning) is incorrect. Reinforcement learning is used for training agents to make sequential decisions in an environment to maximize a reward, which is not applicable to customer segmentation.
Option D (Deep Learning) is incorrect. Deep learning is a subset of machine learning using neural networks, and while it can be applied to both supervised and unsupervised tasks, it describes an architectural approach rather than a learning paradigm suitable for this specific problem and constraint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_6',
  'A large e-commerce company wants to analyze its extensive customer database to identify distinct groups of customers with similar purchasing habits and preferences. The company has collected vast amounts of data, including transaction history, browsing behavior, and demographic information, but it does not have any pre-defined labels or categories for customer segments. The goal is to discover natural groupings within the data to inform targeted marketing strategies. Which type of machine learning is most appropriate for this task?',
  'mcq',
  '["Reinforcement Learning, to train an agent to interact with customer data and maximize rewards.","Supervised Learning, to predict customer segments based on historical labeled data.","Unsupervised Learning, to discover hidden patterns and structures in unlabeled customer data.","Deep Learning, to build complex neural networks for high-dimensional customer data analysis."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct. The scenario explicitly states that the company has "no pre-defined labels or categories for customer segments" and wants to "discover natural groupings." Unsupervised learning is the machine learning paradigm specifically designed for finding hidden patterns, structures, or clusters within unlabeled datasets. Techniques like clustering (e.g., K-means) are ideal for segmenting customers based on their inherent similarities without prior knowledge of the segments.
Option A, Reinforcement Learning, is used for training agents to make sequential decisions in an environment to maximize a reward signal. This is not suitable for discovering customer segments from static historical data.
Option B, Supervised Learning, requires labeled training data, where each customer would already be assigned to a segment. The problem statement clearly indicates the absence of such labels, making supervised learning inappropriate for the initial discovery phase. This option represents anti-pattern [ap-1.1-002] by confusing supervised and unsupervised learning.
Option D, Deep Learning, is a subset of machine learning that uses neural networks with multiple layers. While deep learning models can be used for both supervised and unsupervised tasks, simply stating "Deep Learning" doesn''t specify the learning paradigm (supervised or unsupervised) and doesn''t directly address the core requirement of working with unlabeled data to discover patterns. It''s a technique, not a learning type that fits the constraint of unlabeled data discovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_7',
  'A new software engineer joins a team that is developing an application involving image recognition for identifying objects in photos and generating descriptive captions for them. The engineer hears terms like ''AI,'' ''Machine Learning,'' and ''Deep Learning'' used interchangeably and is confused about their distinct meanings and hierarchical relationship within the project''s context. The team lead needs to clarify these concepts.',
  'mcq',
  '["AI, Machine Learning, and Deep Learning are essentially synonymous terms that describe any system capable of performing intelligent tasks.","Machine Learning is the broadest field, with AI as a subset, and Deep Learning as a further subset of AI.","Deep Learning is the broadest field, encompassing all AI and Machine Learning techniques, especially those involving neural networks.","AI is the broadest field of machines simulating human intelligence, Machine Learning is a subset of AI that learns from data, and Deep Learning is a subset of Machine Learning that uses neural networks with multiple layers."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct. This option accurately describes the hierarchical relationship: Artificial Intelligence (AI) is the overarching concept of machines simulating human intelligence. Machine Learning (ML) is a subset of AI that focuses on enabling systems to learn from data without explicit programming. Deep Learning (DL) is a specialized subset of ML that uses artificial neural networks with multiple layers (deep neural networks) to learn complex patterns, particularly effective for tasks like image and speech recognition.

Option A is incorrect. Treating AI, ML, and DL as synonymous is a common misconception (Anti-pattern: [ap-1.1-001]). While related, they represent different levels of abstraction and specificity.

Option B is incorrect. This reverses the correct hierarchy. AI is the broadest, not ML.

Option C is incorrect. This also reverses the correct hierarchy. Deep Learning is a specific technique within ML, not the broadest field.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_8',
  'A marketing analytics team wants to understand their customer base better by grouping customers with similar purchasing behaviors and demographics. They have a large dataset of customer information, but they do not have any predefined categories or labels for these customer segments. The goal is to discover natural groupings within the data to tailor marketing campaigns more effectively.',
  'mcq',
  '["Supervised learning, because it uses labeled data to predict outcomes for new customers.","Unsupervised learning, because it identifies hidden patterns and structures in unlabeled data, which is ideal for customer segmentation without predefined groups.","Reinforcement learning, as it involves an agent learning through trial and error in an environment.","Deep learning, because it can process large datasets and automatically extract complex features for any type of learning task."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Unsupervised learning is the most appropriate type of machine learning for this scenario. Since the marketing team does not have predefined labels or categories for customer segments, unsupervised learning algorithms (like clustering algorithms) can be used to discover inherent groupings and patterns within the unlabeled customer data. This allows for the identification of natural customer segments.

Option A is incorrect. Supervised learning requires labeled data (e.g., historical customer segments) to train a model to predict those labels for new data (Anti-pattern: [ap-1.1-002]). The scenario explicitly states there are no predefined labels.

Option C is incorrect. Reinforcement learning is used for training agents to make a sequence of decisions in an environment to maximize a reward, which is not applicable to customer segmentation.

Option D is incorrect. Deep learning is a subset of machine learning that uses neural networks. While deep learning models can be used in both supervised and unsupervised contexts, simply stating ''deep learning'' doesn''t specify the learning paradigm suitable for unlabeled data segmentation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_9',
  'A content creation company wants to automate the generation of unique product descriptions for its e-commerce website. They have a vast catalog of existing product information and images, and they need an AI system that can produce novel, engaging text descriptions that sound natural and are relevant to the product''s features. The company is exploring different AI paradigms for this task.',
  'mcq',
  '["A discriminative AI model, as it excels at classifying existing content and can therefore select the best existing descriptions.","A supervised learning model, which can be trained to categorize products and then retrieve pre-written descriptions.","A generative AI model, specifically a Large Language Model (LLM), because it is designed to produce new, original content by learning the patterns and distribution of existing data.","A clustering model, which groups similar products together to apply a single, generalized description to each group."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct. The requirement is to ''produce new, original content'' (unique product descriptions). Generative AI, particularly Large Language Models (LLMs), are specifically designed for tasks like text generation. They learn the patterns and structures from vast amounts of text data and can then generate novel, coherent, and contextually relevant text.

Option A is incorrect. Discriminative AI models are designed to classify or predict from input data (e.g., classifying a product description as good or bad), not to create new content (Anti-pattern: [ap-1.1-003]).

Option B is incorrect. A supervised learning model would be used to predict a label or category based on input, or to select from existing options, not to generate entirely new text.

Option D is incorrect. A clustering model would group similar products, but it would not generate unique descriptions for them. It would only facilitate applying existing descriptions to groups.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_5_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_4',
  'A construction company needs to automatically identify specific safety hazards, such as workers not wearing hard hats, from thousands of daily surveillance camera images. They want to integrate this capability into their existing safety monitoring system to alert supervisors in real-time. The company requires a fully managed service that can process images efficiently without needing to train custom computer vision models from scratch.',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon SageMaker","Amazon Textract"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon Rekognition) is the correct choice. Amazon Rekognition is a fully managed service that provides pre-trained computer vision capabilities, including object and scene detection, facial analysis, and custom labels. It can readily identify common objects and safety gear like hard hats in images and videos without requiring custom model training, making it highly efficient for this use case.
Option B (Amazon Comprehend) is incorrect because it is an NLP service used for text analysis, not image analysis.
Option C (Amazon SageMaker) is incorrect. While Amazon SageMaker can be used to build, train, and deploy custom computer vision models, the scenario specifies the need for a fully managed service that *doesn''t* require training custom models from scratch for common tasks, making SageMaker an over-engineered solution for this specific requirement and less efficient in terms of development time.
Option D (Amazon Textract) is incorrect because it is designed for extracting text and data from documents, not for general object detection in images or videos.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_5',
  'A global customer support center receives thousands of customer emails daily in multiple languages. The support team needs to quickly understand the sentiment of these emails (positive, negative, neutral) and automatically translate them into English for agents. The company wants to use a fully managed AWS solution that minimizes operational overhead and is MOST cost-effective for these specific tasks.',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Comprehend and Amazon Translate","Amazon SageMaker and Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Lex and Amazon Polly","AWS DeepRacer and Amazon Rekognition"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon Comprehend and Amazon Translate) is the correct combination. Amazon Comprehend is a natural language processing (NLP) service that uses machine learning to find insights and relationships in text, including sentiment analysis. Amazon Translate is a neural machine translation service that delivers fast, high-quality, and affordable language translation. Both are fully managed services that directly address the requirements of sentiment analysis and language translation with minimal operational overhead and cost-effectiveness for these specific tasks.
Option B (Amazon SageMaker and Amazon Transcribe) is incorrect. Amazon SageMaker is for building, training, and deploying custom ML models, which is an over-engineered and more costly solution for standard sentiment analysis and translation tasks that managed services can handle. Amazon Transcribe is for converting speech to text, not text translation or sentiment analysis.
Option C (Amazon Lex and Amazon Polly) is incorrect. Amazon Lex is for building conversational interfaces (chatbots), and Amazon Polly is for converting text into lifelike speech. Neither service addresses the core requirements of text sentiment analysis or translation.
Option D (AWS DeepRacer and Amazon Rekognition) is incorrect. AWS DeepRacer is for learning reinforcement learning through autonomous racing, and Amazon Rekognition is for computer vision. These services are completely unrelated to the scenario''s requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_6',
  'A global media company manages a vast digital archive of news footage, interviews, and documentaries. They need to implement a solution that can automatically detect and identify specific public figures (e.g., politicians, celebrities) within their video content, both for existing archives and new uploads. The primary goal is to enable efficient content search and compliance checks while minimizing the development effort and ongoing operational overhead. Which AWS service should the company use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker, to build and train a custom computer vision model for face recognition.","Amazon Rekognition, leveraging its pre-trained capabilities for face detection and recognition in videos.","Amazon Comprehend, to analyze the audio transcripts of the videos for mentions of public figures.","Amazon Textract, to extract text from on-screen graphics and subtitles within the video content."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Amazon Rekognition is a fully managed AWS AI service that provides pre-trained computer vision capabilities, including face detection, recognition, and celebrity recognition in images and videos. It requires no machine learning expertise or model training, directly addressing the requirement to minimize development effort and operational overhead. Its video analysis capabilities can process both existing and new video content to identify specific public figures.
Option A, Amazon SageMaker, allows users to build, train, and deploy custom machine learning models. While it could be used to create a custom computer vision model, this approach would involve significant development effort, ML expertise, and operational overhead for model training, deployment, and maintenance, which contradicts the stated goal of minimizing these aspects. This option represents anti-pattern [ap-1.2-001] by suggesting a complex ML solution when a simpler, managed AI service is available and more appropriate for the constraint.
Option C, Amazon Comprehend, is an NLP service used for text analysis, such as sentiment analysis, entity recognition, and key phrase extraction. While it could analyze transcripts if available, it would not directly identify figures visually within the video content itself, which is the core requirement.
Option D, Amazon Textract, is used for extracting text and data from documents and forms, including scanned PDFs and images. While it could extract text from on-screen graphics in videos, it is not designed for visual identification of faces or public figures within the video stream.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_7',
  'A global e-commerce company wants to improve its customer support by automatically translating incoming customer inquiries from various languages into English and then translating the support agent''s English responses back into the customer''s original language. The company needs a solution that requires minimal custom development and can handle multiple languages efficiently.',
  'mcq',
  '["Leverage Amazon Translate to automatically handle the bidirectional translation of text for customer inquiries and agent responses.","Implement a custom machine learning model for translation using Amazon SageMaker, trained on a large corpus of multilingual text.","Use Amazon Comprehend to detect the language of incoming inquiries and then manually translate using an external service.","Employ Amazon Transcribe to convert spoken inquiries to text, then use Amazon Polly to generate spoken responses in the target language."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the correct solution. Amazon Translate is a fully managed neural machine translation service that delivers fast, high-quality, and affordable language translation. It requires minimal development effort as it provides an API to translate text directly, fulfilling the requirement for automatic, bidirectional translation across multiple languages efficiently. Option B, implementing a custom machine learning model with Amazon SageMaker, would involve significant development effort, data preparation, training, and deployment, which contradicts the ''minimal custom development'' constraint. Option C, using Amazon Comprehend for language detection is correct, but then relying on a manual or external service for translation adds operational complexity and does not meet the ''minimal custom development'' and efficiency requirements for the entire translation process. Option D, Amazon Transcribe and Amazon Polly, are services for speech-to-text and text-to-speech respectively, which are not the primary requirement for translating text-based customer inquiries and responses.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_8',
  'A small business wants to implement a system to automatically send an alert to a manager if any customer order contains more than 10 units of a single item. The business is evaluating whether to use an AWS Machine Learning service or a traditional programming approach for this task. The primary goal is to ensure reliable and cost-effective alerts.',
  'mcq',
  '["Yes, because an AWS ML service can learn complex patterns in order data to predict when an alert is needed, offering greater flexibility.","No, because a simple rule-based system (e.g., an ''if-then'' statement) can deterministically solve this problem with less complexity and cost than an ML model.","Yes, because ML models are inherently more accurate than traditional programming for any data-driven decision-making process.","No, because AWS ML services are only suitable for tasks involving unstructured data like images or text, not structured order data."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. The problem described – flagging orders with more than 10 units of an item – is a simple, deterministic rule. It can be solved reliably and efficiently with a straightforward ''if-then'' statement in traditional programming. Introducing an ML model for such a clear-cut rule would add unnecessary complexity, operational overhead, and cost without providing any additional benefit (Anti-pattern: [ap-1.2-001]).

Option A is incorrect. While ML can learn complex patterns, this specific problem does not require pattern recognition; it requires a direct comparison. Using ML here would be over-engineering.

Option C is incorrect. ML models are not inherently more accurate for all problems. For deterministic rules, traditional programming offers 100% accuracy by definition, which ML cannot guarantee.

Option D is incorrect. AWS ML services can certainly handle structured data. However, the issue isn''t the type of data, but the simplicity of the problem itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_9',
  'A social media company is developing an AI model using Amazon Rekognition to automatically moderate user-uploaded images by detecting inappropriate content. During internal testing, the model shows a higher false positive rate (incorrectly flagging innocent images) for images uploaded by users from certain geographic regions or with specific cultural contexts. The company is concerned about the ethical implications before full deployment.',
  'mcq',
  '["Transparency, by ensuring the model''s internal decision-making process is fully explainable to all users.","Privacy, by encrypting all user-uploaded images to prevent unauthorized access to sensitive data.","Robustness, by ensuring the model can withstand adversarial attacks and maintain performance under various conditions.","Fairness, because the model''s biased performance across different user groups could lead to discriminatory outcomes and erode user trust."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct. The scenario describes a situation where the AI model exhibits differential performance (higher false positive rate) for specific demographic or cultural groups. This directly relates to the Responsible AI principle of fairness. Biased outcomes can lead to discriminatory treatment, alienate users, and severely damage the company''s reputation. Addressing fairness requires examining the training data for biases and implementing mitigation strategies (Anti-pattern: [ap-1.2-002]).

Option A is incorrect. While transparency is an important Responsible AI principle, the immediate and most critical issue described by the scenario is the biased performance, which falls under fairness, not just explainability.

Option B is incorrect. Privacy is crucial for handling user data, but the scenario''s core problem is about the model''s biased output, not unauthorized data access. Encryption is a data security measure, not a solution for model bias.

Option C is incorrect. Robustness refers to a model''s resilience to errors or malicious inputs. While important, it doesn''t directly address the issue of systematic bias in performance across different user groups.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_10',
  'An automotive manufacturer wants to improve the quality control process on its assembly line. They need to automatically detect subtle surface defects, such as scratches or dents, on newly painted car parts as they move along the conveyor belt. The system must be able to process high-resolution images in real-time and flag defective parts for immediate removal. The manufacturer is looking for a fully managed AWS AI service to achieve this.',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Comprehend, to analyze text descriptions of defects from quality reports.","Amazon Forecast, to predict the number of defects based on historical production data.","Amazon Rekognition, specifically its custom labels feature, to train a model to identify unique visual defects on car parts.","Amazon Polly, to generate spoken alerts when a defect is detected on the assembly line."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct. Amazon Rekognition is a fully managed computer vision service that can analyze images and videos. For detecting specific, unique defects like scratches or dents on car parts, Amazon Rekognition Custom Labels allows users to train a custom object detection or image classification model using their own labeled images, without requiring any machine learning expertise. This directly addresses the need for real-time visual defect detection.

Option A is incorrect. Amazon Comprehend is an NLP service for text analysis, not for image analysis or visual defect detection.

Option B is incorrect. Amazon Forecast is a time-series forecasting service, used for predicting future values based on historical data, not for real-time image analysis of defects.

Option D is incorrect. Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service. While it could be used to generate spoken alerts, it does not provide the core functionality for detecting visual defects.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_11',
  'An online streaming service wants to provide highly personalized content recommendations to its users based on their viewing history, ratings, and preferences. The service has a vast catalog of movies and TV shows and needs to ensure that recommendations are relevant and dynamic, adapting as user tastes evolve. The company is seeking a fully managed AWS AI service to build this recommendation engine without extensive machine learning expertise.',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Forecast, to predict future popular content based on historical viewing trends.","Amazon Personalize, to build real-time personalized recommendation systems for users.","Amazon Kendra, to provide intelligent search capabilities across their content library.","Amazon Comprehend, to analyze the sentiment of user reviews for content."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Amazon Personalize is a fully managed machine learning service specifically designed to build real-time personalized recommendation systems. It allows developers to easily add sophisticated personalization capabilities to their applications, such as recommending products, content, or other items, using the same technology used by Amazon.com.

Option A is incorrect. Amazon Forecast is used for time-series forecasting (e.g., predicting future sales or demand), not for individual user recommendations.

Option C is incorrect. Amazon Kendra is an intelligent enterprise search service that helps users find information across various data sources, not a recommendation engine.

Option D is incorrect. Amazon Comprehend is an NLP service used for text analysis, such as sentiment detection or entity extraction, which is not its primary purpose for building a recommendation engine.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

