INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_29',
  'A large e-commerce company processes millions of customer transactions daily. They are developing a machine learning model to detect fraudulent activities, but the raw transaction data is highly unstructured, contains numerous missing values, and has inconsistent formats across different sources. The data science team needs to efficiently clean, transform, and prepare this complex dataset for model training without writing extensive custom code. Which AWS service should the company use to achieve this with the LEAST manual effort?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","Amazon S3","Amazon SageMaker Studio","Amazon Comprehend"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option 0 (Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler) is the correct choice. It provides a visual interface and built-in transformations to aggregate, prepare, and clean data for machine learning, significantly reducing the manual effort of data preprocessing and feature engineering. Option 1 (Amazon S3) is a storage service and does not offer data preparation capabilities. Option 2 (Amazon SageMaker Studio) is an IDE for ML development but doesn''t specifically automate data preparation in the same way Data Wrangler does. Option 3 (Amazon Comprehend) is an NLP service for text analysis and is not designed for general-purpose data preparation across various data types. This question tests the understanding of the ML development lifecycle, specifically data preprocessing, and avoids the anti-pattern [ap-1.3-002] by emphasizing the importance of dedicated tools for data preparation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_30',
  'A media streaming service has deployed an Amazon Personalize recommendation model to suggest movies and TV shows to its users. Initially, the model performed well, but the data science team suspects that user preferences are evolving, causing the model''s recommendations to become less relevant over time. They need an automated way to detect when the deployed model''s performance degrades or when the input data distribution shifts, to trigger necessary retraining or updates. Which AWS service or feature should they implement to achieve this goal?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually review recommendation logs daily for anomalies","Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","Set up a fixed schedule to retrain the model every month","Use Amazon CloudWatch to monitor endpoint CPU utilization"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option 1 (Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor) is the correct solution. It automatically detects data drift and model quality issues in deployed machine learning models, allowing the team to identify performance degradation and trigger retraining. Option 0 (Manually review recommendation logs daily for anomalies) is a high-effort, non-scalable approach that does not meet the requirement for an automated detection mechanism. Option 2 (Set up a fixed schedule to retrain the model every month) is a reactive approach that might miss early degradation or retrain unnecessarily, lacking the intelligence to detect actual drift. Option 3 (Use Amazon CloudWatch to monitor endpoint CPU utilization) monitors infrastructure health, not the model''s performance or data quality, which are critical for detecting model drift. This question addresses the anti-pattern [ap-1.3-003] by highlighting that model deployment is not the end of the ML lifecycle and continuous monitoring is essential.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_31',
  'A cybersecurity company is developing a machine learning model to identify rare but critical security breaches in network traffic. The dataset is highly imbalanced, with only 0.1% of traffic samples representing actual breaches. The initial model achieves 99.9% accuracy, but security analysts are concerned that it might be missing many real breach events, leading to significant risks. The team needs to select an evaluation metric that specifically focuses on the model''s ability to correctly identify these critical positive cases, even if they are rare. Which metric is most appropriate for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Accuracy","Precision","Recall (Sensitivity)","Root Mean Square Error (RMSE)"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option 2 (Recall, also known as Sensitivity) is the most appropriate metric. Recall measures the proportion of actual positive cases (breaches) that were correctly identified by the model. In a highly imbalanced dataset where missing positive cases is critical, maximizing recall helps ensure that the model catches as many true breaches as possible. Option 0 (Accuracy) is misleading in imbalanced datasets (anti-pattern [ap-1.3-001]), as a model can achieve high accuracy by simply predicting the majority class. Option 1 (Precision) measures the proportion of positive predictions that were actually correct, which is important but less critical than recall when the cost of false negatives (missed breaches) is very high. Option 3 (Root Mean Square Error - RMSE) is a metric used for regression problems, not for classification tasks like breach detection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_32',
  'A large e-commerce company has successfully deployed a machine learning model to predict customer churn. After several months in production, the data science team observes that the model''s prediction accuracy has started to decline, likely due to evolving customer behavior patterns. The team needs a solution to automatically detect when the model''s performance degrades or when the input data distribution shifts, enabling proactive intervention. They require a mechanism that offers the LEAST operational overhead for continuous monitoring.',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually review model performance metrics and data characteristics daily using custom scripts.","Implement Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to automatically detect data and model quality issues.","Schedule a full model retraining process every week, regardless of current performance.","Develop a custom AWS Lambda function to periodically run inference and compare results against a baseline."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is a fully managed service designed to continuously monitor the quality of ML models in production. It automatically detects data drift, model quality degradation, and bias, providing alerts and insights with minimal operational overhead. This directly addresses the requirement for automatic detection and low operational effort.
Option A is incorrect because manually reviewing metrics daily using custom scripts involves significant operational overhead and is prone to human error, failing the ''LEAST operational overhead'' constraint. This represents anti-pattern [ap-1.3-003] by not leveraging automated monitoring.
Option C is incorrect because scheduling a full model retraining process every week without performance-based triggers can be inefficient and costly, potentially retraining unnecessarily or missing critical degradation periods between scheduled retrains. It does not provide continuous monitoring for drift or degradation.
Option D is incorrect because developing a custom AWS Lambda function for monitoring requires significant development effort, maintenance, and custom logic for drift detection, which is higher operational overhead compared to a purpose-built managed service like SageMaker Model Monitor.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_33',
  'A global e-commerce company has deployed an Amazon SageMaker model to predict customer churn. Initially, the model performed with high accuracy, but over the last six months, its predictive power has gradually declined, leading to an increase in false positives and false negatives. The data science team suspects that customer behavior patterns have evolved, causing the model to become less relevant. To maintain the model''s effectiveness and minimize manual intervention, which strategy should the team implement as part of the ML development lifecycle with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually retrain the model every quarter regardless of performance.","Implement continuous model monitoring to detect data drift and trigger automated retraining.","Increase the number of features used for training without re-evaluating existing data.","Redeploy the exact same model to Amazon SageMaker Endpoints, assuming the issue is with the deployment."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Implementing continuous model monitoring to detect data drift and trigger automated retraining is the most effective strategy for maintaining model effectiveness with the least operational overhead. Model drift occurs when the relationship between input data and target variable changes over time, requiring models to be retrained. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor can detect such drift and trigger alerts or automated retraining workflows, ensuring the model adapts to new patterns. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.3-003] by treating model deployment as an ongoing process, not the end of the lifecycle.

Option A is incorrect because manually retraining every quarter is a fixed schedule that might not align with when drift actually occurs, potentially leading to unnecessary retraining or delayed response to performance degradation. It also involves more manual overhead than an automated solution.

Option C is incorrect because simply increasing features without understanding the data''s evolution or performing proper feature engineering can introduce noise or irrelevant data, potentially worsening performance rather than improving it. This does not address the core issue of model drift.

Option D is incorrect because redeploying the exact same model will not resolve the underlying issue of model drift caused by changing data patterns. The problem lies with the model''s learned patterns becoming stale, not with the deployment mechanism itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_3_34',
  'A healthcare startup is developing an AI model to detect a rare disease from patient medical records. The dataset is highly imbalanced, with only 0.5% of records indicating the presence of the disease. The data science team initially focused on maximizing overall accuracy, achieving 99.5%. However, upon further review, they realized the model was failing to identify most of the actual disease cases. To properly evaluate the model''s ability to identify the rare positive cases, which evaluation metric should the team prioritize?',
  'mcq',
  '["Overall Accuracy, as it reflects the proportion of correctly classified instances.","F1-score, which balances precision and recall, especially useful for imbalanced datasets.","Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), as it measures the average magnitude of errors.","Training Loss, as it indicates how well the model is learning during the training phase."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. The F1-score is a crucial metric for evaluating models on imbalanced datasets because it provides a balance between Precision (the proportion of positive identifications that were actually correct) and Recall (the proportion of actual positives that were correctly identified). In this scenario, high overall accuracy is misleading due to the vast majority of negative cases. A model could achieve 99.5% accuracy by simply predicting ''no disease'' for every patient, but it would fail to identify any actual disease cases. The F1-score helps to ensure that the model is effective at identifying the minority class, addressing anti-pattern [ap-1.3-001].

Option A is incorrect because overall accuracy is highly misleading for imbalanced datasets. A model can achieve high accuracy by correctly classifying the majority class, while completely failing to identify the minority class.

Option C is incorrect because Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) is a metric typically used for regression problems, where the goal is to predict a continuous value, not for classification problems like disease detection.

Option D is incorrect because training loss is an internal metric used to guide the optimization process during model training. While important for understanding if the model is learning, it is not an appropriate metric for evaluating the final performance of a trained model on unseen data, especially for assessing class imbalance issues.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_5_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_20',
  'A creative content agency wants to automate the generation of unique marketing slogans and short social media posts based on a product''s description. They are looking for an AI approach that can produce novel, coherent, and contextually relevant text rather than just classifying existing content. The goal is to rapidly create new textual content for various campaigns. Which type of AI is best suited for this specific task?',
  'mcq',
  '["Discriminative AI for sentiment analysis","Generative AI using Large Language Models (LLMs)","Reinforcement learning for content optimization","Supervised learning for text classification"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option 1 (Generative AI using Large Language Models (LLMs)) is the correct choice. Generative AI focuses on creating new content (text, images, audio, etc.) by learning patterns from existing data. LLMs are a prime example of generative AI capable of producing coherent and contextually relevant text, making them ideal for generating marketing slogans and social media posts. Option 0 (Discriminative AI for sentiment analysis) is incorrect because discriminative AI''s primary function is to classify or predict from input data, not to generate new content (anti-pattern [ap-1.1-003]). Option 2 (Reinforcement learning for content optimization) is a learning paradigm focused on an agent learning through interaction with an environment to maximize rewards, which is not the primary goal here. Option 3 (Supervised learning for text classification) would be used to categorize existing text, not to create new text.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_21',
  'A marketing department at a financial institution wants to segment its customer base to tailor product offerings. They have a vast amount of historical transaction data, demographic information, and interaction logs for millions of customers. However, they do not have predefined labels or categories for customer segments; their goal is to discover inherent groups or patterns within the unlabeled data to understand natural customer groupings. Which type of machine learning approach is MOST appropriate for this task?',
  'mcq',
  '["Unsupervised learning to identify hidden structures and groupings in the data.","Supervised learning to predict customer segments based on historical labels.","Reinforcement learning to optimize customer engagement strategies through trial and error.","Deep learning, as it is the most advanced form of machine learning for any data type."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the correct choice. Unsupervised learning is specifically designed for finding hidden patterns or intrinsic structures in unlabeled datasets. Techniques like clustering (e.g., K-means, hierarchical clustering) are ideal for segmenting customers when no predefined categories exist, directly matching the scenario''s requirement.
Option B is incorrect because supervised learning requires labeled data (i.e., historical examples of customer segments) to train a model to predict those labels. The scenario explicitly states that the company ''do not have predefined labels or categories'', making supervised learning inappropriate. This represents anti-pattern [ap-1.1-002] by confusing supervised and unsupervised learning.
Option C is incorrect because reinforcement learning is used for training agents to make sequences of decisions in an environment to maximize a reward, typically through trial and error. It is not suitable for discovering patterns in static, historical customer data.
Option D is incorrect because deep learning is a subset of machine learning that uses neural networks with many layers. While deep learning can be applied to both supervised and unsupervised tasks, it is a technique or architecture, not a fundamental type of learning paradigm (like supervised or unsupervised). Stating it''s ''most advanced for any data type'' is a generalization and doesn''t specify the learning approach needed for unlabeled data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_22',
  'A marketing analytics firm wants to understand customer behavior by grouping their vast database of customer interactions, purchase history, and website visits. They have collected a large amount of data, but there are no predefined categories or labels for customer segments. The goal is to discover inherent patterns and natural clusters within the data to identify distinct customer groups for targeted campaigns. Which machine learning paradigm is MOST suitable for this task?',
  'mcq',
  '["Supervised learning, as it focuses on predicting outcomes from labeled data.","Unsupervised learning, as it aims to find hidden patterns and structures in unlabeled data.","Reinforcement learning, as it involves an agent learning through trial and error in an environment.","Deep learning, as it uses neural networks to process complex data for classification."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Unsupervised learning is the most suitable paradigm for this task because the goal is to discover hidden patterns and natural groupings (clusters) within unlabeled data. The firm has no predefined customer segments, which is characteristic of problems where unsupervised learning algorithms like clustering are applied. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.1-002] by correctly distinguishing between supervised and unsupervised learning.

Option A is incorrect because supervised learning requires labeled data, meaning the desired output (e.g., specific customer segments) would need to be known beforehand for each data point. The scenario explicitly states there are no predefined categories or labels.

Option C is incorrect because reinforcement learning is used for training agents to make a sequence of decisions in an environment to maximize a reward, which is not applicable to grouping existing customer data.

Option D is incorrect because deep learning is a technique or subset of machine learning that uses neural networks, not a learning paradigm itself in the context of supervised vs. unsupervised. While deep learning models can be used for clustering, the fundamental approach for discovering patterns in unlabeled data falls under unsupervised learning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_23',
  'A creative agency is exploring ways to automate content generation for marketing campaigns. They want an AI system that can produce novel and diverse marketing copy, social media posts, and even short video scripts based on a few input prompts, rather than simply classifying or predicting from existing data. The key requirement is the ability to create new, original content that has not been explicitly seen before. Which category of AI models is specifically designed for this type of creative content generation?',
  'mcq',
  '["Discriminative AI, which focuses on classifying or predicting outcomes from input data.","Supervised learning models, which learn mappings from inputs to outputs using labeled datasets.","Reinforcement learning models, which learn optimal actions through trial and error in an environment.","Generative AI, which creates new content by learning the patterns and distributions of training data."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct. Generative AI models are specifically designed to create new, original content (text, images, audio, code, etc.) by learning the underlying patterns and distributions of their training data. This capability aligns perfectly with the creative agency''s need to produce novel marketing copy and scripts. This option directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.1-003] by correctly identifying generative AI for content creation, distinguishing it from discriminative tasks.

Option A is incorrect because Discriminative AI models are used for tasks like classification or prediction (e.g., determining if an email is spam or predicting a stock price). They discriminate between different categories or predict a value, rather than generating new content.

Option B is incorrect because supervised learning is a paradigm that uses labeled data to learn a mapping from input to output. While generative models can be trained using supervised techniques, ''supervised learning models'' is a broad category, and the specific capability of creating new content is characteristic of generative AI.

Option C is incorrect because reinforcement learning models are focused on an agent learning to make optimal decisions through interactions with an environment to maximize a reward, which is not the primary mechanism for generating creative content.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_1_24',
  'A manufacturing company utilizes an ML model to perform quality control by analyzing images of products on an assembly line. For critical components, a decision must be made within milliseconds to stop the line if a defect is detected. For less critical components, a large batch of images accumulated over an hour can be processed together to identify trends in defects. What are these two distinct operational modes of applying a trained machine learning model to new data called?',
  'mcq',
  '["Real-time inference and batch inference.","Model training and model evaluation.","Data preprocessing and feature engineering.","Supervised learning and unsupervised learning."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. The scenario describes two common modes of applying a trained ML model: real-time inference (or online inference) for immediate, low-latency predictions on individual data points (critical components), and batch inference for processing large volumes of data together at scheduled intervals (less critical components). Both are forms of ''inference,'' which is the process of using a trained model to make predictions on new, unseen data.

Option B is incorrect because model training and model evaluation are stages in the ML development lifecycle, not modes of applying a trained model to new data for predictions. Training builds the model, and evaluation assesses its performance.

Option C is incorrect because data preprocessing and feature engineering are steps involved in preparing data before it is used for training or inference, not modes of applying the model itself.

Option D is incorrect because supervised learning and unsupervised learning are paradigms for how a model learns from data (during the training phase), not modes of making predictions with a deployed model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_5_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_21',
  'A manufacturing plant uses sensors to monitor the temperature of critical machinery. If any sensor reports a temperature exceeding 100 degrees Celsius, an immediate alert must be sent to the maintenance team to prevent equipment damage. The plant manager is considering implementing an advanced machine learning solution to detect these high-temperature events. However, the engineering team argues for a simpler approach. Which solution is most appropriate for this specific problem, considering the requirement for immediate and deterministic alerts?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a simple rule-based system that triggers an alert when temperature > 100°C.","Train an Amazon SageMaker model to predict temperature anomalies based on historical data.","Use Amazon Forecast to predict future temperature spikes and proactively send alerts.","Deploy a deep learning model to analyze sensor data patterns for subtle temperature shifts."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option 0 (Implement a simple rule-based system that triggers an alert when temperature > 100°C) is the most appropriate solution. The problem has a clear, deterministic threshold and requires an immediate, unambiguous response. Machine learning adds unnecessary complexity and cost when a simple rule can solve the problem reliably (anti-pattern [ap-1.2-001]). Option 1 (Train an Amazon SageMaker model to predict temperature anomalies) is overkill for a simple threshold and introduces potential latency and false positives/negatives. Option 2 (Use Amazon Forecast to predict future temperature spikes) is designed for time-series forecasting, not for immediate, rule-based threshold alerts. Option 3 (Deploy a deep learning model to analyze sensor data patterns) is also an overly complex solution for a problem that can be solved with basic conditional logic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_22',
  'A global e-learning platform provides courses in various subjects to students worldwide. They want to enhance accessibility and engagement by offering real-time translation of course materials, student questions, and instructor responses across multiple languages. The platform needs a highly accurate and scalable service that can automatically translate text content without requiring extensive linguistic expertise or custom model development. Which AWS service is best suited for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Polly","Amazon Translate","Amazon Lex","Amazon Comprehend"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option 1 (Amazon Translate) is the correct service. Amazon Translate is a neural machine translation service that delivers fast, high-quality, and affordable language translation. It is specifically designed for translating text between different languages in real-time or in batch. Option 0 (Amazon Polly) converts text into lifelike speech, which is text-to-speech, not text translation. Option 2 (Amazon Lex) is a service for building conversational interfaces (chatbots), not for general text translation. Option 3 (Amazon Comprehend) is an NLP service for tasks like sentiment analysis, entity recognition, and key phrase extraction, but it does not perform language translation. This question tests the ability to identify practical use cases for AWS AI services, specifically NLP for language translation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_23',
  'A human resources department is developing an AI model to screen job applications and recommend candidates for interviews. During initial testing, the team observes that the model consistently ranks candidates from certain educational backgrounds or with specific names lower, despite having comparable qualifications to higher-ranked candidates. The company is committed to fair hiring practices and wants to ensure the AI system does not perpetuate or amplify existing biases. What is the most critical step the team should take to address this issue in line with responsible AI principles?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the model''s overall accuracy metric to improve general performance.","Deploy the model as is, assuming any initial biases will diminish with more data over time.","Evaluate the training dataset for inherent biases and lack of diverse representation, then retrain and re-evaluate the model.","Manually adjust the ranking of lower-ranked candidates from affected groups after the model''s predictions."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option 2 (Evaluate the training dataset for inherent biases and lack of diverse representation, then retrain and re-evaluate the model) is the most critical and principled step. AI models learn from the data they are trained on, and if that data contains historical or systemic biases, the model will perpetuate and amplify them (anti-pattern [ap-1.2-002]). Addressing the root cause in the data and then retraining is essential for fairness. Option 0 (Increase the model''s overall accuracy metric) is insufficient because high accuracy does not guarantee fairness and can mask bias (anti-pattern [ap-1.3-001]). Option 1 (Deploy the model as is, assuming any initial biases will diminish with more data over time) is irresponsible and directly contradicts responsible AI principles by ignoring known bias. Option 3 (Manually adjust the ranking) is a reactive, unsustainable workaround that does not fix the underlying problem in the model or data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_24',
  'A global media company manages an extensive archive of news footage, comprising millions of video clips from various events over decades. They need to quickly identify and extract all segments within these videos where specific public figures (e.g., politicians, celebrities) appear, to enable efficient content search and compilation of highlight reels. The company wants to achieve this with the LEAST development effort and without building custom machine learning models from scratch.',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually review all video footage to tag appearances of public figures.","Develop a custom computer vision model using Amazon SageMaker to detect public figures.","Utilize Amazon Rekognition''s celebrity recognition feature to analyze video content.","Employ Amazon Comprehend to analyze audio transcripts from the videos for mentions of names."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the correct choice. Amazon Rekognition is a fully managed artificial intelligence service that provides pre-trained computer vision capabilities, including celebrity recognition in images and videos. This service requires minimal development effort as it''s an API call, directly addressing the requirement to identify public figures without building custom ML models.
Option A is incorrect because manually reviewing millions of video clips is an extremely labor-intensive, time-consuming, and impractical approach for an extensive archive, failing the ''LEAST development effort'' and efficiency constraints.
Option B is incorrect because developing a custom computer vision model using Amazon SageMaker, while technically feasible, requires significant ML expertise, data labeling, model training, and deployment effort. This contradicts the requirement for ''LEAST development effort'' and ''without building custom machine learning models from scratch''. This represents anti-pattern [ap-1.2-001] by opting for a custom ML solution when a managed AI service is readily available for the specific task.
Option D is incorrect because Amazon Comprehend is a natural language processing (NLP) service designed to analyze text. While it could analyze transcripts for mentions of names, it would not visually identify public figures appearing in the video footage itself, which is the core requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_25',
  'A small online bookstore wants to implement a system to recommend books to customers. The current approach is to recommend books based on simple rules: if a customer buys a ''fantasy'' book, recommend other ''fantasy'' books; if they buy a ''sci-fi'' book, recommend other ''sci-fi'' books. The company is considering using machine learning to improve recommendations but is concerned about the complexity and cost. Given the current simple, deterministic logic, which approach is MOST appropriate for the bookstore to continue providing recommendations with the LEAST complexity and cost?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploying Amazon Personalize to generate highly tailored recommendations for each customer.","Continuing with the existing rule-based system using conditional logic.","Training an Amazon SageMaker model to classify customer preferences and recommend books.","Implementing an Amazon Lex chatbot to ask customers for their preferred genres."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Continuing with the existing rule-based system using conditional logic is the most appropriate approach for the bookstore given the stated constraint of ''LEAST complexity and cost'' for simple, deterministic logic. The problem can be solved effectively with straightforward rules, making machine learning an unnecessary overhead in terms of development, deployment, and ongoing maintenance. This directly aligns with anti-pattern [ap-1.2-001], which advises against applying ML to problems solvable by simple rule-based logic.

Option A is incorrect because deploying Amazon Personalize, while excellent for complex recommendation engines, introduces significant complexity and cost compared to a simple rule-based system. It is an advanced ML service that is overkill for the current requirement.

Option C is incorrect because training an Amazon SageMaker model would involve data collection, preprocessing, model training, and deployment, incurring substantial complexity and cost that is not justified for the current simple recommendation logic. This also falls under anti-pattern [ap-1.2-001].

Option D is incorrect because implementing an Amazon Lex chatbot is for conversational interfaces and would not directly solve the problem of recommending books based on purchase history. It adds an entirely different layer of interaction not requested by the scenario.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_26',
  'A technology company is developing an AI-powered hiring tool to automatically filter resumes and identify top candidates. The model is trained on historical hiring data, which includes past successful candidates and their attributes. There are concerns that the historical data might reflect unconscious biases, potentially leading the AI model to perpetuate discriminatory hiring practices. To ensure the ethical deployment of this AI tool, which principle of Responsible AI is MOST crucial to address during development and before deployment?',
  'mcq',
  '["Robustness, by ensuring the model is resilient to adversarial attacks and data poisoning.","Transparency, by making the model''s decision-making process easily interpretable by humans.","Fairness, by rigorously evaluating the model for discriminatory patterns and ensuring equitable outcomes across demographic groups.","Scalability, by designing the model to handle a large volume of resumes efficiently without performance degradation."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct. Fairness is the most crucial principle to address in this scenario. If the historical hiring data contains biases, an AI model trained on that data will learn and perpetuate those biases, leading to discriminatory outcomes in resume filtering. Rigorously evaluating the model for discriminatory patterns and ensuring equitable outcomes across demographic groups is essential for responsible AI deployment, directly addressing anti-pattern [ap-1.2-002].

Option A is incorrect because robustness, while important for model security and reliability, is not the primary concern related to ethical bias in hiring decisions. It focuses on the model''s performance under malicious or unexpected inputs.

Option B is incorrect because transparency (or interpretability) is important for understanding how a model makes decisions, which can help in identifying bias, but the core issue here is the biased outcome itself, which falls under fairness. Transparency is a means to achieve fairness, but fairness is the direct principle being violated.

Option D is incorrect because scalability is an operational concern related to the model''s ability to handle workload, not an ethical principle addressing bias or discrimination.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_27',
  'A global customer support center receives a large volume of customer feedback in various languages through text channels. The company wants to automatically understand the sentiment of these messages (positive, negative, neutral) and identify key phrases or entities mentioned, regardless of the original language. The goal is to process this feedback efficiently and gain insights into customer satisfaction trends across different regions. Which combination of AWS AI services would BEST meet these requirements for language processing and sentiment analysis?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Transcribe for language translation and Amazon Polly for sentiment analysis.","Amazon Rekognition for sentiment analysis and Amazon Textract for language translation.","Amazon Forecast for sentiment analysis and Amazon Personalize for entity extraction.","Amazon Translate for language translation and Amazon Comprehend for sentiment analysis and entity extraction."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct. Amazon Translate is a fully managed neural machine translation service that can accurately translate text from various languages into a common language (e.g., English). Amazon Comprehend is a natural language processing (NLP) service that can analyze text to detect sentiment (positive, negative, neutral) and extract key phrases and entities. This combination directly addresses the requirements for multi-language text processing, sentiment analysis, and entity extraction using managed AWS AI services.

Option A is incorrect because Amazon Transcribe is a speech-to-text service, not for language translation of text, and Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service, not for sentiment analysis.

Option B is incorrect because Amazon Rekognition is a computer vision service for image and video analysis, not for text-based sentiment analysis. Amazon Textract is for extracting text and data from documents, not for translating arbitrary text.

Option C is incorrect because Amazon Forecast is a time-series forecasting service, and Amazon Personalize is a recommendation engine service. Neither is designed for language translation, sentiment analysis, or entity extraction from text.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_5_m_5_2_28',
  'A construction company wants to enhance safety on its job sites by automatically detecting the presence of hard hats and safety vests in real-time video feeds from surveillance cameras. The company needs a solution that can process live video streams to identify people and specific safety equipment without requiring extensive machine learning expertise or custom model development. Which AWS AI service is purpose-built to provide this kind of computer vision capability for object detection in images and videos?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker, by building and training a custom object detection model.","Amazon Rekognition, utilizing its pre-trained computer vision capabilities.","Amazon Textract, to extract safety equipment information from video frames.","Amazon Comprehend, to analyze the sentiment of safety violations."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Amazon Rekognition is a fully managed computer vision service that can detect objects, people, text, and activities in images and videos. It offers pre-trained models that can identify common objects like hard hats and safety vests, making it ideal for the scenario''s requirements without needing extensive ML expertise or custom model development. For specific custom objects, Amazon Rekognition Custom Labels can be used, but the core service provides general object detection.

Option A is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker requires significant machine learning expertise to build, train, and deploy custom models. While it can be used for object detection, the scenario explicitly asks for a solution ''without requiring extensive machine learning expertise or custom model development,'' making Amazon Rekognition a more suitable, managed AI service for this specific task.

Option C is incorrect because Amazon Textract is designed for extracting text and data from documents, not for detecting objects like hard hats or safety vests in video streams.

Option D is incorrect because Amazon Comprehend is a natural language processing (NLP) service used for text analysis, such as sentiment detection and entity extraction, and has no capabilities for computer vision tasks like object detection in video.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain5_1',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_5_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

