INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_18',
  'A technology startup is developing a new customer service application that requires integrating a large language model (LLM) to handle common customer inquiries and generate personalized responses. The development team is small, and the company prioritizes minimizing the operational overhead associated with managing the underlying infrastructure for the LLM. They need to quickly access and utilize state-of-the-art foundation models without deep expertise in model deployment or server management. Which AWS service should the startup choose to meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Bedrock, leveraging its managed foundation models and APIs.","Deploying a pre-trained LLM on Amazon SageMaker JumpStart instances.","Provisioning Amazon EC2 instances with AWS Trainium accelerators and self-managing an open-source LLM.","Setting up a custom containerized LLM on Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) for maximum control."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Amazon Bedrock provides serverless access to a variety of leading foundation models through a simple API, significantly reducing operational overhead. Developers can focus on building their applications without managing any underlying infrastructure, model deployment, or scaling. Option B is incorrect because while Amazon SageMaker JumpStart simplifies model deployment, it still involves managing SageMaker endpoints and potentially instances, which has higher operational overhead than Amazon Bedrock''s fully managed API access. Option C is incorrect because self-managing an LLM on Amazon EC2 instances with AWS Trainium requires extensive expertise in infrastructure management, model serving, scaling, and security, representing significant operational overhead (Anti-pattern ap-2.3-001). Option D is incorrect because deploying and managing a custom LLM on Amazon EKS introduces even greater operational complexity and overhead, requiring Kubernetes expertise and ongoing cluster management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_19',
  'A healthcare company is building an internal knowledge base chatbot to assist medical staff with queries about specific patient treatment protocols and drug interactions. This information is highly sensitive, frequently updated, and stored in various proprietary documents. The chatbot needs to provide accurate, up-to-date answers by semantically searching through these documents and grounding its responses in the retrieved information. The company requires an efficient and scalable solution for storing and querying the document embeddings. Which AWS service combination is MOST suitable for managing the knowledge base for this Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) use case?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon DynamoDB for storing document text and performing exact-match lookups.","Amazon OpenSearch Service with k-NN vector search capabilities for storing and querying embeddings.","Amazon S3 for storing document files and using S3 Select for content retrieval.","Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) with a standard relational schema for text storage."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because Amazon OpenSearch Service with k-NN (k-Nearest Neighbor) vector search capabilities is specifically designed for efficient storage and semantic similarity search of vector embeddings, which is crucial for RAG architectures. This allows the LLM to retrieve contextually relevant information from the proprietary documents. Option A is incorrect because Amazon DynamoDB is a key-value and document database that primarily supports exact-match or range queries, not semantic similarity search on vector embeddings. Using it for RAG knowledge bases would be inefficient and ineffective (Anti-pattern ap-2.3-002). Option C is incorrect because Amazon S3 is object storage and, while it can store documents, it does not inherently provide vector indexing or semantic search capabilities required for RAG. S3 Select is for filtering data within objects, not for similarity search across a knowledge base. Option D is incorrect because Amazon RDS, a relational database, is not optimized for vector similarity search out-of-the-box. While pgvector can be added to Amazon Aurora, a standard relational schema alone is insufficient for efficient semantic search.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_20',
  'A software development firm wants to enhance its developers'' productivity by providing an AI assistant that can help with code generation, debugging, explaining complex code snippets, and answering general programming questions. The firm is looking for a solution that integrates seamlessly into their development environment and provides accurate, context-aware assistance without requiring the developers to switch between multiple tools or manage separate AI services. Which AWS service is purpose-built to address these developer needs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Bedrock for accessing various foundation models.","Amazon SageMaker JumpStart for deploying pre-trained models.","Amazon Q Developer for business productivity and developer assistance.","AWS Inferentia instances for high-performance inference."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because Amazon Q Developer is specifically designed as an AI assistant for developers, providing capabilities like code generation, debugging, code explanation, and answering technical questions. It integrates with IDEs and other development tools to enhance developer productivity. Option A is incorrect because Amazon Bedrock provides API access to foundation models, but it is a general-purpose service for building generative AI applications, not a specialized developer assistant. Developers would need to build their own assistant on top of Bedrock. Option B is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker JumpStart is used for deploying pre-trained models or fine-tuning them, which is not the primary function of a developer assistant. Option D is incorrect because AWS Inferentia instances are hardware accelerators for high-performance inference, not an end-user service or tool for developer assistance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_21',
  'A small startup wants to integrate a large language model (LLM) into their customer support chatbot to generate natural language responses. They have limited DevOps resources and prioritize minimizing infrastructure management and operational overhead. The solution must be scalable and cost-effective for varying user loads, allowing them to focus on application development rather than underlying infrastructure.',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize Amazon Bedrock to access a managed foundation model via API, integrating it directly into their application.","Self-host an open-source LLM on Amazon EC2 instances, managing all dependencies, scaling, and security patches.","Deploy a pre-trained LLM from Amazon SageMaker JumpStart on a dedicated Amazon SageMaker endpoint, managing endpoint scaling.","Develop a custom LLM from scratch using AWS Trainium instances, then deploy and manage it on Amazon SageMaker."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the most suitable choice for minimizing operational overhead. Amazon Bedrock provides serverless access to leading foundation models via a simple API, abstracting away all infrastructure management, scaling, and patching. This allows the startup to focus entirely on integrating the LLM into their application.
Option B is incorrect because self-hosting an LLM on Amazon EC2 instances requires significant operational overhead for managing the infrastructure, dependencies, scaling, and security, directly violating the constraint of least operational overhead. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.3-001].
Option C is incorrect because while Amazon SageMaker JumpStart simplifies deploying pre-trained models, managing the Amazon SageMaker endpoint (including scaling and monitoring) still involves more operational overhead compared to the fully managed, serverless experience of Amazon Bedrock.
Option D is incorrect because developing a custom LLM from scratch using AWS Trainium instances and then deploying it on Amazon SageMaker represents the highest level of operational overhead and complexity, which is entirely contrary to the startup''s goal.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_22',
  'A startup is developing a new customer service chatbot that needs to generate personalized responses based on user queries. The development team is small and has limited MLOps expertise. They want to leverage a large language model (LLM) for text generation but need to minimize the time and effort spent on infrastructure management, model deployment, and scaling. The solution must provide access to state-of-the-art foundation models with the LEAST operational overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy an open-source LLM on Amazon EC2 instances, manage its lifecycle with Amazon SageMaker, and handle scaling manually.","Utilize Amazon Bedrock to access a managed foundation model via API, allowing the team to focus solely on application development.","Use Amazon SageMaker JumpStart to deploy a pre-trained LLM model endpoint, then manage the underlying infrastructure for scaling and updates.","Develop a custom LLM from scratch using AWS Trainium instances and deploy it as a custom endpoint on Amazon SageMaker."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Amazon Bedrock provides serverless access to leading foundation models via API, significantly reducing operational overhead by eliminating the need for infrastructure management, model deployment, and scaling. This allows the small development team to focus on application logic.
Option A is incorrect. Self-hosting an open-source LLM on Amazon EC2 and managing it with Amazon SageMaker requires substantial operational effort for deployment, scaling, security, and updates, which contradicts the ''LEAST operational overhead'' constraint. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.3-001].
Option C is incorrect. While Amazon SageMaker JumpStart simplifies initial deployment, managing the underlying infrastructure for scaling and and updates for the deployed endpoint still incurs operational overhead compared to a fully managed service like Amazon Bedrock.
Option D is incorrect. Developing a custom LLM from scratch is a highly complex, time-consuming, and resource-intensive task, requiring deep ML expertise and significant operational overhead, which is not suitable for a startup with limited MLOps expertise and a goal of minimal overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_23',
  'A data science team is tasked with quickly deploying a pre-trained image classification model to an API endpoint for a new proof-of-concept application. The team has identified a suitable model from a public repository and wants to avoid the complexities of manual model hosting, infrastructure provisioning, and endpoint management. They need to deploy this model with the FASTEST time to production for their prototype.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon SageMaker JumpStart to select and deploy a pre-trained model directly to a SageMaker endpoint with minimal configuration.","Manually download the model, containerize it using Docker, upload it to Amazon ECR, and deploy it to an Amazon ECS cluster.","Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to load the model from Amazon S3 and perform inference, managing function scaling and concurrency.","Train a new custom image classification model from scratch using Amazon SageMaker training jobs and then deploy it to an endpoint."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. Amazon SageMaker JumpStart provides a streamlined process to quickly deploy pre-trained models from a curated hub directly to a SageMaker endpoint. This significantly reduces the time and effort required for model hosting, infrastructure setup, and endpoint management, making it the fastest option for deploying a prototype.
Option B is incorrect. Manually containerizing, uploading, and deploying a model to Amazon ECS involves significant manual steps and operational overhead, which is not the fastest approach for a quick prototype deployment.
Option C is incorrect. While AWS Lambda can be used for inference, loading large models from Amazon S3 and managing cold starts, concurrency, and scaling for a production-grade model endpoint can be complex and time-consuming, especially for a data science team focused on speed.
Option D is incorrect. Training a new custom model from scratch is a lengthy process involving data preparation, model architecture selection, training, and evaluation. This is not suitable when the goal is to quickly deploy an *already pre-trained* model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_24',
  'A large e-commerce company is developing a new product recommendation engine powered by a custom deep learning model. After extensive training, the model is ready for deployment. The company anticipates extremely high inference traffic, requiring low latency and high throughput for real-time recommendations. They need to deploy this model on specialized hardware to achieve the MOST cost-effective high-throughput inference for their production workload.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the model on general-purpose Amazon EC2 instances (e.g., M-series) and scale horizontally to handle the load.","Package the model into a container and deploy it to an Amazon EKS cluster running on GPU-enabled Amazon EC2 instances.","Deploy the model on Amazon EC2 instances with AWS Trainium accelerators, primarily designed for efficient deep learning model training.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker endpoints configured with AWS Inferentia instances, optimized for high-performance, cost-effective inference."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct. AWS Inferentia instances are purpose-built by AWS for high-performance, low-cost deep learning inference. Deploying the model on Amazon SageMaker endpoints configured with Inferentia provides a managed solution that is specifically optimized for high-throughput inference workloads, making it the most cost-effective choice for this scenario.
Option A is incorrect. General-purpose Amazon EC2 instances are not optimized for deep learning inference and would likely be less cost-effective and performant than specialized accelerators for high-throughput workloads.
Option B is incorrect. While GPU-enabled Amazon EC2 instances can provide high performance, managing an Amazon EKS cluster adds significant operational overhead. Inferentia instances on SageMaker offer a more specialized and often more cost-effective solution for inference compared to general-purpose GPUs, especially when considering the managed service aspect.
Option C is incorrect. AWS Trainium accelerators are designed for deep learning *training*, not inference. Using them for inference would be suboptimal and not cost-effective for a production inference workload.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_25',
  'A small startup needs to integrate a large language model (LLM) into their customer support chatbot to provide immediate responses to common queries. They have limited DevOps resources and want to minimize the time spent on infrastructure management, model deployment, and scaling. The solution must allow them to quickly experiment with different LLMs without significant upfront investment in hardware or complex setup. The primary goal is to achieve this with the LEAST operational overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize Amazon Bedrock to access a managed foundation model via an API, integrating it directly into their application.","Deploy a pre-trained LLM from Hugging Face on Amazon EC2 instances, manually managing the model serving, scaling, security patches, and underlying infrastructure.","Use Amazon SageMaker JumpStart to deploy a foundation model, then manage the SageMaker endpoint and underlying infrastructure.","Purchase AWS Trainium instances and fine-tune an open-source LLM from scratch, then deploy it on custom infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. Amazon Bedrock provides serverless access to leading foundation models via an API, eliminating the need for infrastructure management, model deployment, and scaling. This significantly reduces operational overhead and allows for rapid experimentation.
Option B is incorrect. Self-hosting an LLM on Amazon EC2 instances requires substantial operational effort for deployment, scaling, security, and maintenance, directly contradicting the goal of least operational overhead. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.3-001].
Option C is incorrect. While Amazon SageMaker JumpStart simplifies model deployment, it still requires managing SageMaker endpoints and understanding underlying infrastructure considerations, which is more operational overhead than Amazon Bedrock''s fully managed API access.
Option D is incorrect. Fine-tuning an LLM from scratch and deploying it on custom infrastructure involves immense operational overhead, high costs, and specialized expertise, making it unsuitable for a startup seeking minimal management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_26',
  'A large enterprise wants to empower its sales team with a generative AI assistant that can answer questions about internal sales playbooks, product specifications, and customer relationship management (CRM) data. The solution needs to be deployed quickly, require minimal custom development, and securely connect to various enterprise data sources to provide accurate, grounded responses. The primary objective is to enhance sales team productivity with the LEAST amount of development effort.',
  'mcq',
  '["Develop a custom web application, integrate an LLM via Amazon Bedrock, and build a RAG pipeline to connect to data sources, requiring significant development effort.","Use Amazon SageMaker JumpStart to deploy a pre-trained LLM and build a custom front-end application for the sales team.","Implement Amazon Q Business, connecting it to existing enterprise data sources like Amazon S3, SharePoint, and Salesforce.","Set up an Amazon EC2 instance with an open-source LLM and manually configure connectors to various data repositories."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct. Amazon Q Business is specifically designed for enterprise productivity, offering a managed generative AI assistant that can securely connect to various enterprise data sources (e.g., Amazon S3, SharePoint, Salesforce) out-of-the-box. It requires minimal custom development to deploy and provides grounded, accurate responses based on proprietary data.
Option A is incorrect. Developing a custom web application and RAG pipeline, even with Amazon Bedrock, involves significant development effort and time, which contradicts the goal of least development effort.
Option B is incorrect. While Amazon SageMaker JumpStart provides pre-trained models, building a custom front-end application and integrating it for a business-specific assistant still requires substantial development effort.
Option D is incorrect. Self-hosting an open-source LLM on Amazon EC2 and manually configuring data connectors would involve high operational overhead and significant development effort, making it unsuitable for the stated requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_6_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_16',
  'A content creation company is using a large language model (LLM) to generate creative story ideas and diverse marketing slogans for various campaigns. The team finds that the LLM sometimes produces very similar or repetitive outputs, limiting their brainstorming effectiveness. They want the LLM to explore a wider range of possibilities and generate more imaginative and varied suggestions. Which inference parameter should the company adjust to achieve greater diversity and creativity in the LLM''s outputs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the `temperature` parameter to a higher value.","Set the `temperature` parameter to 0.","Decrease the `max_tokens` parameter.","Increase the `top-k` parameter to a very high value."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because increasing the `temperature` parameter makes the LLM''s output more random and creative by allowing it to consider a wider range of less probable tokens. This is ideal for tasks like brainstorming or creative writing where diversity is desired. Option B is incorrect because setting the `temperature` to 0 makes the LLM''s output highly deterministic and focused, always choosing the most probable token. This is suitable for factual Q&A or code generation but would severely limit creativity and diversity (Anti-pattern ap-2.1-001). Option C is incorrect because decreasing the `max_tokens` parameter only limits the length of the generated output and does not influence its creativity or diversity. Option D is incorrect because increasing `top-k` allows the model to consider more tokens from the most probable ones, which can increase diversity to some extent, but `temperature` has a more direct and pronounced effect on the overall randomness and creativity of the output.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_17',
  'A research institution is utilizing a large language model (LLM) to analyze and summarize extensive scientific papers, some of which are hundreds of pages long. The team has observed that the LLM often misses crucial details or provides incomplete summaries for these very long documents. They suspect that the entire document content is not being fully processed by the model. The institution needs to ensure that the LLM considers all relevant information from these lengthy inputs to produce comprehensive and accurate summaries. What is the MOST effective strategy to address this limitation?',
  'mcq',
  '["Send the entire document as a single, long prompt to the LLM.","Implement a chunking strategy to break down the document into smaller, manageable segments and process them iteratively or with a model with a larger context window.","Increase the `max_tokens` parameter for the LLM''s output to allow for longer summaries.","Set the `temperature` parameter to 0 to ensure deterministic and focused output."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because LLMs have finite context windows. When input content exceeds this limit, it is often truncated or ignored, leading to incomplete responses. Implementing a chunking strategy to break down long documents into smaller segments that fit within the context window, or using a foundation model specifically designed with a larger context window, ensures all relevant information is processed. Option A is incorrect because sending the entire document as a single prompt will likely result in truncation, as most LLMs have context window limits that would be exceeded by hundreds of pages of text (Anti-pattern ap-2.1-003). Option C is incorrect because increasing the `max_tokens` parameter only controls the length of the LLM''s generated output, not the amount of input context it can process. Option D is incorrect because setting the `temperature` to 0 makes the output more deterministic but does not address the fundamental limitation of the context window for processing long inputs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_18',
  'A marketing team is leveraging a large language model (LLM) to generate social media captions for various product launches. They want the captions to consistently follow a specific brand voice – witty, concise, and engaging – and always include a call to action. The team has a few excellent examples of past captions that perfectly embody this style. They need a prompt engineering technique that can guide the LLM to replicate this desired style and structure without requiring extensive model retraining. Which technique should they employ?',
  'mcq',
  '["Zero-shot prompting, providing only the task description.","Few-shot prompting, including examples of desired captions in the prompt.","Chain-of-thought prompting, guiding the model through a reasoning process.","Increasing the `top-p` parameter to allow for more diverse word choices."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because few-shot prompting involves providing the LLM with a few examples of the desired input-output pairs within the prompt itself. This allows the model to learn the desired style, format, and tone from the examples, making it highly effective for tasks requiring specific output characteristics like brand voice and structure. Option A is incorrect because zero-shot prompting provides only the task description without examples, which would likely result in generic outputs that do not consistently match the specific brand voice. Option C is incorrect because chain-of-thought prompting is used to guide the model through multi-step reasoning processes, which is not the primary requirement for replicating a specific writing style. Option D is incorrect because increasing the `top-p` parameter influences the diversity of token selection during generation but does not directly guide the model to adopt a specific stylistic pattern or structure from examples.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_19',
  'A financial news agency uses a large language model (LLM) to generate summaries of market reports and company earnings calls. While the LLM generally performs well, it occasionally produces statements that sound highly plausible but are factually incorrect or misinterpret financial data. These ''hallucinations'' pose a significant risk due to the high-stakes nature of financial information. The agency needs a strategy to mitigate the risk of providing inaccurate information to its readers. Which approach is MOST effective for addressing LLM hallucinations in this high-stakes scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the `max_tokens` parameter to allow the LLM to generate more detailed summaries.","Set the `temperature` parameter to a higher value to encourage more creative and diverse outputs.","Implement human review and fact-checking for all LLM-generated financial summaries, potentially combined with Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) from verified sources.","Trust all LLM outputs without validation, assuming the model''s training data is always current and accurate."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because for high-stakes applications like financial reporting, human review and fact-checking are mandatory to catch and correct LLM hallucinations. Additionally, integrating Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) with verified, up-to-date financial data sources can ground the LLM''s responses in factual information, significantly reducing the likelihood of generating incorrect statements. Option A is incorrect because increasing `max_tokens` only affects the length of the output and does not address the accuracy or factual correctness of the content. Option B is incorrect because setting the `temperature` to a higher value would increase the randomness and creativity of the output, making hallucinations more likely, which is counterproductive for factual tasks. Option D is incorrect because blindly trusting LLM outputs without validation for factual or high-stakes tasks is an anti-pattern (ap-2.1-002) that can lead to severe consequences due to the inherent tendency of LLMs to hallucinate plausible but incorrect information.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_20',
  'A content creation team uses a generative AI model to draft marketing slogans for new product launches. They initially provide only the product name and desired tone, but the output quality is inconsistent, often lacking creativity or relevance to specific brand guidelines. They need to improve the relevance and creativity of the slogans without modifying the underlying model or incurring significant development costs.',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement zero-shot prompting by only providing the product name and a single instruction.","Adjust the model''s temperature parameter to zero to ensure highly deterministic output for all slogan generations.","Use few-shot prompting by including several examples of high-quality, brand-aligned marketing slogans along with the product name and tone in the prompt.","Fine-tune the foundation model with a large, curated dataset of successful marketing slogans from previous campaigns."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the most effective approach. Few-shot prompting involves providing the model with a few examples of desired input-output pairs within the prompt itself. This helps the model understand the desired style, tone, and relevance, leading to more consistent and creative outputs aligned with brand guidelines, without modifying the underlying model or incurring significant development costs.
Option A is incorrect because zero-shot prompting, where only instructions are given without examples, is what the team is likely already doing, leading to inconsistent results. It doesn''t provide the model with enough context to improve relevance and creativity.
Option B is incorrect because setting the temperature parameter to zero makes the model''s output highly deterministic and less creative, which is contrary to the goal of improving creativity. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.1-001] regarding one-size-fits-all inference parameters.
Option D is incorrect because fine-tuning the foundation model, while effective, involves significant development costs, data curation, and model retraining, which violates the constraint of not modifying the underlying model or incurring significant development costs. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.2-001].',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_21',
  'A marketing team is developing a new campaign and wants to use a generative AI model to brainstorm novel slogans and advertising copy. They need the model to produce a wide variety of creative and imaginative suggestions, exploring different linguistic styles and ideas rather than sticking to the most probable or factual responses. The goal is to maximize the diversity and inventiveness of the generated text to inspire new marketing angles.',
  'mcq',
  '["Set the `temperature` parameter to a very low value, close to 0, to ensure highly focused, deterministic, and less creative outputs, which is suboptimal for brainstorming.","Configure the `max_tokens` parameter to a high value, allowing the model to generate very long and detailed responses.","Set the `top_k` parameter to 1, forcing the model to always select the single most probable next token at each step.","Adjust the `temperature` parameter to a higher value (e.g., 0.7-1.0) to increase the randomness and creativity of the generated text."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct. The `temperature` parameter controls the randomness of the model''s output. A higher temperature (e.g., 0.7-1.0) makes the output more diverse, creative, and less deterministic, which is ideal for brainstorming and generating novel ideas.
Option A is incorrect. Setting `temperature` to a very low value (close to 0) makes the model''s output highly deterministic and focused, producing the most probable tokens. This is suitable for factual Q&A or code generation but stifles creativity, aligning with anti-pattern [ap-2.1-001].
Option B is incorrect. The `max_tokens` parameter controls the maximum length of the generated output, not its creativity or diversity. While longer outputs might contain more ideas, `max_tokens` doesn''t directly influence the inventiveness of the content.
Option C is incorrect. Setting `top_k` to 1 means the model only considers the single most probable next token, which severely limits diversity and creativity, making the output highly deterministic and predictable.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_22',
  'A research analyst needs to summarize lengthy scientific papers using a large language model (LLM) available through Amazon Bedrock. Some papers are extremely long, exceeding tens of thousands of words. The analyst observes that the summaries sometimes miss critical details present in the latter parts of the documents. The objective is to ensure that the LLM processes the entire content of each paper to produce comprehensive and accurate summaries, avoiding information loss.',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a chunking strategy to break down long documents into smaller segments, processing each segment separately and then combining the summaries.","Increase the `max_tokens` parameter in the LLM inference configuration to allow for longer output summaries.","Use a different LLM that is known to have a higher `temperature` setting, which will improve its ability to summarize long texts.","Assume the LLM automatically handles documents of any length by internally compressing the input, so no special action is needed, leading to potential information loss."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. Large language models have finite context windows, meaning they can only process a limited number of tokens at a time. For documents exceeding this limit, a chunking strategy is necessary to break the document into smaller, manageable segments. Each segment can then be processed (e.g., summarized), and the individual summaries combined or further processed.
Option B is incorrect. The `max_tokens` parameter controls the length of the *output* generated by the LLM, not the length of the *input* context it can process. Increasing `max_tokens` will not help the LLM process a longer input document.
Option C is incorrect. The `temperature` setting influences the randomness and creativity of the LLM''s output. It does not affect the model''s ability to process longer input contexts or prevent information loss due to context window limits.
Option D is incorrect. LLMs do not automatically handle arbitrarily long documents by compressing input. Content exceeding the context window is typically truncated or ignored, leading to incomplete responses. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.1-003].',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_23',
  'A financial advisory firm is exploring the use of a generative AI assistant to help financial advisors draft initial responses to client queries regarding investment strategies and market trends. The firm''s top priority is to ensure that all information provided to clients is factually accurate and reliable, as incorrect advice could have severe consequences. They are aware that large language models can sometimes generate plausible but incorrect information.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the LLM with a very high `temperature` setting to encourage more creative and diverse responses, which will inherently be more accurate.","Rely solely on the LLM''s output for client responses, assuming its extensive training data guarantees accuracy.","Fine-tune the LLM on a large dataset of general financial news articles to improve its overall knowledge and reduce the likelihood of hallucinations, which is an expensive and time-consuming process that does not guarantee factual accuracy for specific client queries.","Implement a human review process for all LLM-generated responses, combined with Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) using verified internal documents."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct. For high-stakes applications like financial advice, it is crucial to validate LLM outputs. Combining a human review process with Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) ensures that responses are grounded in verified, internal documents and checked for accuracy before being shared with clients. This directly addresses the risk of hallucinations.
Option A is incorrect. A high `temperature` setting increases the randomness and creativity of the LLM''s output, which can exacerbate hallucinations and make responses less factual and reliable, not more accurate.
Option B is incorrect. Relying solely on LLM output for factual or high-stakes tasks is a dangerous anti-pattern [ap-2.1-002]. LLMs can hallucinate, generating plausible-sounding but incorrect information, which is unacceptable for financial advice.
Option C is incorrect. While fine-tuning can adapt an LLM to a specific domain, it does not eliminate the risk of hallucinations or guarantee factual accuracy for specific, real-time queries. It''s also a costly and time-consuming process that doesn''t replace the need for validation in high-stakes scenarios.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_6_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_18',
  'An enterprise customer support department wants to deploy a generative AI chatbot to handle common customer inquiries. The chatbot needs to provide accurate and up-to-date information about the company''s specific products, services, and internal policies, which are constantly evolving and stored in various internal documents and databases. Relying solely on a base foundation model would result in generic or outdated responses due to its knowledge cutoff. The department needs a solution that can ground the LLM''s responses in the most current, proprietary company knowledge. Which approach is MOST suitable for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) by connecting the LLM to a knowledge base containing the company''s internal documents and data.","Use a base foundation model directly, assuming its pre-training data is sufficient for all company-specific knowledge.","Fine-tune a foundation model with all company-specific documents and continuously retrain it with daily updates.","Increase the LLM''s `temperature` parameter to make responses more conversational and engaging."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) allows an LLM to retrieve relevant, up-to-date information from a proprietary knowledge base and use it to ground its responses. This is ideal for enterprise chatbots that need to provide accurate answers based on constantly evolving internal data, overcoming the LLM''s knowledge cutoff limitation. Option B is incorrect because using a base foundation model directly without grounding it in company-specific knowledge will lead to generic, potentially outdated, or incorrect answers, as the model''s training data has a cutoff and doesn''t include proprietary information (Anti-pattern ap-2.2-002). Option C is incorrect because fine-tuning a foundation model with daily updates is expensive, time-consuming, and operationally complex, especially for frequently changing information. RAG is a more efficient and cost-effective solution for dynamic knowledge. Option D is incorrect because increasing the `temperature` parameter affects the creativity and diversity of the output, not its factual accuracy or ability to access proprietary knowledge.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_19',
  'A small marketing agency wants to use a large language model (LLM) to generate short, engaging social media posts that adhere to a very specific, quirky brand voice. They have a collection of about 50 high-performing posts that perfectly capture this voice. The agency has limited budget and technical resources, so they need a solution that achieves the desired brand consistency with the LEAST development effort and cost, without requiring extensive data labeling or model training. Which approach should the agency prioritize?',
  'mcq',
  '["Fine-tune a foundation model using their collection of 50 social media posts as training data.","Utilize prompt engineering techniques, such as few-shot prompting, to guide the LLM to generate posts in the desired brand voice.","Develop and train a custom LLM from scratch on their specific brand data.","Implement Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) with a knowledge base of social media posts."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because prompt engineering, especially few-shot prompting, is a highly cost-effective and low-effort way to guide an LLM to adopt a specific style or tone using a few examples. For a small dataset of 50 examples and a requirement for a specific brand voice, this approach is typically sufficient and avoids the complexity and cost of fine-tuning. Option A is incorrect because fine-tuning a foundation model for a specific brand voice with only 50 examples is generally not recommended. Fine-tuning is expensive and time-consuming, and such a small dataset might not be sufficient to significantly alter the model''s behavior effectively, making it an anti-pattern when prompt engineering would suffice (Anti-pattern ap-2.2-001). Option C is incorrect because developing and training a custom LLM from scratch is an extremely resource-intensive and costly endeavor, completely unsuited for a small agency with limited budget and resources. Option D is incorrect because RAG is primarily used for grounding LLM responses in factual, external knowledge, not for transferring a specific creative writing style from a small set of examples.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_20',
  'A logistics company aims to automate complex customer service interactions that involve multiple steps, such as tracking packages, changing delivery addresses, and initiating return requests. Each of these tasks requires the AI system to interact with various internal backend systems (e.g., order management, inventory, shipping APIs). The company needs a generative AI solution that can intelligently orchestrate these multi-step processes and make calls to external tools to fulfill customer requests. Which generative AI capability is essential for enabling such an automated workflow?',
  'mcq',
  '["Using a simple LLM for direct conversation without any external integrations.","Fine-tuning an LLM on all possible customer service scenarios and API documentation.","Designing an agentic AI workflow using an LLM with tool-use capabilities to interact with backend APIs.","Increasing the `max_tokens` parameter to allow the LLM to generate very long, detailed responses."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because agentic AI workflows, which involve an LLM acting as an agent, are designed to perform multi-step tasks by leveraging ''tool use.'' This allows the LLM to call external APIs or functions (tools) to interact with backend systems, retrieve information, or perform actions necessary to fulfill complex requests like tracking packages or updating addresses. Option A is incorrect because a simple LLM without external integrations cannot interact with backend systems to perform actions or retrieve real-time data, thus failing to automate multi-step tasks. Option B is incorrect because fine-tuning an LLM on all possible scenarios and API documentation would be an impractical, costly, and high-maintenance approach, especially as backend systems and policies evolve. It doesn''t provide the dynamic interaction capability of an agent. Option D is incorrect because increasing `max_tokens` only affects the length of the LLM''s output and does not enable it to interact with external systems or orchestrate multi-step workflows.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_21',
  'A media monitoring company needs to provide its clients with real-time summaries and sentiment analysis of news articles published within the last 24 hours. They plan to use a large language model (LLM) for this task. However, they are concerned that standard foundation models have a knowledge cutoff and might not be aware of very recent events, leading to incomplete or inaccurate analysis. The company requires a solution that ensures the LLM always processes and analyzes the most current information available. Which approach is MOST effective for addressing the LLM''s knowledge cutoff limitation in this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Rely solely on a base foundation model, assuming its training data is continuously updated with the latest news.","Fine-tune the foundation model daily with all new news articles published in the last 24 hours.","Adjust the `top-p` parameter to make the LLM''s output more diverse and comprehensive.","Implement Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) by integrating the LLM with a regularly updated knowledge base of recent news articles."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct because Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) is the most effective approach to overcome an LLM''s knowledge cutoff and provide up-to-date information. By integrating the LLM with a regularly updated knowledge base of recent news articles, the model can retrieve and leverage the latest information to generate accurate summaries and sentiment analysis. Option A is incorrect because base foundation models have a knowledge cutoff, meaning their training data is not continuously updated with real-time events. Relying solely on them would result in outdated or incomplete analysis. Option B is incorrect because fine-tuning a foundation model daily with new data is an extremely costly, time-consuming, and complex process, making it impractical for real-time updates. Option C is incorrect because adjusting the `top-p` parameter influences the diversity of the LLM''s output but does not provide it with access to new, external knowledge beyond its training data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_22',
  'A large enterprise wants to deploy an internal chatbot for employees to quickly find answers from their vast internal knowledge base, which includes confidential policies and technical manuals. The primary concern is ensuring the chatbot provides accurate, up-to-date, and non-hallucinated information strictly from these proprietary sources, while also being cost-effective compared to extensive model retraining.',
  'mcq',
  '["Fine-tune a foundation model with the entire internal knowledge base, then deploy it.","Implement Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) by connecting the foundation model to an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base containing the internal documentation.","Use a base foundation model directly from Amazon Bedrock and rely solely on its pre-trained knowledge for all employee queries.","Configure the foundation model with a very high temperature setting to encourage creative and diverse responses to employee questions."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the most appropriate solution. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases allows the foundation model to retrieve relevant information from the enterprise''s proprietary documentation and use it to ground its responses. This ensures accuracy, minimizes hallucinations, provides up-to-date information, and is more cost-effective and agile than fine-tuning for dynamic knowledge bases.
Option A is incorrect because fine-tuning a foundation model with a large, constantly updating internal knowledge base is expensive, time-consuming, and requires re-fine-tuning for updates. While it can incorporate proprietary knowledge, RAG is generally more cost-effective and efficient for dynamic information retrieval, aligning with anti-pattern [ap-2.2-001].
Option C is incorrect because a base foundation model has a knowledge cutoff and no access to proprietary company data. Relying solely on its pre-trained knowledge would lead to hallucinations and incorrect answers for internal queries, directly violating the accuracy and grounding requirements. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.2-002].
Option D is incorrect because configuring a very high temperature setting encourages more creative, diverse, and potentially less factual or hallucinated responses, which is directly contrary to the requirement for accurate and non-hallucinated information.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_23',
  'A manufacturing company wants to build an internal chatbot that can answer employee questions about company policies, HR benefits, and operational procedures. These documents are frequently updated and contain proprietary information not found in public LLM training data. The company needs a solution that can provide accurate answers based on these internal documents, with the MOST cost-effective approach and the fastest time to implementation.',
  'mcq',
  '["Fine-tune a foundation model on the entire corpus of company policies and HR documents, which is expensive, time-consuming, and not ideal for frequently updated information.","Use a base foundation model with carefully crafted prompts that include snippets of relevant policy text directly in the prompt for each query.","Implement a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system using Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases, connecting it to the company''s document repositories.","Develop a custom machine learning model from scratch to classify employee questions and retrieve answers from a traditional database."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) using Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases is the most cost-effective and fastest way to ground an LLM with proprietary, frequently updated data. It allows the LLM to retrieve relevant information from internal document repositories in real-time before generating a response, without needing to retrain the model.
Option A is incorrect. Fine-tuning a foundation model is expensive, time-consuming, and requires curated training data. It''s also not ideal for frequently updated information, as each update would necessitate re-fine-tuning, aligning with anti-pattern [ap-2.2-001].
Option B is incorrect. While prompt engineering can be effective for small amounts of context, including entire policy texts directly in prompts for every query is impractical for large, frequently updated document corpora due to context window limits and prompt engineering complexity.
Option D is incorrect. Developing a custom machine learning model from scratch is a high-cost, high-effort approach that does not leverage the generative capabilities of LLMs and would be significantly slower to implement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_24',
  'An e-commerce company wants to deploy a generative AI chatbot on its website to assist customers with product inquiries, order status, and return policies. The company frequently updates its product catalog and policies. The chatbot must provide accurate, real-time information that reflects the latest company data, which was not part of the foundation model''s original training. The primary goal is to ensure the chatbot''s responses are always current and specific to the company''s offerings.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use a base foundation model directly from Amazon Bedrock, assuming its vast general knowledge includes all recent product updates.","Implement a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system that retrieves information from the company''s product database and policy documents before generating a response.","Periodically fine-tune the base foundation model with the latest product catalog and policy documents every time there''s an update, which is an expensive and time-consuming process.","Configure the LLM to have a very high `temperature` setting, believing it will enable the model to \"guess\" the latest product information more accurately."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. A Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system is the most effective way to ensure a chatbot provides up-to-date and company-specific information. By retrieving relevant data from the company''s live product database and policy documents, the LLM can generate responses grounded in the latest information, overcoming its knowledge cutoff.
Option A is incorrect. Base foundation models have a knowledge cutoff based on their training data and do not have access to real-time, proprietary company data. Relying on a base model alone would lead to generic, outdated, or incorrect responses. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.2-002].
Option C is incorrect. Periodically fine-tuning the model for every product or policy update is an expensive, time-consuming, and operationally intensive approach that is not practical for frequently changing data.
Option D is incorrect. The `temperature` setting influences the creativity and randomness of an LLM''s output. A high `temperature` would make the responses less factual and more prone to hallucinations, not more accurate regarding specific, real-time product information.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_25',
  'A travel agency wants to build an AI assistant that can not only answer customer questions about travel destinations but also perform actions like checking flight availability, booking hotels, and sending confirmation emails. The assistant needs to interact with external APIs for flight booking systems and the company''s CRM for email. The objective is to automate these complex, multi-step workflows efficiently and reliably.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use a standard LLM via Amazon Bedrock to generate text responses, requiring the customer to manually perform booking actions.","Fine-tune a foundation model on a dataset of travel booking conversations to improve its ability to suggest booking actions, but this does not enable direct interaction with external systems.","Deploy a simple chatbot using Amazon Lex and integrate it with AWS Lambda functions for basic conversational flow, without advanced AI reasoning.","Implement an Amazon Bedrock Agent, configuring it with tools that interact with the external flight booking and CRM APIs to automate multi-step workflows."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct. Amazon Bedrock Agents are designed for building agentic AI workflows that can perform complex, multi-step tasks by interacting with external systems. By configuring the agent with ''tools'' (e.g., AWS Lambda functions that call external APIs for flight booking or CRM), it can autonomously check availability, make bookings, and send emails, automating the entire process.
Option A is incorrect. A standard LLM can generate text responses but cannot directly perform actions or interact with external APIs. It would only provide information, requiring the user to take manual steps.
Option B is incorrect. Fine-tuning improves the model''s language generation capabilities for a specific domain or style. It does not equip the model with the ability to call external APIs or execute actions in a structured, agentic manner.
Option C is incorrect. Amazon Lex is suitable for building conversational interfaces with predefined intents and slots. While it can integrate with AWS Lambda for backend logic, it''s primarily for basic conversational flows and lacks the advanced reasoning and autonomous tool-use capabilities of a generative AI agent required for complex, multi-step workflows.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

