INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_27',
  'A rapidly growing e-commerce company wants to integrate generative AI into its customer service operations to automate responses to common inquiries. The development team has limited experience with machine learning infrastructure and needs to quickly deploy a large language model (LLM) without incurring significant operational overhead for managing servers, scaling, or model updates. The primary goal is to minimize the ongoing effort required to maintain the AI solution while ensuring access to a performant foundation model.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy a pre-trained LLM from Amazon SageMaker JumpStart onto a dedicated Amazon SageMaker endpoint and manage its scaling.","Utilize Amazon Bedrock to access a managed foundation model via an API for generating customer service responses.","Provision Amazon EC2 instances, install open-source LLM frameworks, and self-host a foundation model.","Develop a custom LLM from scratch using AWS Trainium instances and deploy it on Amazon SageMaker."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because Amazon Bedrock provides a serverless, managed service for accessing leading foundation models via an API. This approach significantly reduces operational overhead as AWS handles the underlying infrastructure, scaling, and model updates, aligning perfectly with the company''s goal of minimizing maintenance effort.
Option A is incorrect because while Amazon SageMaker JumpStart offers pre-trained models, deploying them to a SageMaker endpoint still requires managing the endpoint''s scaling and infrastructure, which incurs more operational overhead compared to Amazon Bedrock''s fully managed API access.
Option C is incorrect as provisioning Amazon EC2 instances and self-hosting an LLM involves substantial operational overhead for setup, maintenance, security, and scaling, directly contradicting the requirement for least operational overhead. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.3-001] "Self-hosting and managing LLMs instead of using Amazon Bedrock."
Option D is incorrect because developing a custom LLM from scratch is a highly complex, time-consuming, and resource-intensive task, far exceeding the requirement for quickly deploying a solution with minimal operational overhead. AWS Trainium is for training, not for simply using a pre-trained model with minimal overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_28',
  'A rapidly growing e-commerce startup is developing a new AI-powered chatbot to assist customers with product inquiries and order status updates. The development team needs to integrate a large language model (LLM) quickly into their application. Their primary goal is to minimize the operational overhead associated with managing the underlying infrastructure for the LLM, allowing their small team to focus on core application development. They also want the flexibility to easily switch between different leading foundation models as needed. Which AWS approach should the startup choose to meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy an open-source LLM on Amazon EC2 instances, managing the model lifecycle and scaling manually.","Utilize Amazon Bedrock to access managed foundation models via API, integrating directly into their application.","Use Amazon SageMaker JumpStart to deploy a pre-trained LLM onto a SageMaker endpoint, managing the endpoint''s scaling and updates.","Implement Amazon Q Business to provide conversational AI for customer support, leveraging its pre-built integrations."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Amazon Bedrock provides serverless access to a variety of leading foundation models via API, significantly minimizing operational overhead as AWS manages all underlying infrastructure, scaling, and model updates. This allows the startup''s small team to focus entirely on application development and easily switch between models.
Option A is incorrect. Self-hosting an open-source LLM on Amazon EC2 instances requires significant operational effort for deployment, scaling, security, and maintenance, directly contradicting the goal of least operational overhead. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.3-001].
Option C is incorrect. While Amazon SageMaker JumpStart simplifies model deployment, it still requires managing SageMaker endpoints, including scaling configurations and updates, which introduces more operational overhead compared to the fully managed, serverless experience of Amazon Bedrock.
Option D is incorrect. Amazon Q Business is designed for business productivity and developer assistance, providing a complete conversational AI solution. While powerful, it''s not the most direct solution for integrating a raw LLM into a custom-built customer support chatbot where the primary constraint is minimal operational overhead for the LLM itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_29',
  'A global manufacturing company wants to build an internal knowledge base system that allows engineers to ask natural language questions about complex machinery manuals, troubleshooting guides, and design specifications. The system must provide highly accurate and contextually relevant answers by retrieving information from a vast repository of proprietary technical documents. To achieve this, the company plans to convert these documents into vector embeddings for semantic search. Which AWS service is the MOST appropriate for storing and efficiently querying these vector embeddings to enable Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon DynamoDB, storing vector embeddings as attributes within items for quick key-value lookups.","Amazon S3, storing vector embeddings as individual files in a structured folder hierarchy for easy access.","Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) with a standard relational database schema.","Amazon OpenSearch Service, leveraging its k-NN (k-Nearest Neighbor) capabilities for vector search."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct. Amazon OpenSearch Service, particularly with its k-NN plugin, is specifically designed to store and efficiently perform semantic similarity searches on vector embeddings. This capability is crucial for Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) systems to retrieve contextually relevant information.
Option A is incorrect. Amazon DynamoDB is a key-value and document database optimized for high-performance, single-item lookups. It does not natively support efficient vector similarity search, which is essential for RAG. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.3-002].
Option B is incorrect. Amazon S3 is object storage and is not designed for efficient real-time querying of vector embeddings for semantic similarity. While embeddings could be stored there, retrieving and searching them would be highly inefficient without an additional dedicated search layer.
Option C is incorrect. Amazon RDS, a relational database service, is not optimized for vector similarity search. While some extensions like pgvector for Amazon Aurora exist, a standard relational database schema would not provide the necessary performance for efficient semantic search across a large number of embeddings.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_3_30',
  'A rapidly growing e-commerce startup wants to integrate a generative AI model into their customer support system to automate responses for frequently asked questions and provide product recommendations. The startup has a small development team with limited machine learning expertise and needs a solution that minimizes operational overhead for model deployment and management. They require access to leading foundation models without managing underlying infrastructure.',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize Amazon Bedrock to access managed foundation models directly through an API.","Deploy a pre-trained foundation model from Amazon SageMaker JumpStart onto Amazon SageMaker endpoints.","Self-host an open-source large language model on Amazon EC2 instances and manage its lifecycle.","Develop a custom large language model from scratch using Amazon SageMaker and deploy it to a SageMaker endpoint."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Amazon Bedrock provides serverless access to a variety of leading foundation models through a simple API, significantly minimizing operational overhead as it handles all infrastructure management. This aligns perfectly with the startup''s need for limited ML expertise and reduced management burden. Option B is incorrect. While Amazon SageMaker JumpStart offers pre-trained models, deploying them to SageMaker endpoints still requires managing the endpoint, scaling, and updates, which introduces more operational overhead compared to Amazon Bedrock''s fully managed service. Option C is incorrect. Self-hosting an open-source LLM on Amazon EC2 instances (ap-2.3-001) would require significant effort for setup, scaling, security, and ongoing management, directly contradicting the requirement for minimal operational overhead and limited ML expertise. Option D is incorrect. Developing a custom LLM from scratch is a highly complex, time-consuming, and resource-intensive endeavor that demands deep ML expertise and significant operational effort, making it unsuitable for a startup with limited ML resources and a need for minimal overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_6_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_24',
  'A financial analysis firm is developing an AI assistant to help analysts process complex regulatory documents. The assistant needs to perform multi-step reasoning, such as identifying specific clauses, cross-referencing definitions, and then summarizing the implications. Initial tests with a foundation model show that simple prompts often lead to incomplete or logically flawed summaries when the task requires several sequential analytical steps. The team wants to improve the model''s ability to follow a logical progression to produce more accurate and comprehensive outputs.',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the model''s temperature parameter to encourage more diverse and creative responses.","Provide a single example of a correct input-output pair in the prompt to guide the model''s response.","Restrict the model''s output by setting a very low `top-p` value to focus on the most probable tokens.","Structure the prompt to include intermediate reasoning steps, guiding the model through a sequence of thoughts before providing the final answer."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct because structuring the prompt with intermediate reasoning steps describes Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting. CoT is a technique that enables LLMs to decompose complex problems into manageable steps, significantly improving their ability to perform multi-step reasoning and achieve more accurate results for tasks requiring logical progression, such as analyzing regulatory documents.
Option A is incorrect because increasing the temperature parameter makes the model''s output more random and creative, which is counterproductive when accuracy and logical reasoning are paramount. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.1-001] "Setting temperature to 0 for all generative AI use cases" (or in this case, setting it high when it should be low for accuracy).
Option B describes few-shot prompting, which provides examples but might not be sufficient for complex multi-step reasoning where the model needs to explicitly show its thought process. While helpful, it''s less effective than CoT for complex reasoning.
Option C is incorrect because setting a very low `top-p` value makes the model''s output more deterministic and focused on highly probable tokens, which can limit its ability to explore necessary reasoning paths or generate comprehensive summaries, especially for complex tasks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_25',
  'A healthcare startup is developing an AI assistant to help medical professionals quickly access information from clinical research papers. The assistant needs to summarize findings and answer specific questions. Given the critical nature of medical information, the startup is highly concerned about the model generating plausible-sounding but factually incorrect information, known as ''hallucinations,'' which could lead to serious consequences. The team requires a strategy to ensure the assistant''s responses are accurate and reliable. Which approach should the startup prioritize to mitigate the risk of hallucinations for factual or high-stakes tasks?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system to ground responses in verified clinical research papers and incorporate human review for critical outputs.","Increase the model''s temperature parameter to encourage more diverse and creative responses, hoping it covers all possible factual scenarios.","Fine-tune the foundation model on a larger, more diverse public dataset of general medical knowledge to improve its overall understanding.","Set the `max_tokens` parameter to a very high value to ensure the model provides exhaustive responses, reducing the chance of missing information."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. Implementing a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system allows the LLM to retrieve information from a trusted, verified knowledge base (clinical research papers) before generating a response, significantly reducing hallucinations by grounding the output in factual data. Combining this with human review for high-stakes tasks is a robust mitigation strategy, directly addressing anti-pattern [ap-2.1-002].
Option B is incorrect. Increasing the `temperature` parameter makes the model''s output more random and creative, which would likely increase the risk of hallucinations and factual inaccuracies, making it unsuitable for high-stakes medical information.
Option C is incorrect. While fine-tuning on a larger medical dataset can improve a model''s general knowledge, it doesn''t guarantee factual accuracy for specific, up-to-date information, nor does it prevent hallucinations, especially if the fine-tuning data itself isn''t perfectly curated or if the model still extrapolates beyond its training. RAG provides a more direct grounding mechanism.
Option D is incorrect. Setting a high `max_tokens` parameter only controls the length of the generated response. It does not influence the factual accuracy or reduce the likelihood of hallucinations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_26',
  'A content creation agency uses a large language model (LLM) to generate summaries of long articles, often exceeding 5,000 words, for their clients. The team has observed that the generated summaries frequently omit key details or critical arguments, especially those appearing in the middle or towards the end of the original articles. They suspect the LLM is not processing the entire input document effectively. Which strategy should the agency adopt to ensure the LLM processes all relevant information from these lengthy documents and produces comprehensive summaries?',
  'mcq',
  '["Adjust the model''s `top-k` parameter to a higher value, allowing the model to consider more token options during generation.","Increase the model''s `temperature` parameter to encourage more creative and expansive summaries that might inadvertently cover more details.","Implement a document chunking strategy to break down long articles into smaller, manageable segments, processing each segment sequentially.","Set the `top-p` parameter to a lower value, making the model''s output more focused and deterministic, thereby reducing irrelevant information."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct. LLMs have finite context windows, meaning they can only process a limited number of tokens at a time. When documents exceed this limit, parts of the input are truncated or ignored, leading to incomplete responses. Implementing a chunking strategy to break down long documents into smaller segments that fit within the context window, processing each segment, and then combining or summarizing the segment outputs, ensures all relevant information is considered. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-2.1-003].
Option A is incorrect. Adjusting the `top-k` parameter influences the diversity and quality of token selection during generation but does not address the fundamental limitation of the context window for input processing.
Option B is incorrect. Increasing the `temperature` parameter makes the model''s output more random and creative. This might lead to more varied summaries but will not help the model process information that falls outside its context window.
Option D is incorrect. Setting the `top-p` parameter to a lower value makes the output more focused and deterministic, but like `top-k` and `temperature`, it affects the output generation process, not the input processing capacity of the context window.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_1_27',
  'A content creation agency is leveraging a large language model (LLM) to generate detailed articles based on extensive research documents provided by their clients. The agency has observed that when processing particularly long documents, the generated articles sometimes omit crucial facts or sections, even though the source documents contain all the necessary information. The LLM consistently produces coherent but incomplete summaries for these lengthy inputs.',
  'mcq',
  '["The LLM is experiencing hallucinations and fabricating information, leading to omissions.","The input document content is exceeding the LLM''s defined context window, causing truncation.","The temperature inference parameter is set too low, making the output overly deterministic.","The prompt engineering technique used is not sufficiently complex or detailed enough to guide the LLM through very extensive documents."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Large language models have a finite context window (ap-2.1-003), which defines the maximum number of tokens they can process in a single input. When the input document content exceeds this limit, the LLM will truncate or ignore the excess information, leading to incomplete responses or omissions of crucial facts, as described in the scenario. Option A is incorrect. Hallucinations involve generating plausible but incorrect information, not typically omitting entire sections of provided input due to length constraints. The scenario describes missing information, not fabricated information. Option C is incorrect. The temperature parameter controls the randomness and creativity of the LLM''s output. A low temperature makes the output more deterministic and focused, but it does not cause the model to ignore parts of the input due to exceeding context limits. Option D is incorrect. While prompt engineering is vital, the issue described specifically relates to the length of the input causing omissions, which points to a context window limitation rather than a prompt quality issue. A better prompt would not overcome the hard limit of the context window.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_6_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_26',
  'A healthcare provider is developing an internal knowledge base application powered by generative AI to assist medical staff with patient care guidelines and drug interaction information. The primary concern is that foundation models have a knowledge cutoff and might "hallucinate" incorrect medical advice, which could have severe consequences. The solution must ensure that all generated responses are strictly based on the organization''s verified, up-to-date medical documents and clinical protocols, minimizing the risk of inaccurate information.',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) by connecting Amazon Bedrock to an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base containing the organization''s medical documents.","Fine-tune a general-purpose foundation model on the organization''s entire medical document repository to embed the knowledge directly into the model weights.","Increase the context window of the foundation model to load the entire medical document into the prompt for every query.","Use a base foundation model directly from Amazon Bedrock and rely on prompt engineering to instruct it to only use factual information."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases is the most effective approach to ground LLM responses in specific, proprietary data. This method retrieves relevant information from the organization''s verified medical documents and provides it to the LLM as context, significantly reducing hallucinations and ensuring responses are accurate and up-to-date, addressing the critical concern for a healthcare provider.
Option B is incorrect because fine-tuning a foundation model for this use case is expensive, time-consuming, and requires extensive data preparation. More importantly, fine-tuning updates model weights and doesn''t guarantee that the model will *only* use the fine-tuned data for every response, nor does it easily handle rapidly changing information. RAG is generally preferred for grounding models with external, frequently updated knowledge. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.2-001] "Fine-tuning a foundation model when prompt engineering or RAG would suffice."
Option C is incorrect because even if a model had an extremely large context window to fit an entire medical document repository, it would be highly inefficient, expensive, and still not guarantee that the model would strictly adhere to only that information or avoid hallucinations. LLMs have finite context windows, and exceeding them leads to truncation (anti-pattern [ap-2.1-003]).
Option D is incorrect because using a base foundation model without grounding it in company-specific knowledge will inevitably lead to responses based on its general training data, which has a knowledge cutoff and is prone to hallucinations when asked about proprietary or very specific domain information. Relying solely on prompt engineering to prevent hallucinations for factual, high-stakes tasks is insufficient. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.2-002] "Using a base model for enterprise chat without grounding it in company-specific knowledge."',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_27',
  'A small marketing firm wants to automate the creation of short, engaging social media captions for new product launches. These captions need to incorporate specific product features, maintain the company''s playful brand voice, and be generated quickly without significant upfront investment in data collection or specialized model training. The firm has a few good examples of past successful captions. They need to achieve high-quality, on-brand content generation with the LEAST amount of effort and cost. Which generative AI strategy is most appropriate for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Fine-tune a foundation model on a large dataset of all past social media posts and product descriptions to teach it the brand voice and product details.","Utilize prompt engineering techniques with a well-crafted system prompt and few-shot examples to guide a foundation model''s output.","Develop a comprehensive Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system to retrieve product details from a database and then generate captions.","Build a custom generative AI model from scratch, training it on a proprietary dataset of marketing materials and social media content."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. For generating short, on-brand content with specific features, prompt engineering, especially with a well-designed system prompt and few-shot examples (providing the model with a few examples of desired output), is often the most cost-effective and least effort-intensive approach. It leverages the existing capabilities of a foundation model without requiring extensive data collection or model training.
Option A is incorrect. Fine-tuning a foundation model is expensive and time-consuming, requiring curated training data and significant compute resources. For a task like generating social media captions with a small set of examples, prompt engineering is usually sufficient and much more efficient. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.2-001].
Option C is incorrect. While RAG is powerful for grounding responses in large knowledge bases, it''s an overkill for generating short social media captions where the necessary context (product features, brand voice) can often be effectively conveyed through prompt engineering and few-shot examples. It introduces unnecessary complexity and cost.
Option D is incorrect. Building a custom generative AI model from scratch is an extremely high-effort, high-cost, and long-term endeavor that is completely disproportionate to the requirement of generating social media captions. It''s reserved for highly specialized use cases where no existing foundation model or fine-tuning can meet the needs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_28',
  'A large enterprise wants to deploy an internal chatbot to help employees quickly find answers to questions about company policies, HR benefits, and IT support procedures. The chatbot must provide accurate and up-to-date information that is specific to the company''s proprietary knowledge base, which frequently changes. Relying solely on a base model''s general knowledge is not acceptable, as it would lack internal context. The primary requirement is to ensure responses are consistently accurate and grounded in the company''s specific, evolving internal knowledge. Which architectural approach should the enterprise choose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually pre-program all possible questions and answers into the chatbot''s logic to ensure 100% accuracy.","Fine-tune a foundation model on the entire collection of company policies and internal documents.","Use a base foundation model directly via Amazon Bedrock, providing only a simple prompt for each query.","Implement a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system using Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases to retrieve relevant information from internal documents before generating a response."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is correct. Implementing a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases is the most effective approach. RAG allows the LLM to retrieve relevant, up-to-date information from the company''s proprietary documents and use it to ground its responses, ensuring accuracy and relevance to internal knowledge. This is particularly beneficial for frequently changing information, as the knowledge base can be updated independently of the model.
Option A is incorrect. Manually pre-programming all possible questions and answers is not scalable for a large enterprise with an evolving knowledge base. It would be extremely high maintenance and defeat the purpose of using generative AI.
Option B is incorrect. Fine-tuning a foundation model on all company policies is expensive, time-consuming, and challenging to keep up-to-date with frequently changing information. RAG offers a more agile and cost-effective solution for dynamic knowledge bases compared to repeated fine-tuning.
Option C is incorrect. Using a base foundation model directly via Amazon Bedrock without grounding it in company-specific knowledge will result in generic, potentially inaccurate, or hallucinated responses, as the model''s training data has a knowledge cutoff and no access to proprietary information. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.2-002].',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_6_m_6_2_29',
  'A large enterprise wants to build an internal AI assistant for its employees to quickly access information from thousands of proprietary internal documents, including HR policies, technical manuals, and recent project reports. The information in these documents is frequently updated, and the assistant must provide accurate, up-to-date, and contextually relevant answers, avoiding any generic responses or outdated facts. The solution needs to be scalable and maintainable with minimal effort to keep the knowledge current.',
  'mcq',
  '["Fine-tune a pre-trained foundation model on the entire corpus of internal documents to incorporate proprietary knowledge.","Use a base foundation model directly through Amazon Bedrock, relying solely on advanced prompt engineering techniques.","Implement a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system using Amazon Bedrock with a Knowledge Base.","Deploy a pre-trained model from Amazon SageMaker JumpStart and rely on its general knowledge for employee queries."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct. Implementing a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system using Amazon Bedrock with a Knowledge Base is the most effective solution. RAG allows the LLM to retrieve relevant, up-to-date information from the company''s proprietary documents and use it to ground its responses, ensuring accuracy and contextual relevance without requiring expensive and time-consuming fine-tuning (ap-2.2-001). Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases automate the ingestion, chunking, embedding, and retrieval process, making it scalable and maintainable. Option A is incorrect. Fine-tuning a foundation model (ap-2.2-001) is expensive, time-consuming, and requires significant data preparation. More importantly, it''s not ideal for frequently updated information, as the model would need to be re-fine-tuned regularly, which is impractical and costly. Option B is incorrect. Using a base foundation model directly (ap-2.2-002) would mean the model only has knowledge up to its training data cutoff and no access to the company''s proprietary, internal documents. Even with advanced prompt engineering, it cannot provide accurate, contextually relevant answers from the internal knowledge base. Option D is incorrect. Deploying a pre-trained model from Amazon SageMaker JumpStart (similar to ap-2.2-002) would provide a model with general knowledge, but it would not have access to the enterprise''s specific, proprietary, and frequently updated internal documents, leading to generic or incorrect responses.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain6_1',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_6_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

