INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_7',
  'A startup is developing an AI-powered content generation tool using Amazon Bedrock. They need to ensure that the generated content is not only grammatically correct and coherent but also aligns with the company''s brand voice and avoids generating toxic or biased language. Before deploying the model to production, the team needs a robust method to assess these qualitative aspects of the model''s output, beyond simple automated metrics. They want to identify potential issues that automated metrics might miss.',
  'mcq',
  '["Conduct human evaluation of the generated content, involving expert reviewers to assess brand alignment, tone, toxicity, and bias.","Rely solely on automated metrics like BLEU and ROUGE scores to measure the n-gram overlap with a set of reference texts.","Deploy the model directly to a small user group and gather implicit feedback through user engagement metrics.","Use Amazon SageMaker Clarify to detect bias in the training data, assuming this will prevent all forms of biased output from the generative model."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. Human evaluation is crucial for assessing qualitative aspects of generative model outputs such as brand alignment, tone, toxicity, and bias, which automated metrics often fail to capture. Human reviewers can provide nuanced feedback on relevance, coherence, and safety, ensuring the content meets specific business and ethical standards before deployment. Option B is incorrect because BLEU and ROUGE scores primarily measure lexical overlap and do not effectively assess semantic quality, coherence, factual accuracy, or the presence of toxicity/bias in generative models (anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002]). Option C is incorrect because deploying directly to users without prior qualitative evaluation risks exposing users to harmful or off-brand content, which could damage reputation and user trust. This approach also represents anti-pattern [ap-3.4-001] by not having proper content moderation guardrails in place during evaluation. Option D is incorrect because while Amazon SageMaker Clarify can help detect bias in training data, it does not guarantee the absence of all biased or toxic outputs from a generative model, nor does it assess brand voice or coherence. Generative models can still exhibit biases or generate undesirable content even with clean training data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_8',
  'A content moderation team is building an application using Amazon Bedrock to automatically review user-generated comments for harmful or inappropriate content. They need a robust solution to filter out hate speech, profanity, and personally identifiable information (PII) before content is displayed to other users. The team wants to implement a proactive defense mechanism that is easy to configure and manage, without requiring custom model training for content filtering.',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to define content policies, filter unwanted categories, and redact PII from model outputs.","Develop a custom content moderation model using Amazon SageMaker and integrate it with the Bedrock application.","Rely on prompt engineering techniques to instruct the foundation model to avoid generating harmful content.","Use automated metrics like ROUGE scores to evaluate the ''safety'' of the generated content against a baseline of safe text."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. Amazon Bedrock Guardrails are specifically designed to implement content policies, filter unwanted categories (like hate speech or profanity), and redact PII from foundation model inputs and outputs. They provide a configurable and managed solution for content moderation without requiring custom model training, directly addressing the team''s requirements for a proactive, easy-to-manage defense mechanism. Option B is incorrect because developing a custom content moderation model on Amazon SageMaker would involve significant development effort, data labeling, training, and deployment, which is more complex and time-consuming than using Bedrock Guardrails for this specific purpose. Option C is incorrect because while prompt engineering can guide a model, it is not a robust or foolproof method for preventing all harmful content, especially against adversarial inputs or model drift. It is insufficient as a primary defense against content moderation issues (anti-pattern [ap-3.4-001]). Option D is incorrect because ROUGE scores measure n-gram overlap and are irrelevant for evaluating content safety, toxicity, or PII presence. They are used for summarization quality, not content moderation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_9',
  'A healthcare provider is deploying an internal chatbot for medical staff to quickly retrieve information from clinical guidelines. The primary concern is to prevent the chatbot from generating any incorrect or misleading medical advice, which could have serious consequences. Before full deployment, the team needs a rigorous method to assess the factual accuracy and safety of the model''s responses, especially regarding medical facts and patient safety. They need to evaluate the model''s ''groundedness'' in the provided clinical documents.',
  'mcq',
  '["Perform human evaluation with medical experts to verify the factual accuracy, groundedness, and safety of the chatbot''s responses against clinical guidelines.","Rely solely on automated metrics like BERTScore to measure the semantic similarity of the chatbot''s responses to a set of pre-approved answers.","Conduct A/B testing by deploying two versions of the chatbot to staff and measuring user satisfaction scores.","Implement Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to filter out profanity and hate speech, assuming this will cover all safety concerns."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. For critical applications like medical advice, human evaluation by domain experts is indispensable. Medical professionals can verify the factual accuracy, ensure responses are ''grounded'' in clinical guidelines, and assess the safety implications of the generated content, which automated metrics cannot reliably do. This directly addresses the primary concern of preventing incorrect or misleading medical advice. Option B is incorrect because while BERTScore measures semantic similarity, it cannot reliably verify factual accuracy or groundedness in a specialized domain like medicine. It does not guarantee that the information is correct or safe. Option C is incorrect because A/B testing for user satisfaction does not directly assess the factual accuracy or safety of medical advice. It measures user experience, not the correctness of the information. Option D is incorrect because while Amazon Bedrock Guardrails are crucial for content filtering (e.g., profanity, hate speech), they do not inherently verify the factual accuracy of generated medical advice. Guardrails prevent harmful *types* of content, not factual errors in domain-specific information, representing anti-pattern [ap-3.4-001] if relied upon as the sole safety measure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_10',
  'A startup is developing a chatbot for customer support. They want to ensure that the chatbot''s responses are always polite, helpful, and never generate offensive or inappropriate language. Before deploying the chatbot to customers, the team needs to rigorously test its behavior to identify any instances of toxicity, bias, or unhelpful responses. They need an evaluation method that goes beyond simple keyword matching to assess the overall quality and safety of the conversational flow.',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation features, including human-in-the-loop evaluation, to assess dimensions like helpfulness, toxicity, and relevance.","Rely solely on automated metrics like BLEU scores to compare the chatbot''s responses against a predefined set of ''safe'' responses.","Deploy the chatbot to production with a small user group and monitor customer satisfaction scores as the primary evaluation metric.","Implement a system that simply checks for a blacklist of offensive words in the chatbot''s output."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation features are designed to help assess foundation model performance across various dimensions, including qualitative aspects like helpfulness, toxicity, and relevance. Integrating human-in-the-loop evaluation is particularly effective for assessing the nuanced quality and safety of conversational AI, ensuring that responses are polite, helpful, and free from offensive language before deployment. Option B is incorrect because BLEU scores measure n-gram overlap and are not suitable for evaluating complex qualitative aspects like politeness, helpfulness, toxicity, or overall conversational flow (anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002]). Option C is incorrect because deploying directly to production without thorough safety and quality evaluation risks exposing customers to inappropriate content, potentially damaging brand reputation and user trust. This represents anti-pattern [ap-3.4-001]. Option D is incorrect because a simple blacklist of offensive words is insufficient to catch all forms of inappropriate language, subtle biases, or unhelpful responses. LLMs can easily circumvent such basic filters.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_11',
  'A content marketing team uses an Amazon Bedrock foundation model to generate blog post ideas. They have noticed that the model sometimes produces ideas that are repetitive or too similar to existing content. The team wants to evaluate the diversity and originality of the generated ideas to ensure they are fresh and engaging. They need an evaluation method that can quantify these qualitative aspects effectively before publishing the ideas.',
  'mcq',
  '["Perform human evaluation by expert content strategists to assess the originality, diversity, and engagement potential of the generated blog post ideas.","Rely solely on automated metrics like BLEU scores to compare the generated ideas against a database of existing blog posts.","Deploy the model to production and measure website traffic and user engagement with the blog posts generated from the ideas.","Use Amazon SageMaker Clarify to detect potential biases in the generated ideas, assuming this will cover originality and diversity."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. For qualitative aspects like originality, diversity, and engagement potential of creative content, human evaluation by expert content strategists is the most effective method. Human reviewers can provide nuanced judgments that automated metrics cannot, ensuring the generated ideas are fresh and align with marketing goals. Option B is incorrect because BLEU scores measure n-gram overlap and are not suitable for evaluating originality or diversity of ideas. High BLEU scores might even indicate lack of originality if the ideas are too similar to existing content (anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002]). Option C is incorrect because deploying to production without prior evaluation risks publishing repetitive or unengaging content, potentially harming brand reputation and user experience. This is a post-deployment metric, not a pre-deployment evaluation of the ideas themselves. Option D is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Clarify focuses on bias detection, which is unrelated to assessing the originality or diversity of creative content ideas.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_12',
  'A social media company is developing an AI-powered content creation tool. They want to ensure that the generated content is engaging and relevant to current trends, but also strictly adheres to ethical guidelines and avoids generating misinformation or harmful narratives. Before deploying, the team needs to evaluate the model''s outputs for potential biases, toxicity, and its tendency to generate ungrounded claims. They need a comprehensive evaluation strategy.',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize a combination of human evaluation, automated metrics for relevance, and Amazon Bedrock Guardrails for content filtering and safety policies.","Rely solely on automated metrics like ROUGE and BLEU to measure the quality of the generated content against a set of reference texts.","Deploy the model in a shadow mode to a small percentage of users and monitor their engagement metrics as the primary evaluation.","Only use prompt engineering to instruct the model to be ethical and avoid misinformation, without any explicit evaluation of outputs."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. A comprehensive evaluation strategy for content generation should combine multiple approaches. Human evaluation is critical for assessing subjective qualities like engagement, relevance, and ethical adherence, as well as detecting subtle biases or harmful narratives. Automated metrics can provide quantitative insights into aspects like relevance, while Amazon Bedrock Guardrails are essential for proactive content filtering and enforcing safety policies against toxicity and misinformation. This multi-faceted approach ensures thorough assessment. Option B is incorrect because ROUGE and BLEU scores are primarily for summarization and translation quality (n-gram overlap) and do not effectively measure ethical adherence, bias, toxicity, or the generation of misinformation (anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002]). Option C is incorrect because shadow deployment primarily measures performance in a live environment, but it does not provide explicit, detailed feedback on specific issues like bias, toxicity, or misinformation before full deployment. It also represents anti-pattern [ap-3.4-001] if guardrails are not in place during shadow deployment. Option D is incorrect because relying solely on prompt engineering for ethical behavior is insufficient and unreliable. Models can still generate harmful content despite instructions, and explicit evaluation is necessary to verify adherence.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_13',
  'A company is developing a new AI-powered creative writing assistant. They want the assistant to generate engaging stories, but they are concerned about the model generating repetitive plots or unoriginal character arcs. Before releasing the assistant, the team needs to evaluate the creativity and originality of the generated stories. They need a method to assess these subjective qualities effectively.',
  'mcq',
  '["Conduct human evaluation with creative writers and literary experts to assess the originality, creativity, and narrative quality of the generated stories.","Rely solely on automated metrics like ROUGE and BLEU scores to compare the generated stories against a database of popular novels.","Deploy the creative writing assistant in a limited beta program and collect user ratings on story engagement.","Use Amazon SageMaker Clarify to detect if the generated stories contain any common literary tropes, assuming this measures originality."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. For evaluating highly subjective and qualitative aspects like creativity, originality, and narrative quality in creative writing, human evaluation by domain experts (creative writers and literary experts) is indispensable. These experts can provide nuanced feedback that automated metrics cannot, directly addressing the concern about repetitive plots or unoriginal character arcs. Option B is incorrect because ROUGE and BLEU scores measure n-gram overlap and are not suitable for assessing creativity, originality, or narrative quality in generative text. High scores might even indicate a lack of originality if the text is too similar to existing works (anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002]). Option C is incorrect because while user ratings provide valuable feedback, they are a post-deployment metric and do not offer the detailed, expert-level qualitative assessment needed to identify and address specific creative deficiencies before release. Option D is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Clarify is designed for bias detection, not for assessing literary originality or creativity. Detecting tropes does not equate to measuring originality.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_14',
  'A public sector agency is building an AI application to assist citizens with inquiries about government services. It is critical that the application provides unbiased and fair information, avoiding any discriminatory language or unfair treatment based on demographics. Before launching, the agency needs to rigorously evaluate the model''s outputs for potential biases and fairness issues, especially in its responses to different user demographics. They need a robust method to detect and mitigate these risks.',
  'mcq',
  '["Conduct human evaluation with diverse reviewers to assess the fairness and bias of responses across various demographic groups, alongside using Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation features for bias detection.","Rely solely on automated metrics like BLEU scores to ensure the responses are consistent with a set of ''fair'' reference answers.","Deploy the application to a pilot group of citizens and monitor their feedback on perceived fairness.","Implement Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to filter out profanity, assuming this will prevent all forms of bias and unfairness."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct. To rigorously evaluate for bias and fairness, a combination of human evaluation with diverse reviewers and specialized tools like Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation features (which can include bias detection capabilities) is essential. Human reviewers from various demographic groups can identify subtle biases or discriminatory language that automated tools might miss, ensuring the application provides unbiased and fair information. Option B is incorrect because BLEU scores measure n-gram overlap and are completely unsuitable for evaluating complex ethical dimensions like fairness or bias in responses (anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002]). Option C is incorrect because relying solely on pilot group feedback for fairness is reactive and may not capture all forms of bias, especially if the pilot group is not sufficiently diverse or if biases are subtle. This also represents anti-pattern [ap-3.4-001] if guardrails are not in place during evaluation. Option D is incorrect because Amazon Bedrock Guardrails are effective for filtering profanity and other harmful content, but they do not inherently detect or mitigate all forms of subtle bias or unfairness in the model''s reasoning or language generation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_7_m_7_4_15',
  'A media company is developing an AI-powered content summarization tool using Amazon Bedrock. This tool will process a wide range of articles, including user-generated content, before publishing. Before deploying to production, the company needs to ensure that the generated summaries are not only accurate and coherent but also strictly adhere to ethical guidelines, meaning they must be free from harmful, biased, or inappropriate content. The company requires a proactive mechanism to filter and moderate the outputs to prevent the generation of undesirable content.',
  'mcq',
  '["Rely solely on human evaluators to review every generated summary for harmful or biased content before publication.","Implement automated metrics like ROUGE and BLEU scores to assess the quality and safety of the generated summaries.","Integrate Amazon Bedrock Guardrails into the application to proactively detect and filter harmful content, PII, and specific denied topics.","Use Amazon SageMaker Clarify to analyze the model''s outputs for bias and explainability during the development phase."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the most effective solution for the media company''s requirement. Amazon Bedrock Guardrails are specifically designed to implement content moderation and safety policies for foundation model applications. They provide configurable content filters, PII redaction, and topic denial lists, allowing the company to proactively detect and prevent the generation of harmful, biased, or inappropriate content, ensuring adherence to ethical guidelines before publication.

Option A (human evaluators) is a reactive and labor-intensive approach that does not scale well for high volumes of content and does not provide a proactive filtering mechanism. Option B (automated metrics like ROUGE and BLEU) measure linguistic overlap and fluency but do not assess content safety, bias, or ethical adherence. Relying solely on these metrics is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-002) for evaluating generative model quality beyond basic linguistic similarity. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Clarify) is primarily used for detecting bias in training data and model predictions, and for model explainability, typically during the development and evaluation phases. While valuable for understanding model behavior, it is not designed as a real-time, proactive content moderation and filtering service for generative AI outputs in a production application like Amazon Bedrock Guardrails.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain7_1',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_7_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
