INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_36',
  'A financial services company processes millions of customer transactions daily, generating gigabytes of data. This data is critical for an anomaly detection machine learning model. The data scientists require a highly scalable and cost-effective solution to store this raw transaction data, which will be frequently queried for exploratory data analysis and used as input for AWS Glue ETL jobs. The solution must support various data formats and allow for schema evolution. Which AWS service is the MOST appropriate for storing this large volume of diverse ML data while meeting the requirements for scalability, cost-effectiveness, and flexibility?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Redshift, configured for high-performance analytical queries.","Amazon S3, organized into a data lake with a defined folder structure.","Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, leveraging its robust relational database features.","Amazon DynamoDB, utilizing its low-latency key-value store capabilities."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon S3 is highly scalable, cost-effective for storing vast amounts of raw data, and supports various data formats (CSV, JSON, Parquet). When organized as a data lake, it provides the flexibility needed for schema evolution and serves as an excellent source for AWS Glue ETL jobs. Option A, Amazon Redshift, is a data warehouse optimized for structured analytical queries, but it would be significantly more expensive for storing raw, diverse, and potentially unstructured data compared to Amazon S3. Option C, Amazon RDS, is a relational database better suited for structured operational data, not large-scale raw ML data lakes. Option D, Amazon DynamoDB, is a NoSQL key-value store designed for low-latency, high-throughput operational workloads, not cost-effective bulk storage and analytical querying of raw ML data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_37',
  'A media company is building a content recommendation system that relies on user interaction data. They collect clickstream data, viewing history, and ratings, which arrive continuously in high volumes. This streaming data needs to be ingested and made available for near real-time feature engineering and batch processing for model training. The company requires a fully managed, scalable service to capture and store this data stream reliably before it is processed further. Which AWS service is the MOST suitable for ingesting this high-volume, continuous stream of user interaction data?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3, with data uploaded periodically in large batches.","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, configured with appropriate shards for throughput.","Amazon Redshift, using its COPY command for bulk data loading.","Amazon DynamoDB, storing each interaction as a separate item."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is a fully managed, scalable service specifically designed for real-time ingestion of high-volume streaming data. It allows for continuous capture and storage of data, making it ideal for near real-time analytics and subsequent processing for ML. Option A, Amazon S3, is suitable for batch ingestion but not for continuous, real-time streaming. Option C, Amazon Redshift, is a data warehouse primarily for analytical queries, and while it can ingest data, it''s not optimized for continuous, high-volume streaming ingestion. Option D, Amazon DynamoDB, is a NoSQL database for operational workloads, and while it can handle high write throughput, it''s not primarily an ingestion service for analytical streaming data and can become costly for raw, high-volume stream storage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_38',
  'A large e-commerce company has accumulated petabytes of historical customer order data, product information, and website clickstream logs over several years. This data is stored in various formats, including CSV, JSON, and XML, within Amazon S3. The data science team needs to prepare this data for machine learning model training. To optimize query performance and reduce storage costs for analytical workloads, they want to convert this diverse data into a columnar format. Which data format should the team prioritize for storing their ML data to achieve the MOST cost-effective analytical querying and storage?',
  'mcq',
  '["CSV (Comma Separated Values) for its simplicity and wide compatibility.","JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) for its flexibility with semi-structured data.","Parquet (Apache Parquet) for its columnar storage and efficient compression.","TFRecord (TensorFlow Record) for its optimized binary format for TensorFlow."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the correct choice. Apache Parquet is a columnar storage format that provides significant advantages for analytical workloads, including high compression ratios and efficient query performance due to predicate pushdown. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.1-002] by moving away from row-based formats for analytical ML workloads. Option A, CSV, is a row-based format that is inefficient for analytical queries on large datasets. Option B, JSON, is also a row-based format and less efficient than columnar formats for analytical querying. Option D, TFRecord, is a binary format optimized for TensorFlow, but Parquet is generally preferred for raw data storage and analytical querying in a data lake context due to its broader ecosystem support and columnar benefits.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_39',
  'A machine learning engineer is preparing a large dataset, consisting of several terabytes of images and their corresponding labels, for an image classification model. The data is currently stored in Amazon S3. During model training with Amazon SageMaker, the engineer needs to ensure that the training job can efficiently access the data without downloading the entire dataset to the training instance before training begins, as this causes significant startup latency and increases training time. Which SageMaker data input mode should the engineer configure to stream data directly to the training instance during the training process, minimizing startup time for large datasets?',
  'mcq',
  '["File mode, which downloads the entire dataset to the instance.","Pipe mode, which streams data directly to the training algorithm.","FastFile mode, which uses optimized S3 access for faster downloads.","FullyManaged mode, which automatically handles data transfer."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Pipe mode in Amazon SageMaker streams data directly from Amazon S3 to the training algorithm, eliminating the need to download the entire dataset to the training instance beforehand. This significantly reduces startup latency and is ideal for large datasets, directly addressing anti-pattern [ap-1.1-001]. Option A, File mode, downloads the entire dataset before training, which is precisely the anti-pattern to avoid for large datasets. Option C, FastFile mode, is an optimized version of File mode that still downloads the data but uses a more efficient mechanism; however, it still involves a full download, unlike Pipe mode. Option D, FullyManaged mode, is not a standard SageMaker data input mode.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_40',
  'A research institution is training a large language model using a massive corpus of text data, totaling hundreds of terabytes. This data is stored in Amazon S3. During the training process with Amazon SageMaker, the institution wants to minimize the time spent on data transfer and maximize GPU utilization. They need an input mode that can stream data directly to the training instances at high throughput, avoiding the overhead of downloading the entire dataset before training commences. Which SageMaker data channel input mode is MOST suitable for this scenario to achieve optimal training efficiency for very large datasets?',
  'mcq',
  '["File mode, which is the default and downloads data to the instance.","Pipe mode, which streams data directly to the algorithm without local storage.","FastFile mode, which optimizes S3 downloads but still stages data locally.","EFS mode, which mounts an Amazon EFS file system to the instance."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Pipe mode in Amazon SageMaker streams data directly from Amazon S3 to the training algorithm, eliminating the need to download the entire dataset to the training instance beforehand. This significantly reduces startup latency and is ideal for very large datasets, directly addressing anti-pattern [ap-1.1-001]. Option A, File mode, downloads the entire dataset before training, which is inefficient for large datasets. Option C, FastFile mode, is an optimized version of File mode that still downloads the data but uses a more efficient mechanism; however, it still involves a full download, unlike Pipe mode. Option D, EFS mode, involves mounting a file system, which is different from direct streaming from S3 and might have different performance characteristics depending on the workload.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_41',
  'A data science team is developing a machine learning model that requires a large dataset of customer reviews. These reviews are currently stored as individual JSON files in an Amazon S3 bucket. The team needs to efficiently ingest and process this data for feature engineering using AWS Glue. To optimize the performance and cost of querying this data with services like Amazon Athena, they want to convert the JSON files into a more efficient, compressed, and columnar format. Which data format should the team choose for their processed ML data to achieve the MOST cost-effective analytical querying and storage?',
  'mcq',
  '["CSV, due to its human-readable nature and wide support.","JSON, maintaining the original format for simplicity.","Parquet, leveraging its columnar storage and compression benefits.","Avro, for its schema evolution capabilities and row-based storage."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the correct choice. Apache Parquet is a columnar storage format that provides significant advantages for analytical workloads, including high compression ratios and efficient query performance due to predicate pushdown. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.1-002] by recommending a columnar format over row-based ones like JSON or CSV for analytical ML workloads, leading to more cost-effective querying with services like Amazon Athena. Option A, CSV, is a row-based format and less efficient for analytical queries. Option B, JSON, is also row-based and less efficient for large-scale analytical querying. Option D, Avro, is a row-based format, which is not as performant or cost-effective as columnar formats for analytical queries.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_42',
  'A large-scale machine learning project involves training models on diverse datasets, including tabular data, images, and text. These datasets are stored in Amazon S3. The data science team needs a centralized repository to store and manage the metadata for these datasets, enabling data discovery and schema enforcement for downstream AWS Glue ETL jobs and Amazon Athena queries. They require a solution that integrates seamlessly with S3 and provides a unified view of their data lake. Which AWS service is MOST suitable for creating a data lake architecture with S3 and managing its metadata?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Redshift, used as the primary data storage and catalog.","Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, storing metadata in a relational database.","AWS Glue Data Catalog, integrated with Amazon S3 for metadata management.","Amazon DynamoDB, for storing key-value pairs of dataset metadata."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the correct choice. AWS Glue Data Catalog is a fully managed metadata repository that integrates seamlessly with Amazon S3 to form a data lake architecture. It allows users to store, discover, and manage metadata (schemas, table definitions, locations) for various data formats, enabling services like AWS Glue and Amazon Athena to query the data efficiently. Option A, Amazon Redshift, is a data warehouse and not primarily a metadata catalog for a data lake. Option B, Amazon RDS, is a relational database and would require significant custom development to serve as a data lake catalog. Option D, Amazon DynamoDB, is a NoSQL database and not designed for comprehensive data lake metadata management and discovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_43',
  'A manufacturing company collects high-volume, high-velocity sensor data from its production lines. This data needs to be continuously ingested into an AWS environment for real-time monitoring and eventual batch processing for machine learning model training. The company requires a fully managed service that can reliably capture and deliver this streaming data to various destinations, including Amazon S3 for long-term storage and AWS Lambda for real-time alerts. Which AWS service is the MOST appropriate for ingesting and delivering this continuous stream of sensor data?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3, with data uploaded via batch jobs every hour.","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, configured to deliver data to S3 and Lambda.","Amazon Redshift, using its streaming ingestion capabilities.","Amazon RDS for MySQL, storing sensor readings in a relational table."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is a fully managed service that reliably captures, transforms, and loads streaming data into data lakes, data stores, and analytics services. It can automatically deliver data to Amazon S3 and invoke AWS Lambda functions, making it ideal for the described scenario of high-volume, high-velocity data ingestion and delivery. Option A, Amazon S3, is a storage service, not an ingestion service for continuous streams. Option C, Amazon Redshift, is a data warehouse and while it has ingestion capabilities, Firehose is specifically designed for streaming data delivery. Option D, Amazon RDS, is a relational database and not suitable for high-volume, high-velocity streaming data ingestion for analytical purposes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_44',
  'A large enterprise is building a data lake on AWS to consolidate various data sources for machine learning. They have structured, semi-structured, and unstructured data arriving from different departments, all stored in Amazon S3. The data engineering team needs a service to automatically discover the schema of this diverse data, catalog it, and make it queryable by services like Amazon Athena and Amazon Redshift Spectrum. This will enable data scientists to easily find and access datasets without manual schema definition. Which AWS service should the team use to automatically discover and catalog metadata for their S3-based data lake?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Redshift, using its internal catalog for structured data.","AWS Glue Data Catalog, leveraging its crawlers for schema discovery.","Amazon RDS for MySQL, storing schema information in custom tables.","Amazon DynamoDB, for storing schema as key-value pairs."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. AWS Glue Data Catalog is a fully managed metadata repository that integrates seamlessly with Amazon S3. Its crawlers can automatically discover schemas from data stored in S3, cataloging tables and partitions, and making this metadata available for querying by services like Amazon Athena and Amazon Redshift Spectrum. This provides a unified view of the data lake and simplifies data discovery. Option A, Amazon Redshift, is a data warehouse and not designed for automatic schema discovery across diverse data lake sources. Option C, Amazon RDS, is a relational database and would require manual schema definition and management. Option D, Amazon DynamoDB, is a NoSQL database and not suitable for comprehensive data lake metadata management and discovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
