INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_36',
  'A retail company is building a recommendation engine and needs to prepare a large dataset of customer purchase history. The dataset contains several categorical features, such as ''product_category'' and ''customer_segment'', which need to be converted into a numerical format for machine learning models. The data engineering team wants to use a managed service that can perform these transformations efficiently and integrate seamlessly with other AWS ML services, while minimizing the operational burden of managing compute infrastructure. Which AWS service is best suited for performing these large-scale categorical feature encoding tasks with LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Develop custom Python scripts on an Amazon EC2 instance to process the data.","Utilize AWS Glue DataBrew to visually prepare and transform the dataset, including encoding categorical variables.","Write and execute Apache Spark jobs on an Amazon EMR cluster for data transformation.","Manually apply one-hot encoding using a Jupyter notebook running on a local machine."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct answer. AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that allows data scientists and analysts to clean, normalize, and transform data, including encoding categorical variables, without writing code. It is a fully managed service, significantly reducing operational overhead compared to managing EC2 instances or EMR clusters. Option A requires managing EC2 instances, increasing operational overhead. Option C, while powerful for large-scale data processing, involves managing an Amazon EMR cluster, which has higher operational overhead than DataBrew. Option D is not scalable for large datasets and is highly manual, making it impractical for production ML workflows.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_37',
  'A data science team is developing a machine learning model to predict customer churn. They have identified several features, including customer demographics, service usage, and historical interactions. Before training, the team needs to ensure that numerical features like ''monthly_bill'' and ''data_usage'' are scaled appropriately to prevent features with larger numerical ranges from dominating the model. They also need to handle missing values in some columns by imputing them with the mean. The team prefers a managed service that offers a visual interface for these common data preparation tasks. Which AWS service should they use for these transformations?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Feature Store to store pre-computed features.","AWS Glue DataBrew to visually clean, normalize, and transform the dataset.","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth to label the churn status for customers.","Amazon SageMaker Clarify to detect bias in the processed dataset."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct answer. AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that allows users to clean, normalize, and transform data without writing code. It provides built-in transformations for scaling numerical features (e.g., normalization, standardization) and handling missing values (e.g., imputation), making it ideal for the described scenario. Option A, SageMaker Feature Store, is for storing and serving features, not for the initial transformation. Option C, SageMaker Ground Truth, is for data labeling. Option D, SageMaker Clarify, is for bias detection and explainability, not for general data transformation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_38',
  'A manufacturing company collects sensor data from machinery at a high frequency. This data is used to predict equipment failures. The raw sensor readings often contain noise and outliers, and some sensors occasionally report missing values. Before feeding this data into a machine learning model, the data science team needs to apply transformations such as smoothing, outlier detection, and imputation of missing values. They require a scalable and managed service that can process these transformations on large datasets without requiring extensive infrastructure management. Which AWS service is best suited for these data cleaning and preprocessing tasks?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for labeling anomalous sensor readings.","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store for storing cleaned and transformed features.","AWS Glue for performing ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) operations on the data.","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams for real-time ingestion of raw sensor data."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the correct choice. AWS Glue is a fully managed ETL service that is ideal for large-scale data cleaning, transformation, and preprocessing tasks. It supports various data sources and targets, and its Spark-based engine can efficiently handle operations like smoothing, outlier detection, and imputation of missing values on large datasets, all without managing servers. Option A, SageMaker Ground Truth, is for data labeling. Option B, SageMaker Feature Store, is for storing and serving features, not for the initial cleaning and transformation. Option D, Kinesis Data Streams, is for data ingestion, not for complex transformations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_39',
  'A data science team is preparing a dataset for a machine learning model that predicts housing prices. The dataset includes features like ''square_footage'', ''number_of_bedrooms'', and ''neighborhood''. The ''neighborhood'' feature is a categorical variable with many unique values. To use this feature in a numerical model, it needs to be encoded. The team wants to apply one-hot encoding to this feature and ensure that the encoding scheme is consistently applied across both training and future inference data. Which AWS service provides a managed way to perform this encoding and store the resulting features for consistent retrieval?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Glue DataBrew for visual data preparation and export to S3.","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store for storing and serving consistently transformed features.","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for labeling the ''neighborhood'' categories.","Amazon Athena for querying the raw data in Amazon S3."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker Feature Store is designed to store, share, and reuse machine learning features, ensuring consistency between training and inference. While the encoding itself might be performed by SageMaker Data Wrangler or SageMaker Processing, the Feature Store is the mechanism to store and serve these consistently transformed features, directly addressing anti-pattern [ap-1.2-002] by preventing training-serving skew. Option A, AWS Glue DataBrew, can perform the encoding but doesn''t inherently manage the consistent serving of these features for inference. Option C, SageMaker Ground Truth, is for data labeling. Option D, Amazon Athena, is a query service, not a feature engineering or storage solution for consistent feature serving.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_40',
  'A data science team is working on a sentiment analysis model for customer feedback. The raw text data contains various inconsistencies, such as inconsistent capitalization, special characters, and HTML tags, which need to be cleaned before feature extraction. Additionally, they want to apply tokenization and remove stop words. The team requires a scalable and managed service that can perform these text preprocessing steps efficiently on a large corpus of documents. Which AWS service is best suited for these data cleaning and text transformation tasks with minimal operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Develop and run custom Python scripts on an Amazon EC2 instance.","Use AWS Glue DataBrew to visually prepare and clean the text data.","Deploy a custom AWS Lambda function to process each document individually.","Manually clean the text data using a local Python environment."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct answer. AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that allows users to clean, normalize, and transform data, including text data, without writing extensive code. It offers various built-in transformations suitable for text preprocessing (e.g., removing special characters, lowercasing, tokenization, stop word removal), making it ideal for this scenario with minimal operational overhead. Option A requires managing EC2 instances, increasing operational overhead. Option C, while serverless, might be less efficient for large batch processing of a corpus compared to DataBrew''s capabilities and could involve more custom code. Option D is not scalable for large datasets and is highly manual.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_41',
  'A company is developing a machine learning model to predict customer behavior based on their website interactions. The dataset includes a ''session_duration'' feature, which is heavily skewed. To improve model performance, the data scientists want to apply a log transformation to this feature. They also need to normalize other numerical features like ''page_views'' using a Min-Max scaler. It is crucial that these transformations are applied consistently to both the training data and any new inference data to avoid discrepancies. Which AWS service should the team use to define and apply these transformations and ensure consistency across the ML lifecycle?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Glue DataBrew to visually apply transformations and save the processed data to S3.","Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to define transformation flows and export them to SageMaker Pipelines for consistent execution.","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store to store the raw features and apply transformations at inference time.","Amazon SageMaker Processing to run a one-off Spark job for transformation."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler allows users to define complex data transformation flows, including log transformations and normalization. It can then integrate these flows with Amazon SageMaker Pipelines, ensuring that the exact same transformations are applied consistently during both training and inference. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.2-002] by preventing training-serving skew. Option A, AWS Glue DataBrew, can apply transformations but doesn''t inherently provide the same level of integration for consistent feature serving as SageMaker Data Wrangler with Pipelines. Option C, SageMaker Feature Store, is for storing and serving features, but Data Wrangler is used to define the transformations that populate it. Applying transformations only at inference time without a consistent training-time definition can lead to skew. Option D, SageMaker Processing, can run transformations but doesn''t inherently manage the consistency across the entire ML lifecycle without additional orchestration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_42',
  'A data science team is preparing a dataset for a machine learning model. The dataset contains numerical features that vary widely in scale, such as ''age'' (0-100) and ''income'' (thousands to millions). To ensure that no single feature dominates the model training due to its magnitude, the team needs to apply a normalization technique, like Min-Max scaling, to these features. They also want to ensure that the scaling parameters (min and max values) are derived only from the training data and then applied to both the training and test sets. Which AWS service or approach should the team use to perform this normalization while avoiding data leakage?',
  'mcq',
  '["Apply Min-Max scaling to the entire dataset before splitting it into training and test sets.","Fit the Min-Max scaler on the training data, then transform both the training and test sets using the fitted scaler.","Apply Z-score standardization to the entire dataset, as it''s less sensitive to outliers.","Use Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to perform normalization on the combined dataset."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct approach. To avoid data leakage, scalers (like Min-Max or StandardScaler) must be fitted *only* on the training data. The parameters learned from the training data are then used to transform both the training and test sets. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.2-001], which warns against fitting scalers on the full dataset (including test data) as it causes data leakage. Option A represents this anti-pattern. Option C, while a valid scaling technique, still needs to follow the train-test split rule to avoid leakage. Option D, using SageMaker Data Wrangler, is a tool for transformation, but the *method* of application (fitting only on training data) is the critical aspect for avoiding leakage, which Data Wrangler can facilitate if configured correctly.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_43',
  'A data science team is developing a machine learning model to predict customer churn. The dataset contains a ''customer_satisfaction_score'' feature, which has many missing values. Instead of removing these records, which would lead to data loss, the team decides to impute the missing values using the median of the existing scores. They also need to normalize the ''monthly_bill'' feature using Z-score standardization. The team requires a managed service that can perform these data cleaning and feature engineering tasks efficiently on a large dataset. Which AWS service is best suited for these data transformation tasks?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for labeling the dataset.","AWS Glue DataBrew to visually prepare and transform the data.","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store for storing raw features.","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams for real-time data ingestion."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct answer. AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that provides a wide range of built-in transformations, including handling missing data through imputation (e.g., median imputation) and feature scaling (e.g., Z-score standardization). It allows users to visually apply these transformations on large datasets with minimal operational overhead, making it ideal for the described scenario. Option A, SageMaker Ground Truth, is for data labeling. Option C, SageMaker Feature Store, is for storing and serving features, not for the initial transformation. Option D, Kinesis Data Streams, is for data ingestion, not for complex data transformations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_44',
  'A data science team is developing a model to predict customer churn. The dataset contains a ''customer_lifetime_value'' feature that is highly skewed. To improve the model''s performance, they decide to apply a log transformation to this feature. Additionally, they need to ensure that the imputation of missing values for ''age'' (using the mean) and the log transformation for ''customer_lifetime_value'' are applied identically during both model training and subsequent real-time inference. Which AWS service should the team use to define these transformations and ensure their consistent application across the ML lifecycle?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Glue DataBrew to perform transformations and store the output in S3.","Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to build a transformation flow and export it to SageMaker Pipelines.","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth to label the ''churn'' column for training.","Amazon SageMaker Processing to run a one-time batch transformation job."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler allows users to define complex data transformation flows, including imputation and log transformations. It can then export these flows as SageMaker Pipelines, ensuring that the exact same transformations are applied consistently during both training and inference. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.2-002] by preventing training-serving skew. Option A, AWS Glue DataBrew, can apply transformations but doesn''t inherently provide the same level of integration for consistent feature serving as SageMaker Data Wrangler with Pipelines. Option C, SageMaker Ground Truth, is for data labeling. Option D, SageMaker Processing, can run transformations but doesn''t inherently manage the consistency across the entire ML lifecycle without additional orchestration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

