INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_41',
  'A data science team is training a large deep learning model for natural language processing. The training process is computationally intensive and expected to run for several days on powerful GPU instances. The team is highly cost-conscious and needs to minimize the training expenses without significantly impacting the overall project timeline due to potential interruptions. They are aware that EC2 Spot Instances offer substantial cost savings but are concerned about job interruptions. Which Amazon SageMaker feature should the team leverage to achieve cost optimization for these long-running training jobs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize On-Demand instances with a lower-cost GPU family to ensure uninterrupted training.","Configure Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training with checkpointing enabled.","Implement distributed training across multiple On-Demand instances to reduce overall training time.","Manually stop and restart training jobs on Spot Instances to avoid long-running charges."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training allows users to train models using EC2 Spot Instances, which can reduce training costs by up to 90% compared to On-Demand instances. By enabling checkpointing, the training job can save its progress periodically. If a Spot Instance is interrupted, SageMaker can resume the training from the last saved checkpoint on a new Spot Instance, minimizing lost work and fulfilling the requirement for cost optimization without significant impact on the project timeline. This directly addresses the anti-pattern [ap-2.2-002] of not using Spot Instances for long-running training jobs.
Option A is incorrect. While using a lower-cost GPU family might save some money, On-Demand instances do not provide the significant cost savings of Spot Instances. Ensuring uninterrupted training at a higher cost contradicts the primary goal of cost optimization.
Option C is incorrect. Distributed training can reduce overall training time, but if it uses On-Demand instances, it will not achieve the same level of cost savings as Spot Instances. The primary constraint here is cost optimization.
Option D is incorrect. Manually stopping and restarting training jobs is operationally intensive, error-prone, and does not provide the automated resume capabilities of SageMaker Managed Spot Training with checkpointing, leading to potential data loss and delays.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_42',
  'A machine learning engineer has developed a new model architecture for a recommendation system and is preparing it for training on Amazon SageMaker. The model''s performance is highly sensitive to the choice of hyperparameters, such as learning rate, batch size, and the number of hidden layers. The engineer needs to systematically explore a range of hyperparameter values to find the optimal combination that maximizes the model''s accuracy, without manually running hundreds of individual training jobs. Which Amazon SageMaker feature should the engineer use to efficiently achieve this goal?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually run multiple SageMaker Training Jobs, each with a different set of hyperparameters, and compare results.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) with a defined search strategy and objective metric.","Implement SageMaker Debugger to identify the best hyperparameters during a single training run.","Deploy the model with default hyperparameters and adjust them manually after observing production performance."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) automates the process of finding the best hyperparameters for a model. It allows engineers to define a range of hyperparameter values, an objective metric (e.g., accuracy), and a search strategy (e.g., Bayesian optimization). SageMaker HPO then intelligently runs multiple training jobs, evaluating different hyperparameter combinations to converge on the optimal set, significantly reducing manual effort and improving model performance.
Option A is incorrect. Manually running multiple training jobs is inefficient, time-consuming, and does not leverage the intelligent search strategies provided by HPO, making it a suboptimal approach.
Option C is incorrect. Amazon SageMaker Debugger is used for analyzing training job performance and detecting issues like vanishing gradients or overfitting, not for hyperparameter optimization.
Option D is incorrect. Deploying with default hyperparameters and adjusting them in production is a reactive and risky approach. It''s crucial to optimize hyperparameters during development to ensure optimal performance before deployment. Additionally, the anti-pattern [ap-2.2-001] warns against running HPO with too wide a search space and few trials, implying that HPO is the correct tool, but needs to be used effectively.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_43',
  'A data science team is training a very large deep learning model on a massive dataset for a computer vision task. The model architecture is complex, and the dataset is too large to fit into the memory of a single GPU instance. To accelerate the training process and handle the scale of the data, the team needs to distribute the workload across multiple GPU instances. They want to ensure that each instance processes a portion of the data independently and efficiently, while gradients are aggregated to update the model. Which distributed training strategy is most appropriate for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Model parallelism, where different layers of the model are placed on different instances.","Data parallelism, where the dataset is split across multiple instances, and each trains a full copy of the model.","Pipeline parallelism, where data flows sequentially through a pipeline of instances.","Horizontal scaling of the inference endpoint to handle more requests."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Data parallelism is the most common and appropriate distributed training strategy when dealing with large datasets that cannot fit into a single GPU''s memory or when aiming to accelerate training. In data parallelism, the training data is split across multiple instances, and each instance trains a full copy of the model on its subset of data. Gradients are then aggregated (e.g., averaged) across instances to update the model weights, ensuring efficient and scalable training. Amazon SageMaker supports data parallelism for various frameworks.
Option A is incorrect. Model parallelism is used when the model itself is too large to fit into a single GPU''s memory, and different layers or parts of the model are distributed across instances. While relevant for very large models, data parallelism is typically the first choice for large datasets.
Option C is incorrect. Pipeline parallelism is a specific form of model parallelism where different stages of a model''s computation are assigned to different devices, forming a pipeline. It''s not the primary strategy for handling large datasets that exceed single-GPU memory.
Option D is incorrect. Horizontal scaling of the inference endpoint is related to deploying and serving a trained model, not to accelerating the training process itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_44',
  'A machine learning research team is conducting extensive experiments to develop a novel fraud detection model. They are constantly iterating on different algorithms, hyperparameter configurations, feature sets, and datasets. With numerous training jobs running daily, it has become challenging to keep track of which model version was trained with which parameters, what metrics it achieved, and how it compares to previous iterations. The team needs a centralized, organized way to log, track, and compare all their machine learning experiments. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch for logging training job metrics.","Amazon SageMaker Experiments for tracking and managing ML experiments.","Amazon S3 for storing model artifacts and training logs.","Amazon SageMaker Model Registry for registering model versions."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Amazon SageMaker Experiments is specifically designed to help machine learning teams organize, track, compare, and evaluate their ML experiments. It automatically captures input parameters, configurations, and results for each training run, making it easy to reproduce experiments and understand the lineage of models. This directly addresses the team''s need for a centralized way to manage their numerous iterations and comparisons.
Option A is incorrect. Amazon CloudWatch can log metrics, but it doesn''t provide the structured organization, comparison features, or experiment lineage tracking capabilities that SageMaker Experiments offers for ML workflows.
Option C is incorrect. Amazon S3 is used for storing data and model artifacts, but it does not provide the functionality for tracking and comparing experiment metadata and results in an organized manner.
Option D is incorrect. Amazon SageMaker Model Registry is used for cataloging models, managing their versions, and approving them for deployment, typically after experiments are concluded and a model is deemed ready. It''s not for tracking the iterative experimentation process itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

