INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_47',
  'A team of machine learning engineers is training a complex deep learning model using Amazon SageMaker. They are experiencing issues where the model''s training loss fluctuates erratically and sometimes diverges, suggesting potential numerical instability or issues with gradient flow (e.g., vanishing or exploding gradients). Manually inspecting logs is time-consuming and doesn''t provide real-time insights into the model''s internal state. The team requires an automated solution to detect these specific training anomalies and potentially stop the job before wasting compute resources. Which SageMaker feature should they implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon SageMaker Experiments to track the training loss and accuracy metrics.","Implement Amazon SageMaker Clarify to analyze post-training bias and feature importance.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Debugger with built-in rules to monitor tensors and detect gradient issues.","Set up Amazon CloudWatch alarms on the GPU utilization metric for the training instances."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker Debugger is specifically designed to monitor and analyze the internal state of training jobs, including tensors like gradients and weights. It offers built-in rules (e.g., for `ExplodingGradients`, `VanishingGradients`) that can automatically detect these types of anomalies and even trigger actions like stopping the training job, saving significant compute costs and engineering time. Option A, SageMaker Experiments, is for tracking and comparing experiment metadata, not for real-time debugging of internal training issues. Option B, SageMaker Clarify, is for bias detection and explainability after training. Option D, CloudWatch alarms on GPU utilization, might indicate a job is stuck but won''t diagnose the specific underlying ML problem like gradient issues.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_15_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_49',
  'A startup is building a new feature to classify customer support tickets into predefined categories (e.g., ''billing,'' ''technical support,'' ''feature request''). They have a large dataset of historical tickets with corresponding labels. The team has limited ML expertise and wants to quickly develop a high-performing text classification model with minimal custom code and operational overhead. They need a solution that is optimized for distributed training and can scale efficiently on AWS infrastructure. Which approach should the startup choose for building their model?',
  'mcq',
  '["Develop a custom text classification model using a TensorFlow script and deploy it on Amazon EC2 instances.","Utilize the Amazon SageMaker BlazingText built-in algorithm for text classification.","Implement a scikit-learn based text classifier and run it on a single Amazon EC2 instance.","Build a complex custom neural network from scratch using PyTorch and manage its infrastructure manually."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker BlazingText is a built-in algorithm optimized for text classification and word embeddings, offering high performance and scalability with minimal operational overhead. It''s designed to run efficiently on SageMaker''s managed infrastructure. Option A and D, developing custom models with TensorFlow or PyTorch on EC2, would require significant custom development, infrastructure management, and optimization for distributed training, which goes against the ''minimal custom code and operational overhead'' constraint and aligns with anti-pattern [ap-2.1-001]. Option C, using scikit-learn on a single EC2 instance, might not scale efficiently for a ''large dataset'' and would still require more operational management than a SageMaker built-in algorithm.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_50',
  'A real estate company wants to predict house prices based on various features like square footage, number of bedrooms, and location. They have a large dataset of historical sales data with corresponding prices. The company needs a highly accurate model that can handle both numerical and categorical features, and they want to leverage a managed service to minimize the effort required for infrastructure setup and scaling. The goal is to achieve the best predictive performance for a regression task with tabular data. Which Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithm is best suited for this problem?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker k-means for clustering similar properties together.","Amazon SageMaker XGBoost for high-performance regression on tabular data.","Amazon SageMaker DeepAR for forecasting future house price trends over time.","Amazon SageMaker Image Classification for analyzing property images to predict prices."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker XGBoost (eXtreme Gradient Boosting) is a highly efficient and popular algorithm for tabular data, excelling in both classification and regression tasks. It is known for its high predictive performance and ability to handle various feature types effectively, making it ideal for predicting house prices. Option A, SageMaker k-means, is for clustering, not regression. Option C, SageMaker DeepAR, is for time-series forecasting, which is not the primary goal here. Option D, SageMaker Image Classification, is for image analysis, which is irrelevant for predicting prices from tabular features. Using XGBoost as a built-in algorithm also aligns with avoiding anti-pattern [ap-2.1-001] by leveraging optimized SageMaker solutions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_51',
  'A marketing department wants to segment its customer base to tailor promotional campaigns. They have a large dataset of customer demographics, purchase history, and website activity, but no predefined labels for customer segments. The goal is to discover natural groupings within the customer data to identify distinct customer profiles. The team has limited experience with complex model development and prefers a managed solution that can scale. Which Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithm should they choose for this unsupervised learning task?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner for predicting customer lifetime value.","Amazon SageMaker XGBoost for classifying customer churn probability.","Amazon SageMaker k-means for clustering customers into distinct segments.","Amazon SageMaker Object Detection for identifying objects in customer profile pictures."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker k-means is an unsupervised learning algorithm specifically designed for clustering data points into distinct groups based on their features. This directly addresses the requirement to discover natural groupings within customer data without predefined labels. Option A, SageMaker Linear Learner, is for supervised regression or classification. Option B, SageMaker XGBoost, is for supervised classification or regression. Option D, SageMaker Object Detection, is for computer vision tasks. Using SageMaker k-means as a built-in algorithm also aligns with avoiding anti-pattern [ap-2.1-001] by leveraging optimized SageMaker solutions for common ML problems.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_52',
  'A bioinformatics research team is working with a high-dimensional dataset containing thousands of genetic markers for various diseases. They need to reduce the number of features to simplify the model, speed up training, and potentially improve model interpretability, without losing significant information. The team wants to use a well-established dimensionality reduction technique that can be easily integrated into their existing Amazon SageMaker workflow. They prefer a managed solution to avoid implementing complex algorithms from scratch. Which Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithm is most suitable for this task?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker PCA (Principal Component Analysis) to reduce feature dimensionality.","Amazon SageMaker k-means to cluster genetic markers into groups.","Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner to predict disease susceptibility.","Amazon SageMaker DeepAR to forecast disease prevalence over time."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker PCA (Principal Component Analysis) is a built-in algorithm specifically designed for dimensionality reduction. It transforms data into a new set of orthogonal components, capturing the most variance and effectively reducing the number of features while retaining important information. This directly addresses the team''s need to simplify models and speed up training for high-dimensional genetic data. Option B, SageMaker k-means, is for clustering. Option C, SageMaker Linear Learner, is for supervised learning (regression/classification). Option D, SageMaker DeepAR, is for time-series forecasting. Choosing a built-in algorithm like PCA avoids the need to build custom algorithms, aligning with best practices and anti-pattern [ap-2.1-001].',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_53',
  'An e-commerce company wants to forecast the sales of thousands of individual products on a daily basis for the next three months. They have extensive historical sales data for each product, exhibiting complex seasonal patterns and interdependencies. The team needs a scalable solution that can handle a large number of related time series and produce accurate probabilistic forecasts. They want to minimize the development effort and leverage a managed service. Which Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithm is best suited for this specific forecasting problem?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker XGBoost for general regression on sales data.","Amazon SageMaker DeepAR for probabilistic forecasting of multiple time series.","Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner for simple linear regression of sales trends.","Amazon SageMaker BlazingText for analyzing product review sentiment to predict sales."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker DeepAR is a built-in algorithm specifically designed for forecasting time series data, especially when dealing with multiple related time series (like thousands of products) and complex seasonal patterns. It uses recurrent neural networks to produce probabilistic forecasts, which is ideal for understanding uncertainty in sales predictions. Option A, SageMaker XGBoost, and Option C, SageMaker Linear Learner, are general-purpose regression algorithms that can be used for forecasting but are not specialized for complex, multiple time-series probabilistic forecasting like DeepAR. Option D, SageMaker BlazingText, is for text processing. Using DeepAR as a built-in algorithm aligns with avoiding anti-pattern [ap-2.1-001] by leveraging optimized SageMaker solutions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_54',
  'A media company wants to analyze a massive collection of news articles to identify key topics and perform sentiment analysis. They need to generate high-quality word embeddings for downstream natural language processing tasks, such as text classification and information retrieval. The dataset is extremely large, requiring an efficient and scalable solution for training. The team wants to use a managed service that provides optimized algorithms for text processing. Which Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithm is the most appropriate choice for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner for simple text classification based on bag-of-words features.","Amazon SageMaker BlazingText for fast and scalable word embeddings and text classification.","Amazon SageMaker k-means for clustering articles into topic groups without generating embeddings.","Amazon SageMaker DeepAR for time-series analysis of article publication frequency."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker BlazingText is a built-in algorithm that provides highly optimized implementations for word embeddings (like Word2Vec and FastText) and text classification. It is designed for speed and scalability, making it ideal for processing massive text datasets and generating high-quality embeddings for downstream NLP tasks. Option A, SageMaker Linear Learner, is a general-purpose linear model, less specialized for advanced text processing. Option C, SageMaker k-means, is for clustering and does not generate word embeddings. Option D, SageMaker DeepAR, is for time-series forecasting. Choosing BlazingText as a built-in algorithm aligns with avoiding anti-pattern [ap-2.1-001] by leveraging optimized SageMaker solutions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_55',
  'A small startup needs to quickly deploy an image classification model to categorize product images uploaded by users. They have a limited budget for data labeling and model training, and they need to get a functional model into production as fast as possible. The team has identified that a pre-trained model on a large public dataset would be a good starting point, which they can then fine-tune with their specific product images. Which Amazon SageMaker feature provides pre-built solutions and models that can be rapidly deployed or fine-tuned?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Experiments for tracking custom training runs and model metrics.","Amazon SageMaker JumpStart for pre-built solutions, models, and algorithms.","Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning for optimizing hyperparameters of custom models.","Amazon SageMaker Debugger for monitoring the internal state of training jobs."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker JumpStart provides a broad range of pre-built solutions, models, and algorithms, including many pre-trained models for common tasks like image classification. This allows users to quickly deploy a functional model or fine-tune it with their specific data, significantly reducing development time and labeling costs, which perfectly matches the startup''s requirements. Option A, SageMaker Experiments, is for tracking custom training runs. Option C, SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning, is for hyperparameter optimization of models. Option D, SageMaker Debugger, is for monitoring training job health. None of these offer the ''pre-built solution'' aspect that JumpStart does.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_15_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_45',
  'A research institution is conducting extensive machine learning experiments, involving numerous training jobs that can run for several hours or even days. These jobs are fault-tolerant, meaning they can be interrupted and resumed from a checkpoint without significant loss of progress. The institution operates under a tight budget and needs to minimize compute costs for these long-running training tasks. Which Amazon SageMaker feature should the institution leverage to achieve the MOST cost-effective training while maintaining fault tolerance?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances for all SageMaker Training Jobs to ensure uninterrupted execution.","Configure Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training with checkpointing enabled for cost savings.","Manually provision and manage a cluster of Amazon EC2 Spot Instances and install ML frameworks.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning to reduce the total training time for each job."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training allows users to leverage Amazon EC2 Spot Instances for training jobs, which can significantly reduce costs (up to 90%) compared to On-Demand instances. By enabling checkpointing, the training job can save its progress periodically and resume from the last checkpoint if an instance is interrupted, ensuring fault tolerance and minimizing lost work, directly addressing anti-pattern [ap-2.2-002]. Option A, using On-Demand instances, would be significantly more expensive. Option C, manually managing Spot Instances, adds significant operational overhead that SageMaker Managed Spot Training abstracts away. Option D, Automatic Model Tuning, optimizes hyperparameters but doesn''t directly reduce the cost of the underlying compute instances in the same way Spot Instances do.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_46',
  'A data science team is developing a new fraud detection model and needs to find the optimal combination of hyperparameters (e.g., learning rate, batch size, number of layers) to achieve the best model performance. Manually experimenting with different hyperparameter values is time-consuming and inefficient. The team wants to systematically explore the hyperparameter space and converge on the best model configuration with minimal computational resources and time. Which Amazon SageMaker feature should they use to efficiently optimize their model''s hyperparameters?',
  'mcq',
  '["Perform a manual grid search by running individual SageMaker Training Jobs for each hyperparameter combination.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) with a Bayesian optimization search strategy.","Implement a simple random search for hyperparameters by manually adjusting values between training runs.","Increase the number of training epochs significantly to allow the model to find optimal parameters on its own."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) is designed to efficiently find the best hyperparameters for a model. It supports various search strategies, including Bayesian optimization, which intelligently explores the hyperparameter space and converges on optimal values faster and with fewer trials than random or grid search. This directly addresses the need for systematic and efficient optimization. Option A and C, manual grid or random search, are less efficient and more time-consuming, especially for a large hyperparameter space. Option D, increasing training epochs, is a training parameter, not a hyperparameter tuning strategy, and won''t help find the optimal combination of other hyperparameters. This choice also directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-2.2-001] by advocating for an efficient HPO strategy over inefficient manual or wide-search approaches.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_47',
  'A machine learning research team is conducting a series of experiments to compare different model architectures, feature engineering techniques, and hyperparameter settings for a new computer vision task. They need a centralized system to track all aspects of their experiments, including input datasets, code versions, hyperparameter values, evaluation metrics, and resulting model artifacts. This system should enable easy comparison and reproducibility of results across multiple team members. Which Amazon SageMaker feature is specifically designed to meet these experiment tracking requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Experiments to organize, track, and compare ML experiments.","Amazon SageMaker Debugger to monitor the internal state of training jobs for anomalies.","Amazon SageMaker Clarify to analyze model bias and explain predictions.","Amazon CloudWatch Logs to store raw logs from training jobs for manual review."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the correct choice. Amazon SageMaker Experiments is a purpose-built service for tracking, organizing, and comparing machine learning experiments. It allows data scientists to automatically capture and store all relevant metadata, including hyperparameters, metrics, and artifacts, making it easy to reproduce results and collaborate across teams. Option B, SageMaker Debugger, is for monitoring training job health. Option C, SageMaker Clarify, is for bias detection and explainability. Option D, CloudWatch Logs, stores raw logs but does not provide the structured tracking, comparison, and reproducibility features of SageMaker Experiments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_48',
  'A company is training a large deep learning model on a massive dataset that exceeds the memory capacity of a single GPU. The model itself is also too large to fit into the memory of a single GPU. The team needs to distribute the training process across multiple GPU instances to handle both the large dataset and the large model efficiently. They want to ensure that the model parameters are split across different GPUs. Which distributed training strategy is most appropriate for this specific scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement data parallelism, where each GPU processes a different batch of data.","Utilize model parallelism, where different parts of the model are placed on different GPUs.","Train the model on a single, larger GPU instance with more memory.","Reduce the size of the dataset to fit within a single GPU''s memory."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct choice. Model parallelism is the appropriate strategy when the model itself is too large to fit into the memory of a single GPU. In this approach, different layers or parts of the model are distributed across multiple GPUs, allowing the training of extremely large models. Option A, data parallelism, is used when the dataset is too large, but the model fits on a single GPU; each GPU gets a copy of the model and processes a different batch of data. Option C, training on a single larger GPU, is not feasible if the model is too large for *any* single GPU. Option D, reducing the dataset size, is not a solution for a model that is too large for a single GPU.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_49',
  'A company is training a large language model on Amazon SageMaker using Managed Spot Training to reduce costs. These training jobs are long-running and can be interrupted if Spot Instances are reclaimed. To ensure that training progress is not lost and jobs can resume seamlessly, the team needs a mechanism to regularly save the model''s state and data. Which configuration should they implement to enable fault tolerance and minimize data loss during potential interruptions?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable checkpointing in the SageMaker Training Job configuration to periodically save model artifacts.","Switch to using Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances to guarantee uninterrupted training.","Manually restart the training job from the beginning each time an interruption occurs.","Increase the instance count for the training job to reduce the probability of all instances being interrupted simultaneously."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the correct choice. Enabling checkpointing in the SageMaker Training Job configuration is the standard and most effective way to ensure fault tolerance with Managed Spot Training. Checkpointing allows the model''s state and artifacts to be saved periodically to Amazon S3. If a Spot Instance is interrupted, the training job can then resume from the last saved checkpoint, minimizing lost work and leveraging the cost savings of Spot Instances. Option B, switching to On-Demand instances, defeats the purpose of using Spot Training for cost reduction, directly aligning with anti-pattern [ap-2.2-002]. Option C, manually restarting from the beginning, would lead to significant loss of progress and wasted compute. Option D, increasing instance count, might reduce the *chance* of total interruption but doesn''t guarantee progress preservation if interruptions do occur.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

