INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_47',
  'A data science team is developing multiple machine learning models for various business units. Each model needs to be versioned, approved by a governance committee, and tracked through its lifecycle before deployment to production. The team wants to ensure that only approved model versions are deployed and that there is a clear audit trail for all model changes. They are looking for a solution that minimizes manual intervention and provides a centralized repository for model artifacts and metadata, adhering to a strict governance policy. Which AWS service should the team use to meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually store model artifacts in Amazon S3 buckets, maintaining versioning through object versioning and tracking metadata in a spreadsheet.","Utilize AWS CodeCommit to store model artifacts and use Git tags for versioning, with manual review processes for approvals.","Implement Amazon SageMaker Model Registry to manage model versions, track lineage, and integrate with approval workflows.","Develop a custom application on AWS Lambda and Amazon DynamoDB to store model metadata and manage approval states."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because Amazon SageMaker Model Registry is specifically designed to manage model versions, track model lineage, and facilitate model governance, including approval workflows. It provides a centralized repository for model artifacts and metadata, automating much of the tracking and approval process, thereby minimizing operational overhead for model governance. Option A is incorrect because manually storing models in Amazon S3 with spreadsheet tracking lacks automation, auditability, and a structured approval process, representing anti-pattern [ap-3.2-001] by not using a model registry or approval workflow. Option B is incorrect because while AWS CodeCommit can version code, it is not designed for model artifact management, lineage tracking, or integrated approval workflows for models, requiring significant custom development. Option D is incorrect because developing a custom application for model registry and governance introduces significant development and maintenance overhead, which goes against the requirement for the least operational overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_48',
  'A machine learning engineering team is responsible for deploying and managing several Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoints. Each endpoint requires specific instance types, auto-scaling configurations, and network settings. The team needs to ensure that the infrastructure for these endpoints is consistently provisioned across different environments (development, staging, production) and that changes can be tracked and rolled back easily. They want to automate the provisioning process to reduce manual errors and improve repeatability. Which approach should the team adopt to manage their SageMaker endpoint infrastructure MOST effectively?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually create and update SageMaker endpoints using the AWS Management Console for each environment.","Write custom Python scripts using the AWS SDK for Python (Boto3) to provision and update SageMaker endpoints.","Define the SageMaker endpoint infrastructure using AWS CloudFormation templates to automate provisioning.","Store configuration details in a version-controlled document and manually apply changes via the AWS CLI."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because AWS CloudFormation allows defining infrastructure as code, including Amazon SageMaker endpoints, instance types, and auto-scaling configurations. This ensures consistent, repeatable provisioning across environments, enables version control of infrastructure definitions, and facilitates easy tracking and rollback of changes, which is highly effective for managing infrastructure. Option A is incorrect as manual provisioning through the AWS Management Console is prone to human error, inconsistent, and does not support versioning or easy rollback, leading to significant operational overhead. Option B is incorrect because while Boto3 scripts can automate provisioning, they require more custom code development and maintenance compared to declarative CloudFormation templates, which are purpose-built for infrastructure as code. Option D is incorrect because manually applying changes via the AWS CLI, even with version-controlled documentation, still introduces manual steps, potential for error, and lacks the declarative, state-managed benefits of CloudFormation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_49',
  'A data science team has developed a custom machine learning model using a specialized deep learning framework not natively supported by Amazon SageMaker''s built-in algorithms. To deploy this model for real-time inference, they need to package the model and its dependencies into a Docker container. The team requires a robust, scalable, and secure way to store and manage these container images, ensuring they are readily available for SageMaker to pull and deploy. What is the MOST appropriate AWS service for managing these custom Docker images?',
  'mcq',
  '["Store the Docker image files directly in an Amazon S3 bucket and provide the S3 URL to SageMaker.","Use Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) to store and manage the Docker container images.","Upload the Docker images to a self-managed Docker Hub repository and configure SageMaker to pull from it.","Package the Docker image within the SageMaker model artifact and upload it to SageMaker Model Registry."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) is a fully managed Docker container registry that makes it easy to store, manage, share, and deploy your Docker container images. It integrates seamlessly with Amazon SageMaker, allowing SageMaker to pull images directly for training and inference, providing a secure and scalable solution. Option A is incorrect because Amazon S3 is an object storage service and is not designed to function as a Docker image registry; SageMaker cannot directly deploy a Docker image from an S3 URL. Option C is incorrect because using a self-managed Docker Hub repository introduces external dependencies, potential security concerns, and additional operational overhead compared to a fully managed AWS service like ECR. Option D is incorrect because SageMaker Model Registry is for managing model artifacts and metadata, not for storing Docker container images themselves; the model artifact points to the container image in ECR.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_50',
  'A large e-commerce company is developing a fraud detection model that needs to be continuously updated and deployed. The data science team frequently experiments with new algorithms and features, resulting in many model versions. They need a mechanism to keep track of each model version, its associated training job, evaluation metrics, and ensure that only validated models are promoted to production. The primary goal is to maintain a clear audit trail and enable quick rollbacks to previous stable versions. Which AWS service should they use to achieve this MOST efficiently?',
  'mcq',
  '["Maintain a custom database to store model metadata and manually link it to model artifacts in Amazon S3.","Use Amazon SageMaker Model Registry to catalog model versions, store metrics, and manage approval status.","Store all model artifacts and their versions in AWS CodeCommit, relying on Git history for tracking.","Implement a system using Amazon DynamoDB to log model deployments and their corresponding S3 paths."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because Amazon SageMaker Model Registry provides a centralized repository to catalog model versions, store their associated metadata (like training job details, evaluation metrics), and manage their approval status. This service is purpose-built for model governance, versioning, and enabling quick rollbacks, directly addressing the company''s needs efficiently. Option A is incorrect because maintaining a custom database and manually linking artifacts is a high-overhead, error-prone approach that lacks the integrated features of SageMaker Model Registry, representing anti-pattern [ap-3.2-001]. Option C is incorrect because AWS CodeCommit is a version control service for code, not specifically for managing compiled model artifacts, their metrics, or approval workflows, making it inefficient for model governance. Option D is incorrect because while Amazon DynamoDB can store logs, it would require significant custom development to replicate the comprehensive features of SageMaker Model Registry for model versioning, lineage, and approval workflows.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_51',
  'A financial institution is building a credit scoring model using Amazon SageMaker. They require a robust and automated way to provision the entire SageMaker environment, including notebooks, training jobs, and real-time inference endpoints, across multiple AWS accounts and regions. The solution must ensure consistency, enable version control of the infrastructure, and allow for easy replication and updates. The team prefers to define their infrastructure using a high-level programming language. Which AWS service or tool would be the MOST suitable choice for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually configure SageMaker resources using the AWS Management Console and document the steps.","Develop custom shell scripts to call the AWS CLI commands for SageMaker resource creation.","Utilize AWS Cloud Development Kit (AWS CDK) to define and provision SageMaker resources programmatically.","Store SageMaker resource configurations in a Git repository and deploy them using AWS CodeDeploy."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because AWS Cloud Development Kit (AWS CDK) allows developers to define cloud infrastructure using familiar programming languages (like Python, TypeScript, Java). It synthesizes into AWS CloudFormation templates, providing the benefits of IaC (consistency, version control, repeatability) while offering a higher-level abstraction and programmatic control, which aligns with the team''s preference for a programming language. Option A is incorrect as manual configuration is error-prone, inconsistent, and does not support version control or easy replication across accounts/regions. Option B is incorrect because while shell scripts can automate CLI commands, they are imperative, harder to maintain, and lack the declarative state management and higher-level abstractions offered by CDK or CloudFormation. Option D is incorrect because AWS CodeDeploy is primarily for deploying application code to compute services like EC2, Lambda, or ECS, not for provisioning or managing SageMaker infrastructure itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_52',
  'A startup is developing a novel recommendation engine using a proprietary machine learning algorithm implemented in a custom Python environment with unique library dependencies. They plan to deploy this model on Amazon SageMaker for real-time inference. To ensure that the exact environment used for training is replicated during inference, including all custom libraries and configurations, the team needs to package their code and dependencies. What is the MOST effective way to achieve this for deployment on SageMaker?',
  'mcq',
  '["Upload all custom libraries and model code directly to an Amazon S3 bucket and reference them in the SageMaker model definition.","Use a SageMaker built-in algorithm container and try to install custom dependencies during endpoint startup.","Create a custom Docker image containing the model code and all dependencies, then push it to Amazon ECR for SageMaker deployment.","Bundle the custom libraries and model code into a Lambda layer and attach it to a SageMaker Serverless Inference endpoint."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because creating a custom Docker image allows the team to precisely control the entire execution environment, including the specific Python version, all custom libraries, and their exact versions. Pushing this image to Amazon ECR makes it readily available for Amazon SageMaker to pull and deploy for inference, ensuring consistency between training and inference environments. Option A is incorrect because simply uploading libraries to Amazon S3 does not define the execution environment or manage dependencies effectively; SageMaker needs a container to run the model. Option B is incorrect because relying on a built-in algorithm container and attempting to install custom dependencies at runtime is often complex, prone to errors, and may not support all custom requirements, making it less effective and reliable. Option D is incorrect because Lambda layers are for AWS Lambda functions, not directly for SageMaker inference endpoints, and SageMaker Serverless Inference still requires a container image from ECR.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_53',
  'A healthcare company is developing a diagnostic AI model. Due to regulatory compliance and patient safety, every model deployed to production must undergo a rigorous review and approval process by a compliance officer before it can serve predictions. The company needs a solution that provides a structured workflow for model validation, versioning, and approval, ensuring that no unapproved model can be accidentally deployed. This process should also maintain an immutable record of approvals. Which AWS service is BEST suited to enforce this governance requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use AWS CodePipeline to automate deployment, with a manual approval step for the model artifact.","Implement Amazon SageMaker Model Registry to manage model versions and integrate with approval workflows.","Store model artifacts in Amazon S3 with object locking and rely on S3 versioning for audit trails.","Create a custom workflow using AWS Step Functions to orchestrate manual approvals and deployment."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because Amazon SageMaker Model Registry is specifically designed to manage model versions, track their lineage, and integrate with approval workflows. It allows models to be registered, evaluated, and then explicitly approved or rejected, providing the necessary governance and audit trail for regulated industries. This directly addresses the anti-pattern [ap-3.2-001] by providing a structured approval workflow. Option A is incorrect because while AWS CodePipeline can include manual approval steps, it''s a generic CI/CD service and doesn''t offer the specialized model versioning, lineage tracking, or rich metadata capabilities inherent in SageMaker Model Registry for model governance. Option C is incorrect because Amazon S3 with object locking and versioning can store artifacts immutably, but it does not provide the workflow, metadata management, or approval mechanisms required for model governance. Option D is incorrect because while AWS Step Functions can orchestrate custom workflows, building a comprehensive model registry and approval system from scratch introduces significant development and maintenance overhead compared to using the purpose-built SageMaker Model Registry.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_54',
  'A data science team is experimenting with various machine learning models and needs to set up reproducible training environments. They frequently modify training scripts, experiment with different hyperparameters, and use specific EC2 instance types for their Amazon SageMaker training jobs. To ensure that the entire training infrastructure, including data sources, compute resources, and output locations, can be consistently provisioned and torn down for each experiment, they want to define this setup as code. Which AWS service is the MOST appropriate for defining and managing this reproducible training infrastructure?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually configure SageMaker training jobs and associated resources through the AWS CLI.","Use AWS CloudFormation to define the SageMaker training job, input data configurations, and output paths.","Store training script configurations in a Git repository and execute them via a local machine.","Create an Amazon S3 bucket to store all training scripts and manually launch jobs from the SageMaker console."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because AWS CloudFormation allows you to define your entire AWS infrastructure, including Amazon SageMaker training jobs, input data S3 buckets, output S3 locations, and IAM roles, as code in a declarative template. This enables reproducible environments, version control of infrastructure, and consistent provisioning and teardown, which is crucial for experimentation and reducing manual errors. Option A is incorrect because manual configuration via the AWS CLI, while automatable, is imperative and less robust for managing the entire infrastructure stack compared to CloudFormation''s declarative approach. Option C is incorrect because storing scripts in Git doesn''t provision the underlying AWS infrastructure needed for training, and executing locally doesn''t leverage SageMaker''s managed training capabilities. Option D is incorrect because storing scripts in S3 and manually launching jobs lacks the infrastructure-as-code benefits of CloudFormation for defining the entire training environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_55',
  'A data science team is adopting Infrastructure as Code (IaC) principles for their machine learning workflows on AWS. They want to define their entire SageMaker infrastructure, including training jobs, processing jobs, and inference endpoints, using a high-level, object-oriented programming language. This approach should allow them to use familiar programming constructs, leverage existing software development practices, and easily share and reuse infrastructure components across projects. Which AWS service or tool is BEST suited for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation, using YAML or JSON templates to define SageMaker resources.","AWS Cloud Development Kit (AWS CDK), writing code in Python or TypeScript to define SageMaker constructs.","AWS Systems Manager Automation documents to orchestrate SageMaker resource creation.","Custom Python scripts using the AWS SDK (Boto3) to interact with SageMaker APIs."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because AWS Cloud Development Kit (AWS CDK) allows developers to define cloud infrastructure using familiar programming languages like Python, TypeScript, Java, and C#. It provides high-level constructs for AWS services, including Amazon SageMaker, enabling object-oriented programming practices, reusability, and integration with existing software development workflows, which aligns perfectly with the team''s requirements. Option A is incorrect because while AWS CloudFormation is a powerful IaC tool, its YAML/JSON templates are declarative and not object-oriented programming languages, which doesn''t meet the team''s preference for high-level programming constructs. Option C is incorrect because AWS Systems Manager Automation documents are primarily for operational tasks and runbooks, not for defining and provisioning entire infrastructure stacks as code. Option D is incorrect because custom Boto3 scripts, while programmatic, are imperative and require more low-level API calls, lacking the higher-level abstractions and declarative nature of CDK for defining infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_16_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_46',
  'A retail company processes millions of customer transaction records daily to generate personalized product recommendations. This process runs once every 24 hours, taking several hours to complete, and involves scoring a large dataset with a pre-trained machine learning model. The company needs a highly cost-effective solution for this inference workload, where compute resources are only active during the actual processing time and automatically shut down afterward. Which Amazon SageMaker inference option is the MOST suitable for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference, configured with auto-scaling to handle the daily load.","Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform, processing the entire dataset in a single job.","Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference, invoked repeatedly for each transaction record.","Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference, submitting all records to a queue for processing."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform is designed for processing large datasets asynchronously. It provisions compute resources, processes the entire dataset, and then terminates the resources, making it highly cost-effective for non-interactive, large-scale batch scoring jobs that run periodically. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-3.1-001] by choosing Batch Transform over real-time inference for large batch scoring. Option A is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference endpoints are designed for interactive, low-latency predictions and maintain persistent instances, making them significantly more expensive and inefficient for large, non-interactive batch workloads. Option C is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference is suitable for infrequent, bursty real-time requests, but not optimized for processing a single, very large batch of data efficiently in a single job. Option D is incorrect because while Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference can handle large payloads and long processing times, Batch Transform is specifically optimized for the ''process a large dataset once'' use case, offering a simpler and often more cost-effective solution for this specific scenario.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_47',
  'A media streaming service uses an Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint to power its content recommendation engine. The traffic to this endpoint is highly variable, with significant spikes during peak viewing hours and very low usage overnight. The company wants to ensure high availability and responsiveness during peak times while minimizing costs during off-peak hours. They need a solution that automatically adjusts the endpoint''s capacity based on demand. Which SageMaker feature should be implemented to meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually scale the SageMaker endpoint instances up and down based on observed traffic patterns.","Configure Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference with Application Auto Scaling based on target utilization metrics.","Provision a fixed number of large SageMaker instances to handle the maximum expected peak traffic.","Use Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference, as it automatically scales without manual configuration."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because configuring Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference with Application Auto Scaling allows the endpoint to automatically adjust the number of instances based on demand, using metrics like CPU utilization or invocation count. This ensures high availability during peak traffic and reduces costs during low traffic periods by scaling down, directly addressing anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002] by configuring endpoint auto-scaling for variable inference traffic. Option A is incorrect because manual scaling is operationally intensive, prone to human error, and cannot react quickly enough to sudden traffic changes, leading to either over-provisioning or under-provisioning. Option C is incorrect because provisioning a fixed number of instances for peak traffic leads to significant over-provisioning and wasted costs during off-peak hours, representing anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002]. Option D is incorrect because while SageMaker Serverless Inference scales automatically, it is optimized for infrequent, bursty workloads with cold starts, and for consistently high or variable traffic patterns, a provisioned endpoint with auto-scaling often provides better latency and cost predictability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_48',
  'A small startup has developed a specialized image classification model that is invoked infrequently, perhaps a few times an hour, but sometimes experiences short bursts of requests. The predictions must be returned in real-time. The company is highly cost-sensitive and wants to avoid paying for idle compute resources. They need an inference solution that automatically scales to zero when not in use and scales up quickly when requests arrive, with minimal operational overhead. Which Amazon SageMaker inference option is BEST suited for this use case?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference with a single small instance and no auto-scaling.","Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform, triggered by an Amazon EventBridge schedule.","Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference, which scales automatically and charges only for inference time.","Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference, to handle the bursty nature of requests."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference is designed for workloads with infrequent or intermittent traffic patterns, where costs are optimized by scaling compute resources to zero when not in use and only charging for the duration of inference requests. It automatically scales capacity based on traffic, eliminating the need for instance management and minimizing operational overhead. Option A is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference with a single instance still incurs costs for the idle instance, which goes against the requirement to avoid paying for idle compute. Option B is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform is for processing large datasets offline, not for real-time, interactive predictions. Option D is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference is for large payloads and long processing times where immediate real-time responses are not critical, which doesn''t fit the ''real-time'' requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_49',
  'A company is deploying a complex natural language processing (NLP) model that requires significant pre-processing of input text and post-processing of model outputs. The inference requests often involve large text documents, and the processing time for each request can be several seconds. While real-time responses are desired, the application can tolerate a few seconds of latency. The company wants to ensure that these large, potentially long-running requests are handled reliably without timing out or impacting the performance of other, smaller requests. Which Amazon SageMaker inference option is MOST appropriate for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference with a large instance type to handle the load.","Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference, due to its automatic scaling capabilities.","Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference, designed for large payloads and long processing times.","Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform, triggered hourly to process accumulated requests."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference is specifically designed for scenarios involving large payloads, long processing times, and requests that can tolerate a few seconds of latency. It queues incoming requests, processes them asynchronously, and stores the results in Amazon S3, providing a robust solution that prevents timeouts and ensures reliable processing without impacting other requests. Option A is incorrect because while a large instance type might handle the load, real-time endpoints have request size and timeout limits that might be exceeded by ''large text documents'' and ''several seconds'' of processing, and it would be more costly for long-running inferences. Option B is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference has stricter payload size limits and might time out for long-running inferences, making it less suitable for this specific requirement. Option D is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform is for offline processing of entire datasets, not for handling individual, real-time-ish requests that arrive continuously.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_50',
  'An automotive manufacturer is deploying a computer vision model for quality control on an embedded device at the edge. The model needs to run efficiently on resource-constrained hardware, and inference latency is critical. The original model was trained in a standard framework (e.g., TensorFlow) but needs to be optimized for the specific hardware architecture of the edge device to reduce its size, improve performance, and lower power consumption. Which Amazon SageMaker capability should the company leverage to achieve this model optimization?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the original TensorFlow model directly to the edge device and rely on the device''s native capabilities.","Use Amazon SageMaker Neo to compile the model for the target hardware, optimizing its performance and size.","Convert the model to a simpler format like ONNX manually and deploy it to the edge device.","Retrain the model from scratch using a lightweight framework specifically designed for edge devices."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because Amazon SageMaker Neo is a model compilation service that optimizes machine learning models for deployment on various hardware targets, including edge devices. It compiles models trained in popular frameworks (TensorFlow, PyTorch, MXNet) into an executable that runs up to 2x faster with less memory footprint, making it ideal for resource-constrained edge environments. Option A is incorrect because deploying the original model without optimization will likely result in suboptimal performance, higher latency, and increased resource consumption on an edge device. Option C is incorrect because manually converting to ONNX (or other formats) requires significant manual effort and expertise, and may not achieve the same level of hardware-specific optimization as SageMaker Neo. Option D is incorrect because retraining a model from scratch is a time-consuming and resource-intensive process, and it might not be necessary if the existing model can be optimized effectively.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_51',
  'A deep learning research team is deploying several large, complex transformer models for natural language understanding. These models require significant computational resources for inference, and the team is looking for the MOST cost-effective way to achieve high-performance inference. They have identified that their models are primarily compute-bound and can benefit from specialized hardware acceleration. Which AWS service or instance type is specifically designed to provide high-performance, cost-optimized inference for deep learning models?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 instances with standard CPU processors, scaled horizontally.","Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference endpoints using GPU-accelerated instances (e.g., ml.g4dn).","AWS Inferentia-powered Amazon EC2 instances (e.g., Inf1) or SageMaker endpoints for deep learning inference.","Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference, leveraging its automatic scaling for cost efficiency."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because AWS Inferentia is a custom-designed machine learning inference chip by AWS, specifically optimized to provide high-performance and cost-effective inference for deep learning models. Deploying models on Inferentia-powered Amazon EC2 Inf1 instances or SageMaker endpoints can significantly reduce inference costs while maintaining high throughput and low latency for deep learning workloads. Option A is incorrect because standard CPU instances are generally less efficient and more costly for high-performance deep learning inference compared to specialized accelerators. Option B is incorrect because while GPU instances provide acceleration, AWS Inferentia is often more cost-effective for inference-specific workloads. Option D is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference is optimized for infrequent, bursty workloads and might not offer the raw performance or cost efficiency for consistently high-volume, compute-intensive deep learning inference compared to Inferentia.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_52',
  'A startup is building an application that uses several small, independent machine learning models for various tasks, such as sentiment analysis, entity recognition, and language detection. Each model is relatively small and has low inference traffic individually. To reduce infrastructure costs and simplify management, the startup wants to deploy all these models on a single Amazon SageMaker endpoint, sharing the underlying compute resources. Which SageMaker feature allows them to achieve this goal MOST efficiently?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy each model to its own Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference endpoint and manage them separately.","Combine all models into a single large model artifact and deploy it to a standard SageMaker endpoint.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints to host multiple models on a single endpoint.","Use Amazon SageMaker Inference Pipelines to chain the models together for sequential execution."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints allow you to host multiple models on a single SageMaker endpoint. This significantly reduces infrastructure costs by sharing the underlying compute instances across many models and simplifies deployment and management, making it ideal for scenarios with numerous small, independent models. Option A is incorrect because deploying each small model to its own endpoint would lead to higher infrastructure costs and increased operational overhead. Option B is incorrect because combining independent models into a single artifact is often complex, reduces flexibility, and might not be feasible if models have different dependencies or update cycles. Option D is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Inference Pipelines are designed for chaining sequential steps (e.g., pre-processing, inference, post-processing) for a single request flow, not for hosting multiple independent models that are invoked separately.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_53',
  'A pharmaceutical company is developing a drug discovery model. The inference process involves a complex sequence: first, a feature engineering script processes raw molecular data, then a deep learning model makes a prediction, and finally, a post-processing script formats the output for downstream systems. All these steps must execute sequentially for each inference request. The company wants to deploy this entire workflow as a single, unified endpoint to simplify application integration and ensure consistent execution. Which Amazon SageMaker feature is BEST suited for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the deep learning model to a SageMaker Real-time Inference endpoint and run pre/post-processing on the client side.","Use Amazon SageMaker Inference Pipelines to chain the pre-processing, model, and post-processing steps into a single endpoint.","Deploy each step (pre-processing, model, post-processing) as separate SageMaker endpoints and orchestrate them with AWS Step Functions.","Combine all logic (pre-processing, model, post-processing) into a single custom Docker container and deploy it to a SageMaker endpoint."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because Amazon SageMaker Inference Pipelines allow you to chain together multiple containers (each representing a step like pre-processing, model inference, or post-processing) into a single SageMaker endpoint. This ensures that the entire sequence of operations executes consistently and efficiently for each inference request, simplifying integration for client applications. Option A is incorrect because running pre/post-processing on the client side adds complexity to the client application, increases latency, and makes it harder to manage and update the entire inference workflow. Option C is incorrect because deploying each step as a separate endpoint and orchestrating with AWS Step Functions introduces higher latency, increased operational overhead, and more complex management compared to a single Inference Pipeline endpoint. Option D is incorrect because while combining all logic into a single custom container is possible, it can lead to a monolithic container that is harder to maintain, update, and scale individual components compared to the modular approach of an Inference Pipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_54',
  'A startup is launching a new AI-powered chatbot service. The underlying natural language understanding model is large and requires a significant amount of GPU memory. The chatbot is expected to handle a moderate, but consistent, volume of requests throughout the day, with low latency being a critical factor for user experience. The team needs to select an appropriate instance type for their Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint that provides sufficient computational power and memory for the model while being cost-effective for continuous operation. Which factor is MOST important when selecting the endpoint instance type?',
  'mcq',
  '["The total number of daily inference requests.","The amount of GPU memory required by the model and the desired inference latency.","The region where the SageMaker endpoint will be deployed.","The number of data scientists on the team who will be managing the model."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because the amount of GPU memory required by the model and the desired inference latency are the most critical factors when selecting an instance type for a real-time inference endpoint, especially for large deep learning models. These directly determine which instance families (e.g., GPU-accelerated instances) and specific sizes will be capable of hosting the model and meeting performance requirements. Option A is incorrect because while the total number of daily requests influences scaling, the primary factor for instance *type* selection is the model''s resource demands, not just the volume. Option C is incorrect because the AWS region affects pricing and available services, but not the fundamental computational requirements of the model itself for instance type selection. Option D is incorrect because the team size is an operational consideration and has no bearing on the technical requirements for the inference instance type.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_55',
  'A financial services company uses an Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint for fraud detection. The inference traffic fluctuates significantly throughout the day, with predictable peaks during business hours and very low traffic overnight. The company wants to optimize costs by ensuring that they only pay for the capacity they need, while also maintaining low latency during peak periods. They previously experienced high costs due to over-provisioning during off-peak hours. Which strategy should the company implement to achieve cost efficiency and performance for their variable traffic?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually scale the endpoint instances up before peak hours and scale down after off-peak hours.","Configure the SageMaker endpoint with Application Auto Scaling based on a target metric like CPU utilization.","Deploy the model on a single, large SageMaker instance to handle all traffic without scaling.","Migrate the model to Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference to automatically handle variable traffic."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because configuring Application Auto Scaling for an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint allows the system to automatically adjust the number of instances based on demand, using metrics such as CPU utilization or invocation count. This ensures that capacity matches traffic, providing low latency during peaks and minimizing costs during low traffic periods by scaling down, directly addressing anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002] by configuring endpoint auto-scaling for variable inference traffic. Option A is incorrect because manual scaling is operationally intensive, prone to errors, and may not react quickly enough to unexpected traffic changes, leading to either over-provisioning or under-provisioning. Option C is incorrect because deploying on a single, large instance to handle peak traffic will result in significant over-provisioning and wasted costs during off-peak hours, representing anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002]. Option D is incorrect because while SageMaker Serverless Inference scales automatically, for predictable and consistently variable traffic patterns, a provisioned endpoint with auto-scaling often provides better latency and cost predictability, especially for models requiring consistent warm instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_16_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_42',
  'A machine learning team is developing a credit risk model. They have established a multi-step workflow that includes data pre-processing, model training, model evaluation, and conditional model registration based on performance metrics. The team needs to automate this entire workflow, ensuring that each step executes in the correct order, and that the process can be easily monitored and debugged. They also want to be able to pass parameters between steps and trigger the workflow programmatically. Which AWS service is BEST suited for orchestrating this end-to-end ML workflow?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Step Functions, to orchestrate individual AWS Lambda functions for each step.","Amazon SageMaker Pipelines, to define and orchestrate the ML workflow as a directed acyclic graph (DAG).","AWS CodePipeline, to automate the deployment of individual training and inference jobs.","Manually execute each step of the workflow using the AWS Management Console or AWS CLI."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because Amazon SageMaker Pipelines is a purpose-built service for building, automating, and managing end-to-end machine learning workflows. It allows defining a workflow as a directed acyclic graph (DAG) with steps for data processing, training, evaluation, and conditional registration, enabling parameter passing, monitoring, and programmatic triggering, which perfectly matches the team''s requirements. Option A is incorrect because while AWS Step Functions can orchestrate workflows, SageMaker Pipelines offers ML-specific steps and integrations that simplify building ML workflows, reducing the need for custom Lambda functions for common ML tasks. Option C is incorrect because AWS CodePipeline is a CI/CD service primarily for software release processes, not for orchestrating the iterative and conditional steps of an ML workflow itself. Option D is incorrect because manual execution is error-prone, not scalable, and lacks automation, monitoring, and reproducibility.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_43',
  'A data science team has developed a new fraud detection model. They want to implement a continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipeline that automatically retrains the model whenever new training data becomes available or when the model code is updated in their Git repository. After successful training and evaluation, the pipeline should automatically deploy the new model version to a staging environment for further testing. Which combination of AWS services should the team use to build this automated ML CI/CD pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeCommit for code storage, AWS Lambda for training, and Amazon S3 for model deployment.","AWS CodePipeline for orchestration, AWS CodeBuild for code and model building, and Amazon SageMaker Pipelines for ML workflow.","Amazon EventBridge for triggering, AWS Step Functions for workflow, and Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform for deployment.","AWS Glue for data processing, Amazon Athena for model evaluation, and Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference for deployment."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because AWS CodePipeline provides the orchestration for the entire CI/CD process, triggering on code changes from AWS CodeCommit. AWS CodeBuild can be used to build Docker images for custom models or execute scripts for data preparation and model training. Amazon SageMaker Pipelines can then orchestrate the end-to-end ML workflow, including training, evaluation, and conditional model registration, before deployment. This combination provides a robust and automated CI/CD pipeline for ML. Option A is incorrect because AWS Lambda is not suitable for long-running ML training jobs, and Amazon S3 is object storage, not a deployment service for models. Option C is incorrect because while EventBridge and Step Functions can orchestrate, SageMaker Pipelines is purpose-built for ML workflows, and Batch Transform is for offline scoring, not continuous deployment to an endpoint. Option D is incorrect because AWS Glue and Amazon Athena are data services, not core components for CI/CD or model deployment orchestration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_44',
  'A financial institution has an existing fraud detection model deployed on Amazon SageMaker. To maintain model accuracy, they need to retrain the model regularly, such as weekly, and also whenever a significant shift in transaction patterns (data drift) is detected. The team wants to automate the triggering of their SageMaker retraining pipeline based on these events, minimizing manual intervention. Which AWS service should they use to schedule and event-driven trigger their ML retraining pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually trigger the SageMaker retraining pipeline using the AWS Management Console or AWS CLI.","Use AWS Lambda functions to periodically check for data drift and trigger the pipeline.","Configure Amazon EventBridge rules to trigger the SageMaker pipeline on a schedule and in response to data drift events.","Set up an Amazon SQS queue to receive data drift notifications and process them with a custom application."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus that makes it easy to connect applications together using data from your own applications, integrated SaaS applications, and AWS services. It can be configured with rules to trigger Amazon SageMaker Pipelines on a schedule (e.g., weekly) or in response to specific events, such as data drift alerts from other monitoring services, providing a robust and automated triggering mechanism. Option A is incorrect because manual triggering is not scalable, error-prone, and does not meet the automation requirement. Option B is incorrect because while AWS Lambda can be used, EventBridge provides a more direct and managed way to schedule and react to events without writing custom code for the triggering logic itself. Option D is incorrect because an Amazon SQS queue would require a consumer application to poll the queue and then trigger the pipeline, adding unnecessary complexity compared to EventBridge''s direct integration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_45',
  'A data science team has deployed an Amazon SageMaker model for predicting customer churn. To maintain the model''s accuracy over time, they need to automatically retrain it when the underlying data distribution changes significantly (data drift) or when the model''s performance degrades below a predefined threshold. They have monitoring systems in place that can detect these conditions and emit events. Which AWS service combination should be used to trigger the SageMaker retraining pipeline in response to these detected events?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use AWS CodePipeline to monitor data drift and performance degradation and trigger retraining.","Configure Amazon EventBridge to capture monitoring events and trigger an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline.","Set up an AWS Lambda function to poll monitoring dashboards and initiate SageMaker training jobs.","Manually review monitoring alerts and then trigger the SageMaker retraining pipeline via the AWS CLI."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus that can receive events from various AWS services (including monitoring services like Amazon CloudWatch for performance degradation or custom data drift detection systems). EventBridge rules can then be configured to filter these events and trigger an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline, automating the retraining process based on detected conditions. Option A is incorrect because AWS CodePipeline is primarily for CI/CD of code and artifacts, not for event-driven orchestration of ML pipelines based on data or model performance metrics. Option C is incorrect because while an AWS Lambda function could be used, EventBridge provides a more direct, managed, and scalable way to react to events without writing custom polling logic. Option D is incorrect because manual intervention does not meet the requirement for automated retraining based on detected events.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_46',
  'A machine learning engineering team is building a CI/CD pipeline for their custom Amazon SageMaker models. These models are developed using a unique set of Python libraries and require a custom Docker image for both training and inference. The team needs to automate the process of building these Docker images, running unit tests on the code within the images, and then pushing the finalized images to a container registry. Which AWS service is BEST suited for automating the build and push process for these custom Docker images within a CI/CD pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeDeploy, to deploy the Docker images directly to SageMaker endpoints.","AWS CodeBuild, to execute Docker build commands and push images to Amazon ECR.","AWS Lambda, to run shell scripts that build and push Docker images.","Amazon EC2 instances, manually configured to build and push Docker images."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because AWS CodeBuild is a fully managed continuous integration service that compiles source code, runs tests, and produces software packages. It is ideal for building Docker images, running unit tests within the build environment, and pushing the resulting images to Amazon ECR as part of a CI/CD pipeline for SageMaker models. Option A is incorrect because AWS CodeDeploy is primarily for deploying application code to compute services, not for building Docker images. Option C is incorrect because AWS Lambda has execution time limits (15 minutes) and is not designed for long-running build processes like Docker image creation, especially for large images. Option D is incorrect because manually configuring Amazon EC2 instances for building images introduces significant operational overhead and does not integrate seamlessly into a managed CI/CD pipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_47',
  'A data science team is developing a complex machine learning workflow that involves multiple sequential and conditional steps: data ingestion, feature engineering, model training, hyperparameter tuning, model evaluation, and conditional model registration based on performance. They need to define this workflow programmatically, pass outputs from one step as inputs to the next, and include conditional logic (e.g., only register the model if its accuracy exceeds a threshold). Which AWS service provides the most comprehensive solution for orchestrating such a complex, end-to-end ML workflow?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Step Functions, orchestrating a series of AWS Lambda functions.","Amazon SageMaker Pipelines, defining the workflow as a DAG with steps, conditions, and parameters.","AWS CodePipeline, to manage the sequential execution of scripts.","Manually execute individual SageMaker jobs and pass parameters using S3 for intermediate outputs."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because Amazon SageMaker Pipelines is purpose-built for orchestrating end-to-end machine learning workflows. It allows defining a workflow as a directed acyclic graph (DAG) with various steps (processing, training, tuning, evaluation, register model), supports passing parameters and outputs between steps, and includes conditional logic for dynamic execution, making it the most comprehensive solution for complex ML workflows. Option A is incorrect because while AWS Step Functions can orchestrate general workflows, SageMaker Pipelines offers ML-specific steps and integrations that simplify building ML workflows, reducing the need for custom Lambda functions for common ML tasks. Option C is incorrect because AWS CodePipeline is a CI/CD service primarily for software release processes, not for orchestrating the iterative and conditional steps of an ML workflow itself. Option D is incorrect because manual execution and S3-based parameter passing are error-prone, lack automation, and are not scalable for complex workflows.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_48',
  'A company wants to establish a fully automated MLOps pipeline for their critical customer churn prediction model. The pipeline should automatically trigger model retraining upon new code commits to a Git repository, build and test the model, register it in a model registry, and then deploy it to a production endpoint using a safe deployment strategy like canary releases. The goal is to ensure continuous delivery of high-quality models with minimal manual intervention. Which combination of AWS services provides the MOST comprehensive solution for this end-to-end MLOps pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeCommit, AWS Lambda, and Amazon S3 for basic versioning and deployment.","AWS CodePipeline, AWS CodeBuild, Amazon SageMaker Pipelines, and Amazon SageMaker Model Registry.","Amazon EventBridge, AWS Step Functions, and Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform for orchestration.","AWS Glue, Amazon Athena, and Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference for data and model serving."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct because this combination provides a comprehensive MLOps solution: AWS CodePipeline orchestrates the entire CI/CD process, triggering on code commits from AWS CodeCommit. AWS CodeBuild handles tasks like building Docker images and running unit tests. Amazon SageMaker Pipelines orchestrates the ML workflow (training, evaluation). Amazon SageMaker Model Registry manages model versions and facilitates approval. Finally, SageMaker endpoints support safe deployment strategies like canary releases. Option A is incorrect because AWS Lambda is not suitable for complex ML training, and Amazon S3 is object storage, not an MLOps orchestration or deployment service. Option C is incorrect because while EventBridge and Step Functions can orchestrate, SageMaker Pipelines and Model Registry are purpose-built for ML workflows, and Batch Transform is for offline scoring, not continuous endpoint deployment. Option D is incorrect because AWS Glue and Amazon Athena are data services, not core components for MLOps CI/CD or model deployment orchestration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_49',
  'A data science team has developed two different versions of a recommendation model, Model A and Model B, and wants to compare their real-world performance in production. They need to deploy both models to the same Amazon SageMaker endpoint and route a small percentage of live user traffic (e.g., 10%) to Model B, while the remaining 90% goes to Model A. The team needs to monitor the performance metrics of both models simultaneously to decide which one performs better. Which Amazon SageMaker endpoint feature should they use to achieve this A/B testing scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy Model A and Model B to separate SageMaker endpoints and manually split traffic at the application layer.","Use Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints to host both models and manually switch between them.","Configure the SageMaker endpoint with multiple production variants (Model A and Model B) and assign traffic weights.","Perform an in-place update on the existing SageMaker endpoint, replacing Model A with Model B, and then monitor."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct because Amazon SageMaker endpoints support multiple production variants, allowing you to deploy different model versions or configurations behind a single endpoint. You can then assign specific traffic weights to each variant, enabling A/B testing scenarios where a percentage of live traffic is routed to a new model (Model B) while the majority goes to the existing one (Model A), and monitor their performance independently. Option A is incorrect because deploying to separate endpoints and manually splitting traffic at the application layer adds complexity and operational overhead compared to SageMaker''s built-in A/B testing capabilities. Option B is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints are for hosting many independent models on shared infrastructure, not for A/B testing different versions of the *same* model with traffic splitting. Option D is incorrect because performing an in-place update replaces the old model entirely, preventing simultaneous comparison and making it unsuitable for A/B testing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

