INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_45',
  'A financial services company has deployed a fraud detection model on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint. The model''s predictions are critical, and any degradation in its performance due to shifts in input data characteristics could lead to significant financial losses. The data science team needs a proactive mechanism to detect potential data quality issues or data drift in the inference requests as they occur, without manually inspecting data. The solution must provide automated alerts to the team so they can retrain the model promptly. Which AWS service combination should be used to achieve this goal with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to analyze data quality and bias drift, then integrate it with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to notify the team.","Set up a custom AWS Lambda function to periodically sample inference requests, analyze them for drift, and send notifications via Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS).","Regularly export inference logs from Amazon SageMaker to Amazon S3, then use Amazon Athena to query the logs for anomalies and manually trigger retraining.","Monitor Amazon CloudWatch metrics for the SageMaker endpoint, such as CPU utilization and memory usage, and set alarms for high resource consumption."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is specifically designed to detect data quality, model quality, bias drift, and feature attribution drift for SageMaker endpoints. It automates the process of collecting inference data, analyzing it against a baseline, and detecting deviations. Integrating it with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms allows for automated notifications to the team when drift is detected, providing a proactive mechanism with the least operational overhead for ML-specific monitoring.
Option B is incorrect because while a custom AWS Lambda function could be built, it would require significant development, maintenance, and operational overhead to implement the drift detection logic, data sampling, and notification mechanisms, which SageMaker Model Monitor provides out-of-the-box.
Option C is incorrect because this approach involves manual querying and inspection of logs, which is reactive and does not provide automated, proactive alerts. It also has higher operational overhead compared to an automated monitoring solution.
Option D is incorrect because monitoring infrastructure metrics like CPU utilization and memory usage (an example of anti-pattern [ap-4.1-001]) does not detect data quality issues or model drift. An endpoint can be perfectly healthy infrastructure-wise while its model''s accuracy degrades due to changes in input data distributions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_46',
  'A retail company uses an Amazon SageMaker endpoint to serve product recommendation models. Over time, the recommendations have become less relevant, indicating a potential degradation in model quality. The data science team suspects that the distribution of customer behavior data has shifted significantly since the model was last trained. They need an automated solution to continuously monitor the model''s performance and detect when its predictions start to deviate from expected behavior, triggering an alert for immediate investigation. Which approach provides the most effective and automated solution for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to track model quality metrics and configure Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to trigger notifications on performance degradation.","Periodically retrain the model on a fixed schedule, regardless of performance, to ensure it adapts to new data patterns.","Manually review model predictions and input data daily to identify any discrepancies or shifts in data distribution.","Monitor the Amazon SageMaker endpoint''s infrastructure metrics like CPU utilization and network I/O using Amazon CloudWatch."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is specifically designed to detect model quality drift and data drift by continuously analyzing the data being sent to and from a SageMaker endpoint. It can track various model quality metrics (e.g., accuracy, F1 score) and compare them against a baseline. Integrating Model Monitor with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms allows for automated alerts when performance degradation or drift is detected, providing a proactive and effective solution with minimal operational overhead.
Option B is incorrect because only retraining models on a fixed schedule without monitoring for drift triggers (an example of anti-pattern [ap-4.2-001]) can lead to models becoming stale if data distributions shift unpredictably between retraining cycles. It''s not as responsive as drift-triggered retraining.
Option C is incorrect because manually reviewing predictions and data is labor-intensive, prone to human error, and not scalable or proactive enough for continuous monitoring of a production system. This approach lacks automation and efficiency.
Option D is incorrect because monitoring infrastructure metrics like CPU utilization and network I/O (an example of anti-pattern [ap-4.1-001]) does not provide insights into model quality or data drift. An endpoint can be healthy infrastructure-wise while the model''s predictions degrade due to changes in the underlying data distributions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_47',
  'A data science team is developing a new credit risk model using Amazon SageMaker. They need to ensure that the model''s predictions are fair and unbiased across different demographic groups. After deploying the model to an Amazon SageMaker endpoint, they want to continuously monitor for any potential bias drift in the model''s behavior over time, especially as new data comes in. The solution must be automated and provide alerts if bias is detected. Which SageMaker Model Monitor capability should the team leverage to achieve this goal?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect bias drift by comparing the inference data against a baseline and setting up Amazon CloudWatch Alarms.","Use Amazon SageMaker Clarify to generate bias reports during model training and periodically retrain the model if reports indicate high bias.","Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to analyze model predictions for fairness metrics and send notifications via Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS).","Monitor the Amazon SageMaker endpoint''s network latency and throughput using Amazon CloudWatch metrics to ensure consistent performance."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor includes specific capabilities to detect bias drift. It allows you to set up a baseline for bias metrics during training and then continuously monitor the inference data against this baseline to identify shifts in bias over time. Integrating this with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms provides automated alerts when bias drift is detected, enabling the team to take corrective action proactively. This is the most direct and automated solution for continuous bias monitoring in production.
Option B is incorrect because while Amazon SageMaker Clarify is excellent for detecting and explaining bias during model development and training, it is not designed for continuous, automated monitoring of bias drift in a live production endpoint. Periodically retraining based on static reports is reactive and less efficient than continuous monitoring.
Option C is incorrect because building a custom AWS Lambda function for bias detection would involve significant development effort to implement the complex logic for fairness metrics and integrate it with the monitoring pipeline. SageMaker Model Monitor provides this functionality out-of-the-box, offering lower operational overhead.
Option D is incorrect because monitoring network latency and throughput (an example of anti-pattern [ap-4.1-001]) are infrastructure metrics that do not provide any insights into the model''s fairness or bias. An endpoint can have excellent network performance while exhibiting significant bias drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_48',
  'A manufacturing company uses Amazon SageMaker for predictive maintenance models. The data scientists often experiment with different algorithms and datasets, leading to multiple versions of models and training jobs. To meet internal auditing and compliance requirements, the company needs a way to track the entire lifecycle of their machine learning models, including the datasets used, the code versions, and the resulting model artifacts. This tracking should be automated and easily auditable. Which AWS service should the company use to achieve comprehensive lineage tracking for their ML solutions?',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking to automatically record and visualize the relationships between ML artifacts and steps.","Store all model metadata and training job details in an Amazon DynamoDB table and manually update it after each experiment.","Use AWS CloudTrail to log all API calls made to Amazon SageMaker and then manually piece together the lineage information.","Implement version control for model code in AWS CodeCommit and store model artifacts in versioned Amazon S3 buckets."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking is specifically designed to automatically track and visualize the lineage of machine learning workflows. It records the relationships between various ML artifacts, such as training jobs, datasets, models, and endpoints, providing an auditable trail of how a model was created. This directly addresses the requirement for comprehensive and automated lineage tracking.
Option B is incorrect because manually storing metadata in an Amazon DynamoDB table would be labor-intensive, error-prone, and would not automatically capture the complex relationships and dependencies that SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking provides out-of-the-box. This increases operational overhead.
Option C is incorrect because while AWS CloudTrail logs API calls, it provides raw event data that would require significant effort to parse, correlate, and reconstruct into a meaningful lineage graph. It''s an auditing tool, not a dedicated ML lineage tracking solution.
Option D is incorrect because while version control for code in AWS CodeCommit and versioned Amazon S3 buckets for artifacts are good practices, they only track individual components. They do not automatically link these components together to show the end-to-end lineage of an ML model, from data to deployment, which SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking does.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_49',
  'A research lab is deploying a new image classification model on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint. The model is critical for their ongoing experiments, and any unexpected changes in the input image data distribution could severely impact the research results. The team needs to be immediately alerted if the characteristics of the incoming inference data deviate significantly from the data the model was trained on. They require an automated system to detect these ''data quality'' issues in real-time. Which SageMaker Model Monitor capability should be configured to meet this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Set up Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect data quality drift by comparing incoming inference data with a baseline dataset, and integrate with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms.","Monitor the Amazon SageMaker endpoint''s `Invocations` and `ModelLatency` metrics in Amazon CloudWatch to identify performance bottlenecks.","Implement a scheduled AWS Lambda function to periodically sample inference requests and manually analyze them for data anomalies.","Use Amazon SageMaker Clarify to generate data quality reports during the model training phase to ensure initial data integrity."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is specifically designed to detect data quality drift on SageMaker endpoints. It allows you to capture incoming inference requests and compare their statistical properties against a baseline dataset (representing the training data distribution). When significant deviations are detected, Model Monitor can trigger Amazon CloudWatch Alarms, which can then notify the research team, providing an automated, real-time solution for data quality monitoring.
Option B is incorrect because monitoring `Invocations` and `ModelLatency` metrics (an example of anti-pattern [ap-4.1-001]) provides insights into the operational health and performance of the endpoint, but it does not detect changes in the underlying data distribution or data quality. An endpoint can be performing well infrastructure-wise even if the input data quality has degraded.
Option C is incorrect because implementing a custom AWS Lambda function for periodic sampling and manual analysis is a reactive and labor-intensive approach. It lacks the automation, comprehensive statistical analysis, and real-time alerting capabilities that SageMaker Model Monitor offers out-of-the-box.
Option D is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Clarify is used during the model development and training phase to understand data quality and bias. It is not a continuous monitoring solution for live inference data on a production endpoint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_50',
  'A data science team has deployed a new machine learning model to an Amazon SageMaker endpoint. They have observed that the model''s accuracy has started to decline over the past few weeks. They suspect that the relationship between the input features and the target variable has changed, indicating a ''concept drift''. The team needs an automated way to detect this specific type of model quality degradation and trigger an alert so they can retrain the model. Which Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor capability should be configured to proactively identify this issue?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect model quality drift by comparing the actual performance metrics against a baseline and setting up Amazon CloudWatch Alarms.","Monitor the Amazon SageMaker endpoint''s `CPUUtilization` and `MemoryUtilization` metrics using Amazon CloudWatch.","Implement a scheduled AWS Lambda function to periodically retrain the model on the latest data, irrespective of performance.","Use AWS CloudTrail to log all API calls to the SageMaker endpoint and analyze for unusual activity indicating performance issues."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is designed to detect model quality drift, which includes concept drift. By comparing the actual performance metrics (e.g., accuracy, F1 score) of the model''s predictions on live inference data against a predefined baseline, Model Monitor can identify when the model''s quality degrades. Integrating this with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms provides automated alerts, enabling the team to respond promptly to concept drift by retraining the model.
Option B is incorrect because monitoring infrastructure metrics like `CPUUtilization` and `MemoryUtilization` (an example of anti-pattern [ap-4.1-001]) only indicates the health of the underlying compute resources, not the quality or performance of the model''s predictions. An endpoint can be healthy infrastructure-wise while the model''s accuracy degrades due to concept drift.
Option C is incorrect because implementing a scheduled retraining (an example of anti-pattern [ap-4.2-001]) without monitoring for drift can lead to stale models if concept drift occurs rapidly between retraining cycles. It''s less responsive and efficient than drift-triggered retraining.
Option D is incorrect because AWS CloudTrail logs API calls for auditing purposes and does not provide real-time model quality metrics or detect concept drift. Analyzing CloudTrail logs for performance issues is not its primary function and would be highly inefficient for this use case.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_51',
  'A company is deploying a new recommendation model on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint. The model''s predictions are crucial for user experience, and any significant shift in the importance of input features could lead to irrelevant recommendations. The data science team needs to continuously monitor the model in production to detect if the contribution of features to the model''s predictions changes unexpectedly over time, indicating ''feature attribution drift''. They require an automated alert system for this specific type of drift. Which SageMaker Model Monitor capability should be configured to address this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect feature attribution drift by comparing feature importance scores against a baseline and integrate with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms.","Monitor the Amazon SageMaker endpoint''s `DataBytesProcessed` and `Latency` metrics in Amazon CloudWatch to ensure data flow and responsiveness.","Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to periodically calculate SHAP values for inference requests and compare them to a historical baseline.","Use Amazon SageMaker Clarify during the model training phase to generate feature attribution explanations for the trained model."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor has the capability to detect feature attribution drift. It allows you to capture inference requests and compare the feature importance scores (e.g., SHAP values) of the live data against a baseline. When significant changes in feature attribution are detected, Model Monitor can trigger Amazon CloudWatch Alarms, providing automated alerts to the data science team. This is the most direct and automated solution for continuous monitoring of feature attribution drift in production.
Option B is incorrect because monitoring `DataBytesProcessed` and `Latency` (an example of anti-pattern [ap-4.1-001]) are infrastructure and performance metrics. They do not provide insights into how features contribute to model predictions or detect feature attribution drift. An endpoint can be performing well infrastructure-wise while feature attributions have shifted.
Option C is incorrect because while a custom AWS Lambda function could be built to calculate SHAP values, implementing and maintaining such a solution would be complex and incur significant operational overhead. SageMaker Model Monitor provides this functionality out-of-the-box, simplifying the process.
Option D is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Clarify is used during the model development and training phase to understand feature attributions and bias. It is not a continuous monitoring solution for live inference data on a production endpoint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_52',
  'A data science team has deployed an Amazon SageMaker endpoint for a critical application. They have noticed that the model''s predictions are occasionally exhibiting unexpected behavior, even though the infrastructure metrics appear normal. They suspect that the underlying data distribution used for inference has subtly changed over time, causing the model to perform suboptimally. The team needs an automated solution to detect these subtle shifts in input data characteristics and trigger alerts for immediate investigation. Which SageMaker Model Monitor capability should be configured to proactively identify this issue?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect data drift by comparing the statistical properties of incoming inference data with a baseline and setting up Amazon CloudWatch Alarms.","Monitor the Amazon SageMaker endpoint''s `HTTP 2xx` and `HTTP 4xx` error rates using Amazon CloudWatch to identify service availability issues.","Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to periodically sample inference requests and manually analyze them for statistical anomalies.","Use Amazon SageMaker Clarify during the model training phase to ensure the initial training data distribution is stable and free of anomalies."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is specifically designed to detect data drift. It continuously analyzes the statistical properties of the incoming inference data against a baseline (typically derived from the training data). When significant deviations are detected, Model Monitor can trigger Amazon CloudWatch Alarms, providing automated alerts to the data science team. This proactive approach allows for timely intervention and retraining of the model, addressing the subtle shifts in input data characteristics.
Option B is incorrect because monitoring `HTTP 2xx` and `HTTP 4xx` error rates (an example of anti-pattern [ap-4.1-001]) provides insights into the operational health and availability of the endpoint, but it does not detect changes in the underlying data distribution or data drift. An endpoint can be fully available and returning successful responses even if the input data has drifted.
Option C is incorrect because implementing a custom AWS Lambda function for periodic sampling and manual analysis is labor-intensive, reactive, and lacks the comprehensive statistical analysis and automated alerting capabilities provided by SageMaker Model Monitor out-of-the-box.
Option D is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker Clarify is used during the model development and training phase to understand data quality and bias. It is not a continuous monitoring solution for live inference data on a production endpoint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_53',
  'A data science team is developing a new machine learning model and needs to track the entire lineage of their ML experiments. This includes recording which datasets were used for training, which code versions produced specific models, and how those models were deployed to endpoints. The goal is to ensure reproducibility, auditability, and easier debugging of their ML workflows. They need an automated solution that integrates seamlessly with Amazon SageMaker. Which AWS service should the team use to achieve comprehensive ML lineage tracking?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking to automatically capture and visualize relationships between ML artifacts and workflow steps.","AWS CloudTrail to log all API calls made to SageMaker and manually reconstruct the lineage from these logs.","Amazon DynamoDB to store metadata about each experiment, requiring manual updates after every training run.","AWS CodeCommit to version control the model training code and Amazon S3 to store different versions of model artifacts."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is correct because Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking is purpose-built to automatically capture and visualize the relationships between various components of an ML workflow, such as data sources, processing jobs, training jobs, models, and endpoints. This provides a comprehensive and auditable record of the entire ML lifecycle, directly addressing the need for reproducibility, auditability, and easier debugging with minimal operational overhead.
Option B is incorrect because while AWS CloudTrail logs API calls, it provides raw event data that would require significant effort to parse, correlate, and reconstruct into a meaningful lineage graph. It''s an auditing tool, not a dedicated ML lineage tracking solution.
Option C is incorrect because manually storing metadata in an Amazon DynamoDB table would be labor-intensive, prone to human error, and would not automatically capture the complex relationships and dependencies that SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking provides out-of-the-box.
Option D is incorrect because while version control for code in AWS CodeCommit and storing versions of artifacts in Amazon S3 are good practices, they only track individual components. They do not automatically link these components together to show the end-to-end lineage of an ML model, from data to deployment, which SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking does.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_54',
  'A financial institution has deployed a fraud detection model on an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint. Over time, the data scientists notice a degradation in the model''s prediction accuracy, even though the underlying infrastructure metrics (CPU, memory, network I/O) for the endpoint appear healthy and stable. The institution needs a proactive and automated mechanism to detect shifts in the input data distribution that could be causing this accuracy degradation, with the least operational overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Periodically download inference request logs from Amazon S3 and manually analyze them for data distribution changes.","Configure Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to analyze data quality and create Amazon CloudWatch Alarms for detected data drift.","Set up Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to monitor the SageMaker endpoint''s `CPUUtilization` and `MemoryUtilization` metrics for anomalies.","Implement AWS CloudTrail logging for all SageMaker API calls and review the logs daily for any unauthorized data access patterns."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the correct solution. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is specifically designed to detect data quality issues, model quality degradation, and data drift in production ML models. By configuring Model Monitor, the institution can automatically analyze the input data distribution of inference requests against a baseline and generate Amazon CloudWatch Alarms when significant drift is detected. This provides a proactive and automated mechanism with minimal operational overhead.
Option A is incorrect because manually downloading and analyzing logs is a reactive, labor-intensive process that does not scale and introduces significant operational overhead, failing the ''least operational overhead'' constraint.
Option C is incorrect because monitoring only infrastructure metrics like CPU and memory utilization (anti-pattern ap-4.1-001) will not detect data drift or model quality degradation. An endpoint can be perfectly healthy infrastructure-wise while the model''s performance suffers due to changes in input data.
Option D is incorrect because AWS CloudTrail monitors API calls for auditing and security purposes, not for detecting data drift or model quality issues in the inference data itself. While important for security, it does not address the core problem of model degradation due to data shifts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

